{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-a9ab211dd70872d7", "type": "tool_result", "content": "1\tDomain-Specific Evaluation of Text-to-Speech Systems: A\n2\tMulti-Metric Benchmarking Study\n3\tAli Jafar1 , Amal Sarmad1 , Shifa Yousaf1 and Maryam Bashir\n4\t\n5\tarXiv:2608.02235v1 [cs.CL] 3 Aug 2026\n6\t\n7\tFAST School of Computing, National University of Computer and Emerging Sciences (FAST-NUCES), Lahore, Punjab, Pakistan\n8\t\n9\tARTICLE INFO\n10\t\n11\tABSTRACT\n12\t\n13\tKeywords:\n14\tNeural text-to-speech\n15\tTTS evaluation\n16\tSpeaker similarity\n17\tLow-resource speech synthesis\n18\tDomain-specific evaluation\n19\t\n20\tRecent advances in neural text-to-speech (TTS) systems have substantially improved speech naturalness and intelligibility across many languages. However, comprehensive evaluation methodologies\n21\tthat jointly assess perceptual quality, speaker similarity, and acoustic fidelity across diverse speech\n22\tdomains remain limited, particularly for low-resource and underrepresented languages. This paper\n23\tpresents a reproducible, multi-metric benchmarking framework for systematic evaluation of modern\n24\tTTS systems through domain-specific analysis. The proposed framework integrates complementary\n25\tsubjective and objective evaluation protocols and is demonstrated through a comprehensive case study\n26\ton a representative low-resource language spanning four speech domains: Formal, Conversational, Literary/Storytelling, and Emotional. Four state-of-the-art TTS systems—Indic-Parler-TTS, MMS-TTS,\n27\tMicrosoft Edge TTS, and Google Gemini TTS—are evaluated using MUSHRA listening tests, ABX\n28\tdiscrimination tests, speaker similarity scoring with Resemblyzer, and acoustic analyses based on melcepstral distortion (MCD) and F0 RMSE over 960 audio pairs. Results reveal substantial variation in\n29\tTTS performance across speech domains, with emotional speech consistently presenting the greatest\n30\tsynthesis challenge (mean MCD 12.03 dB; mean F0 RMSE 889 cents), while conversational speech\n31\tachieves the highest overall acoustic fidelity. Beyond the empirical findings, this work provides a\n32\treproducible evaluation framework, publicly releasing evaluation scripts, result tables, and executable\n33\tColab notebooks to support standardized benchmarking and future research on TTS evaluation for\n34\tlow-resource languages. All evaluation scripts, result tables, and curated Colab notebooks are publicly\n35\treleased at https://github.com/Shifa402/Urdu-Synthetic-Speech-Evaluation.\n36\t\n37\t1. Introduction\n38\tText-to-speech (TTS) synthesis has undergone a dramatic transformation over the past decade, with neural endto-end architectures now producing speech that approaches\n39\tor, in some perceptual dimensions, exceeds the quality of\n40\tnatural recordings in high-resource languages (Lyth and\n41\tKing, 2024; Pratap, Tjandra, Shi, Tomasello, Babu, Kundu,\n42\tElkahky, Ni, Vyas, Fazel-Zarandi, Baevski, Adi, Zhang,\n43\tHsu, Conneau and Auli, 2023). Yet progress in how synthesis quality is measured has not kept pace with progress\n44\tin model capability. Across languages, comparative TTS\n45\tstudies still rely disproportionately on single-domain, singlemetric protocols that cannot reveal whether a system is fit for\n46\ta specific communicative purpose.\n47\tEven in English and other well-resourced settings, evaluation practice remains narrow. Most benchmark studies\n48\treport short neutral utterances rated via the Mean Opinion Score (MOS) (Kirkland, Mehta, Lameris, Henter and\n49\tSzekely, 2023; Le Maguer et al., 2024). As synthetic speech\n50\tquality improves, MOS scores saturate at the top of the\n51\trating scale, obscuring perceptual differences between highperforming systems. More fundamentally, a single-domain\n52\tevaluation cannot show whether a voice that sounds natural\n53\ton read-aloud news text will remain acceptable in conversational dialogue, storytelling, or emotionally expressive\n54\tali.jafar.fast@gmail.com (A. Jafar); amalsarmadmir@gmail.com (A.\n55\tSarmad); shifayousaf4@gmail.com (S. Yousaf); maryam.bashir@nu.edu.pk\n56\t(M. Bashir)\n57\tORCID (s):\n58\t1 These authors contributed equally to this work.\n59\t\n60\tspeech, dimensions directly relevant to real-world deployment. Recent work therefore advocates task- and situationspecific evaluation that assesses whether speech fulfils its intended communicative role rather than limiting comparison\n61\tto surface-level transmission quality (Bailly et al., 2025).\n62\tUrdu provides a timely case study for this broader\n63\tevaluation problem. Multiple publicly accessible synthesis\n64\tsystems, including MMS-TTS (Pratap et al., 2023), IndicParler-TTS (Lyth and King, 2024; Lacombe, Srivastav and\n65\tGandhi, 2024), Microsoft Edge TTS, and Google Gemini\n66\tTTS, are now available, yet no study has compared them\n67\tsystematically across communicative domains. Prior Urdu\n68\twork has almost exclusively used short neutral prompts and\n69\tMOS-style ratings (Naseem et al., 2025; Arshad et al., 2022),\n70\tleaving open the question of whether system rankings are\n71\tstable when speech style, prosody, and pragmatic context\n72\tchange.\n73\tThis paper addresses that gap by presenting a domainstratified benchmark evaluation with Urdu as the target language. Four publicly available systems, namely Indic-ParlerTTS, MMS-TTS, Microsoft Edge TTS (ur-PK-AsadNeural),\n74\tand Google Gemini TTS, are evaluated across four linguistically and pragmatically distinct speech domains: Formal,\n75\tConversational, Literary/Storytelling, and Emotional.\n76\tEach domain is sourced from a dedicated dataset and evaluated using a multi-metric pipeline comprising subjective\n77\tMUSHRA listening tests (International Telecommunication\n78\tUnion, 2015), ABX discrimination tests, speaker-identity\n79\tsimilarity scoring via Resemblyzer (Wan, Wang, Papir\n80\tand Moreno, 2018), mel-cepstral distortion (MCD), and\n81\tfundamental frequency root-mean-square error (F0 RMSE)\n82\t\n83\tJafar, Sarmad, Yousaf & Bashir: Preprint submitted to Computer Speech & Language\n84\t\n85\tPage 1 of 17\n86\t\n87\t\fDomain-Specific Evaluation of Urdu TTS Systems\n88\t\n89\tover 960 audio pairs. The combination of a domain-stratified\n90\tcorpus, multiple complementary metrics, and a fully reproducible pipeline constitutes a methodological advance over\n91\tprior single-domain, MOS-only TTS evaluations.\n92\tThe contributions of this paper are as follows:\n93\t\n94\tThe Multiple Stimuli with Hidden Reference and Anchor (MUSHRA) protocol (International Telecommunication Union, 2015) addresses several of these shortcomings.\n95\tUnlike MOS, MUSHRA presents the hidden reference, one\n96\tor more anchor conditions, and all systems under test simultaneously, enabling listeners to make direct comparisons\n97\t1. A domain-stratified benchmarking framework is introon a continuous 0–100 scale. The simultaneous presentation\n98\tduced for systematic evaluation of TTS systems across\n99\tincreases sensitivity to inter-system differences and reduces\n100\tdiverse speech styles, including Formal, Conversathe range effect that distorts absolute MOS ratings (Chitional, Literary/Storytelling, and Emotional domains.\n101\tang and Lee, 2024). MUSHRA was originally designed\n102\tThe framework is demonstrated through a comprefor intermediate-quality audio codecs; however, its relative\n103\thensive case study involving four TTS systems, three\n104\tcomparison framework is well-suited to TTS evaluation\n105\tspeech datasets, and 960 evaluated audio pairs.\n106\twhere the goal is to rank systems against a natural speech\n107\t2. A multi-dimensional evaluation pipeline is proposed\n108\treference, and it is increasingly preferred in benchmark\n109\tthat integrates perceptual, discriminative, acoustic,\n110\tstudies involving multiple systems (Le Maguer et al., 2024).\n111\tand speaker-similarity measures, combining MUSHRAObjective metrics complement subjective tests by prostyle listening tests, ABX discrimination, MCD, speaker- viding scalable, reproducible measurements that do not reembedding cosine similarity, and F0 RMSE. This proquire listener recruitment. Mel-cepstral distortion (MCD) (Kuvides a more holistic assessment of synthesis quality\n112\tbichek, 1993), computed from DTW-aligned mel-cepstral\n113\tthan any single evaluation criterion.\n114\tcoefficient sequences, quantifies spectral envelope similarity\n115\t3. A reproducible evaluation resource is publicly rebetween reference and synthetic speech. Fundamental freleased, including evaluation scripts, result tables, and\n116\tquency RMSE (F0 RMSE), expressed in cents on a logarithexecutable Colab notebooks at https://github.com/\n117\tmic scale (Kominek and Black, 2008), measures pitch accuShifa402 / Urdu-Synthetic-Speech-Evaluation. These\n118\tracy in a perceptually meaningful unit where approximately\n119\tresources provide an extensible foundation for future\n120\tfive cents approximates one just-noticeable difference in\n121\tbenchmarking of TTS systems across languages, dopitch. Speaker embedding cosine similarity, computed via\n122\tmains, and architectures.\n123\tthe GE2E-trained encoder of Resemblyzer (Wan et al.,\n124\t2018), measures whether the synthesised voice preserves\n125\tthe identity characteristics of the reference speaker and has\n126\t2. Related Work\n127\tbeen widely adopted in zero-shot TTS evaluation (Wang\n128\tThis section reviews the literature relevant to the evalet al., 2023b). Taken together, MCD and F0 RMSE capture\n129\tuation of modern text-to-speech (TTS) systems, advances\n130\tacoustic similarity at the spectral and prosodic levels, while\n131\tin speech synthesis for low-resource languages, and prior\n132\tspeaker embedding similarity measures a higher-level idenwork on Urdu and South Asian TTS. Together, these studies\n133\ttity dimension that the two signal-level metrics cannot.\n134\testablish the broader context for this work, highlighting the\n135\tA broader trend in the field is a movement away from\n136\tgrowing need for comprehensive evaluation methodologies\n137\taggregate quality metrics toward task- and situation-specific\n138\tand the limited availability of domain-specific benchmarks\n139\tevaluation (Bailly et al., 2025). MOS and MUSHRA were\n140\tfor low-resource languages.\n141\tdesigned to measure transmission quality rather than to\n142\tassess whether speech fulfils its communicative purpose.\n143\t2.1. TTS Evaluation Methodology\n144\tEvaluation should instead ask whether a voice is appropriSubjective evaluation via the Mean Opinion Score (MOS), ate for its intended use case, such as news reading, storystandardised by ITU-T P.800, has been the dominant evaluatelling, emotional narration, or conversational dialogue, and\n145\ttion protocol in speech synthesis for decades. MOS instructs\n146\twhether domain-specific prosodic and stylistic requirements\n147\tlisteners to rate each stimulus in isolation on a five-point\n148\tare met. The present work operationalises this principle by\n149\tabsolute category rating scale. While its simplicity has\n150\tstructuring the evaluation framework around four commumade it universally adopted, a growing body of evidence\n151\tnicatively distinct domains.\n152\tdocuments its limitations for evaluating modern neural TTS.\n153\tAs the quality of synthetic speech has improved, MOS scores\n154\t2.2. TTS for Low-Resource Languages\n155\texhibit a ceiling effect: high-performing systems cluster\n156\tThe challenge of building high-quality TTS for underat the top of the scale, making it statistically difficult to\n157\tresourced languages is fundamentally a data problem: exdistinguish between them or between high-quality synthetic\n158\tpressive, multi-speaker, studio-quality training corpora do\n159\tspeech and natural recordings (Le Maguer et al., 2024). Kirknot exist at scale for the majority of the world’s languages.\n160\tland et al. (2023) conducted a meta-analysis of Interspeech\n161\tTwo broad strategies have emerged to address this. The first\n162\tand SSW papers and found that researchers underreport\n163\tis transfer learning, which exploits acoustic and phonologmethodological details, vary widely in scale labels and inical overlap between high-resource parent languages and\n164\tstructions, and obtain results that are not transferable across\n165\tthe target low-resource language. Approaches that initialise\n166\tstudies-meaning MOS scores from different publications\n167\tfrom English Tacotron or FastSpeech models and fine-tune\n168\tcannot be compared without re-running all systems jointly.\n169\t\n170\tJafar, Sarmad, Yousaf & Bashir: Preprint submitted to Computer Speech & Language\n171\t\n172\tPage 2 of 17\n173\t\n174\t\fDomain-Specific Evaluation of Urdu TTS Systems\n175\t\n176\ton small Urdu or South Asian corpora have demonstrated\n177\tthat cross-lingual transfer can yield intelligible synthesis\n178\twith limited target-language data (Arshad et al., 2022).\n179\tThe second strategy is massively multilingual pre-training.\n180\tPratap et al. (2023) scaled VITS-based synthesis (Kim, Kong\n181\tand Son, 2021) to over 1,100 languages by training on\n182\taligned religious text recordings, providing a publicly available Urdu model (facebook/mms-tts-urd-script_arabic) that\n183\tserves as a strong baseline in zero-resource settings. Parallel\n184\tefforts for Indian languages have explored multi-speaker and\n185\tmultilingual VITS training across thirteen Indo-Aryan and\n186\tDravidian languages, finding that shared acoustic models\n187\timprove both objective metrics and subjective naturalness\n188\tcompared to monolingual systems, though gains are uneven\n189\tacross language families (Jain et al., 2022).\n190\tMore recently, description-conditioned language models have emerged as a paradigm shift in controllable TTS.\n191\tParler-TTS (Lyth and King, 2024; Lacombe et al., 2024)\n192\tencodes a natural-language speaker description through a\n193\tfrozen Flan-T5 encoder (Chung et al., 2022) and generates audio tokens via a cross-attention-conditioned causal\n194\tTransformer over a Descript Audio Codec with nine residual\n195\tvector quantisation codebooks. This architecture, adopted in\n196\tai4bharat/indic-parler-tts, enables fine-grained voice control without requiring speaker embeddings extracted from\n197\treference recordings-a significant advantage in low-resource\n198\tsettings where clean reference recordings for target speakers\n199\tmay be unavailable.\n200\tDespite these modelling advances, evaluation practice\n201\tfor low-resource TTS has lagged. Single-metric intelligibility checks such as word or character error rate computed\n202\tvia ASR round-trips remain common but are insufficient:\n203\tthey fail to capture naturalness, prosodic quality, or domain\n204\tsuitability, and are blind to failures in language identification\n205\tand script fidelity that matter for non-Latin-script languages\n206\tsuch as Urdu (Anonymous, 2025). The lack of standardised,\n207\tmulti-domain evaluation benchmarks for South Asian languages means that practitioners have no principled basis for\n208\tselecting the most appropriate system for a given application.\n209\t\n210\t2.3. Urdu and South Asian Speech Synthesis\n211\tUrdu presents a distinct set of challenges for TTS that\n212\tare not shared by better-resourced languages. Its Nastaliqstyle Perso-Arabic script encodes vowels as optional diacritics (i’raab), creating text normalisation and grapheme-tophoneme ambiguities that most multilingual models handle\n213\tpoorly. The language’s phonological proximity to Hindiwhile sharing largely the same spoken phoneme inventorymeans that models trained on Hindi data can transfer to Urdu\n214\tsynthesis, but the resulting systems often produce unnaturally Hindustani prosody. Additionally, Urdu-English codeswitching is prevalent in natural speech, a phenomenon that\n215\tbenchmark evaluations on monolingual read-speech corpora\n216\tcannot capture.\n217\tEarly Urdu TTS systems relied on concatenative synthesis or statistical parametric models before the shift to\n218\t\n219\tneural synthesis. Transfer learning from English Tacotron\n220\tand Transformer-TTS has demonstrated that pre-trained\n221\tacoustic models can be adapted to Urdu with limited inlanguage data (Arshad et al., 2022). Naseem et al. (2025)\n222\tcompared Tacotron-based Urdu models against MMS-TTS\n223\tusing MOS, Semantically Unstructured Sentence (SUS)\n224\ttests, and comprehension evaluations, finding that Tacotronbased models with script-specific text analysis modules\n225\toutperform MMS in intelligibility and naturalness on read\n226\tspeech, while MMS shows relative advantages on certain word-boundary consonants. Despite this, evaluation\n227\tin Naseem et al. (2025) is conducted entirely on readspeech prompts with no examination of domain-specific\n228\tperformance variation.\n229\tFor Indian languages more broadly, Jain et al. (2022)\n230\tevaluated multi-speaker VITS systems across thirteen languages, reporting MOS and objective metrics but, again,\n231\tusing a single-domain read-speech protocol. The absence\n232\tof domain-stratified evaluation corpora for South Asian languages reflects a general methodological deficit that extends\n233\tbeyond Urdu: to our knowledge, no published benchmark\n234\texists that systematically evaluates how synthesis quality\n235\tvaries across communicative styles for any South Asian\n236\tlanguage. This paper provides such a benchmark for Urdu,\n237\testablishing a reproducible evaluation framework that can be\n238\textended to related languages as dedicated datasets become\n239\tavailable.\n240\t\n241\t3. Methodology for TTS System Selection\n242\tThis section describes the selection process for the four\n243\ttext-to-speech (TTS) systems evaluated in this study. It first\n244\tpresents the inclusion criteria used to ensure fair and representative system selection, then outlines the architecture and\n245\timplementation details of each system, and finally compares\n246\ttheir key characteristics relevant to the subsequent evaluation.\n247\t\n248\t3.1. Selection Criteria\n249\tSystem selection was governed by three operationally\n250\tdefined criteria applied jointly. First, each system must provide publicly accessible, programmatic Urdu synthesis at the\n251\ttime of data collection (November–December 2024), either\n252\tthrough a released model checkpoint under an open licence\n253\tor through a documented cloud API endpoint, such that any\n254\tresearch group can reproduce inference without institutional\n255\tagreements or manual access requests. Second, each system\n256\tmust accept Urdu Perso-Arabic Unicode text as direct input\n257\twithout requiring the evaluator to perform manual transliteration or romanisation; imposing such pre-processing would\n258\tintroduce a confound external to the synthesis engine and\n259\twould not reflect realistic deployment conditions. Third,\n260\tthe selected systems must collectively span architecturally\n261\tand methodologically distinct paradigms currently represented in the TTS literature, specifically, massively multilingual end-to-end synthesis, description-conditioned audio\n262\t\n263\tJafar, Sarmad, Yousaf & Bashir: Preprint submitted to Computer Speech & Language\n264\t\n265\tPage 3 of 17\n266\t\n267\t\fDomain-Specific Evaluation of Urdu TTS Systems\n268\t\n269\tlanguage modelling, commercial neural TTS, and large audio language model generation, so that benchmark findings\n270\tgeneralise beyond any single modelling approach.\n271\tApplying these criteria yielded four systems: MMSTTS (Pratap et al., 2023), Indic-Parler-TTS (Lyth and King,\n272\t2024; Lacombe et al., 2024), Microsoft Edge TTS (urPK-AsadNeural), and Google Gemini TTS. Two candidate\n273\tsystems were considered but excluded. A publicly available\n274\tTacotron-2 fine-tune trained on the Urdu Mozilla Common\n275\tVoice corpus was withdrawn from its Hugging Face repository prior to data collection, precluding reproducible inference. A FastSpeech-2 checkpoint trained on an institutional\n276\tcorpus at the National University of Sciences and Technology (NUST) required institutional VPN access, violating\n277\tthe public accessibility criterion. No other Urdu-capable\n278\tTTS systems meeting all three criteria were identified in\n279\tthe literature or public model repositories at the time of\n280\tselection.\n281\t\n282\t3.2. Description of Evaluated TTS Models\n283\tMMS-TTS\n284\t(facebook/mms-tts-urd-script_arabic).\n285\tMeta’s Massively Multilingual Speech (MMS) project\n286\textended VITS-based end-to-end TTS (Kim et al., 2021)\n287\tto 1,107 languages by training on sentence-aligned\n288\tread-speech recordings sourced from publicly available\n289\tmultilingual corpora (Pratap et al., 2023). VITS jointly\n290\toptimises a conditional variational autoencoder over\n291\tmel-spectrogram frames, a normalising flow-based\n292\tprior, and an adversarial waveform decoder, enabling\n293\tsingle-stage, non-autoregressive synthesis (Kim et al.,\n294\t2021). Text is processed through the UROMAN universal\n295\tgrapheme-to-phoneme converter before being passed to the\n296\tacoustic model (Pratap et al., 2023). The Urdu checkpoint\n297\ttargets the Perso-Arabic script partition and, per the MMS\n298\ttraining documentation, is derived predominantly from a\n299\tsingle-speaker read-speech source. Inference was performed\n300\tlocally via the Hugging Face transformers library (v4.40.0)\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "3483c3dd-35ce-4c18-9530-2fe55290a039", "uuid": "2d1ccaed-bc2e-4f4e-9b82-8c688fc76eee", "timestamp": "2026-08-04T11:46:07.879Z", "tool_use_result": {"type": "text", "file": {"filePath": "/tmp/2608.02235.txt", "content": "Domain-Specific Evaluation of Text-to-Speech Systems: A\nMulti-Metric Benchmarking Study\nAli Jafar1 , Amal Sarmad1 , Shifa Yousaf1 and Maryam Bashir\n\narXiv:2608.02235v1 [cs.CL] 3 Aug 2026\n\nFAST School of Computing, National University of Computer and Emerging Sciences (FAST-NUCES), Lahore, Punjab, Pakistan\n\nARTICLE INFO\n\nABSTRACT\n\nKeywords:\nNeural text-to-speech\nTTS evaluation\nSpeaker similarity\nLow-resource speech synthesis\nDomain-specific evaluation\n\nRecent advances in neural text-to-speech (TTS) systems have substantially improved speech naturalness and intelligibility across many languages. However, comprehensive evaluation methodologies\nthat jointly assess perceptual quality, speaker similarity, and acoustic fidelity across diverse speech\ndomains remain limited, particularly for low-resource and underrepresented languages. This paper\npresents a reproducible, multi-metric benchmarking framework for systematic evaluation of modern\nTTS systems through domain-specific analysis. The proposed framework integrates complementary\nsubjective and objective evaluation protocols and is demonstrated through a comprehensive case study\non a representative low-resource language spanning four speech domains: Formal, Conversational, Literary/Storytelling, and Emotional. Four state-of-the-art TTS systems—Indic-Parler-TTS, MMS-TTS,\nMicrosoft Edge TTS, and Google Gemini TTS—are evaluated using MUSHRA listening tests, ABX\ndiscrimination tests, speaker similarity scoring with Resemblyzer, and acoustic analyses based on melcepstral distortion (MCD) and F0 RMSE over 960 audio pairs. Results reveal substantial variation in\nTTS performance across speech domains, with emotional speech consistently presenting the greatest\nsynthesis challenge (mean MCD 12.03 dB; mean F0 RMSE 889 cents), while conversational speech\nachieves the highest overall acoustic fidelity. Beyond the empirical findings, this work provides a\nreproducible evaluation framework, publicly releasing evaluation scripts, result tables, and executable\nColab notebooks to support standardized benchmarking and future research on TTS evaluation for\nlow-resource languages. All evaluation scripts, result tables, and curated Colab notebooks are publicly\nreleased at https://github.com/Shifa402/Urdu-Synthetic-Speech-Evaluation.\n\n1. Introduction\nText-to-speech (TTS) synthesis has undergone a dramatic transformation over the past decade, with neural endto-end architectures now producing speech that approaches\nor, in some perceptual dimensions, exceeds the quality of\nnatural recordings in high-resource languages (Lyth and\nKing, 2024; Pratap, Tjandra, Shi, Tomasello, Babu, Kundu,\nElkahky, Ni, Vyas, Fazel-Zarandi, Baevski, Adi, Zhang,\nHsu, Conneau and Auli, 2023). Yet progress in how synthesis quality is measured has not kept pace with progress\nin model capability. Across languages, comparative TTS\nstudies still rely disproportionately on single-domain, singlemetric protocols that cannot reveal whether a system is fit for\na specific communicative purpose.\nEven in English and other well-resourced settings, evaluation practice remains narrow. Most benchmark studies\nreport short neutral utterances rated via the Mean Opinion Score (MOS) (Kirkland, Mehta, Lameris, Henter and\nSzekely, 2023; Le Maguer et al., 2024). As synthetic speech\nquality improves, MOS scores saturate at the top of the\nrating scale, obscuring perceptual differences between highperforming systems. More fundamentally, a single-domain\nevaluation cannot show whether a voice that sounds natural\non read-aloud news text will remain acceptable in conversational dialogue, storytelling, or emotionally expressive\nali.jafar.fast@gmail.com (A. Jafar); amalsarmadmir@gmail.com (A.\nSarmad); shifayousaf4@gmail.com (S. Yousaf); maryam.bashir@nu.edu.pk\n(M. Bashir)\nORCID (s):\n1 These authors contributed equally to this work.\n\nspeech, dimensions directly relevant to real-world deployment. Recent work therefore advocates task- and situationspecific evaluation that assesses whether speech fulfils its intended communicative role rather than limiting comparison\nto surface-level transmission quality (Bailly et al., 2025).\nUrdu provides a timely case study for this broader\nevaluation problem. Multiple publicly accessible synthesis\nsystems, including MMS-TTS (Pratap et al., 2023), IndicParler-TTS (Lyth and King, 2024; Lacombe, Srivastav and\nGandhi, 2024), Microsoft Edge TTS, and Google Gemini\nTTS, are now available, yet no study has compared them\nsystematically across communicative domains. Prior Urdu\nwork has almost exclusively used short neutral prompts and\nMOS-style ratings (Naseem et al., 2025; Arshad et al., 2022),\nleaving open the question of whether system rankings are\nstable when speech style, prosody, and pragmatic context\nchange.\nThis paper addresses that gap by presenting a domainstratified benchmark evaluation with Urdu as the target language. Four publicly available systems, namely Indic-ParlerTTS, MMS-TTS, Microsoft Edge TTS (ur-PK-AsadNeural),\nand Google Gemini TTS, are evaluated across four linguistically and pragmatically distinct speech domains: Formal,\nConversational, Literary/Storytelling, and Emotional.\nEach domain is sourced from a dedicated dataset and evaluated using a multi-metric pipeline comprising subjective\nMUSHRA listening tests (International Telecommunication\nUnion, 2015), ABX discrimination tests, speaker-identity\nsimilarity scoring via Resemblyzer (Wan, Wang, Papir\nand Moreno, 2018), mel-cepstral distortion (MCD), and\nfundamental frequency root-mean-square error (F0 RMSE)\n\nJafar, Sarmad, Yousaf & Bashir: Preprint submitted to Computer Speech & Language\n\nPage 1 of 17\n\n\fDomain-Specific Evaluation of Urdu TTS Systems\n\nover 960 audio pairs. The combination of a domain-stratified\ncorpus, multiple complementary metrics, and a fully reproducible pipeline constitutes a methodological advance over\nprior single-domain, MOS-only TTS evaluations.\nThe contributions of this paper are as follows:\n\nThe Multiple Stimuli with Hidden Reference and Anchor (MUSHRA) protocol (International Telecommunication Union, 2015) addresses several of these shortcomings.\nUnlike MOS, MUSHRA presents the hidden reference, one\nor more anchor conditions, and all systems under test simultaneously, enabling listeners to make direct comparisons\n1. A domain-stratified benchmarking framework is introon a continuous 0–100 scale. The simultaneous presentation\nduced for systematic evaluation of TTS systems across\nincreases sensitivity to inter-system differences and reduces\ndiverse speech styles, including Formal, Conversathe range effect that distorts absolute MOS ratings (Chitional, Literary/Storytelling, and Emotional domains.\nang and Lee, 2024). MUSHRA was originally designed\nThe framework is demonstrated through a comprefor intermediate-quality audio codecs; however, its relative\nhensive case study involving four TTS systems, three\ncomparison framework is well-suited to TTS evaluation\nspeech datasets, and 960 evaluated audio pairs.\nwhere the goal is to rank systems against a natural speech\n2. A multi-dimensional evaluation pipeline is proposed\nreference, and it is increasingly preferred in benchmark\nthat integrates perceptual, discriminative, acoustic,\nstudies involving multiple systems (Le Maguer et al., 2024).\nand speaker-similarity measures, combining MUSHRAObjective metrics complement subjective tests by prostyle listening tests, ABX discrimination, MCD, speaker- viding scalable, reproducible measurements that do not reembedding cosine similarity, and F0 RMSE. This proquire listener recruitment. Mel-cepstral distortion (MCD) (Kuvides a more holistic assessment of synthesis quality\nbichek, 1993), computed from DTW-aligned mel-cepstral\nthan any single evaluation criterion.\ncoefficient sequences, quantifies spectral envelope similarity\n3. A reproducible evaluation resource is publicly rebetween reference and synthetic speech. Fundamental freleased, including evaluation scripts, result tables, and\nquency RMSE (F0 RMSE), expressed in cents on a logarithexecutable Colab notebooks at https://github.com/\nmic scale (Kominek and Black, 2008), measures pitch accuShifa402 / Urdu-Synthetic-Speech-Evaluation. These\nracy in a perceptually meaningful unit where approximately\nresources provide an extensible foundation for future\nfive cents approximates one just-noticeable difference in\nbenchmarking of TTS systems across languages, dopitch. Speaker embedding cosine similarity, computed via\nmains, and architectures.\nthe GE2E-trained encoder of Resemblyzer (Wan et al.,\n2018), measures whether the synthesised voice preserves\nthe identity characteristics of the reference speaker and has\n2. Related Work\nbeen widely adopted in zero-shot TTS evaluation (Wang\nThis section reviews the literature relevant to the evalet al., 2023b). Taken together, MCD and F0 RMSE capture\nuation of modern text-to-speech (TTS) systems, advances\nacoustic similarity at the spectral and prosodic levels, while\nin speech synthesis for low-resource languages, and prior\nspeaker embedding similarity measures a higher-level idenwork on Urdu and South Asian TTS. Together, these studies\ntity dimension that the two signal-level metrics cannot.\nestablish the broader context for this work, highlighting the\nA broader trend in the field is a movement away from\ngrowing need for comprehensive evaluation methodologies\naggregate quality metrics toward task- and situation-specific\nand the limited availability of domain-specific benchmarks\nevaluation (Bailly et al., 2025). MOS and MUSHRA were\nfor low-resource languages.\ndesigned to measure transmission quality rather than to\nassess whether speech fulfils its communicative purpose.\n2.1. TTS Evaluation Methodology\nEvaluation should instead ask whether a voice is appropriSubjective evaluation via the Mean Opinion Score (MOS), ate for its intended use case, such as news reading, storystandardised by ITU-T P.800, has been the dominant evaluatelling, emotional narration, or conversational dialogue, and\ntion protocol in speech synthesis for decades. MOS instructs\nwhether domain-specific prosodic and stylistic requirements\nlisteners to rate each stimulus in isolation on a five-point\nare met. The present work operationalises this principle by\nabsolute category rating scale. While its simplicity has\nstructuring the evaluation framework around four commumade it universally adopted, a growing body of evidence\nnicatively distinct domains.\ndocuments its limitations for evaluating modern neural TTS.\nAs the quality of synthetic speech has improved, MOS scores\n2.2. TTS for Low-Resource Languages\nexhibit a ceiling effect: high-performing systems cluster\nThe challenge of building high-quality TTS for underat the top of the scale, making it statistically difficult to\nresourced languages is fundamentally a data problem: exdistinguish between them or between high-quality synthetic\npressive, multi-speaker, studio-quality training corpora do\nspeech and natural recordings (Le Maguer et al., 2024). Kirknot exist at scale for the majority of the world’s languages.\nland et al. (2023) conducted a meta-analysis of Interspeech\nTwo broad strategies have emerged to address this. The first\nand SSW papers and found that researchers underreport\nis transfer learning, which exploits acoustic and phonologmethodological details, vary widely in scale labels and inical overlap between high-resource parent languages and\nstructions, and obtain results that are not transferable across\nthe target low-resource language. Approaches that initialise\nstudies-meaning MOS scores from different publications\nfrom English Tacotron or FastSpeech models and fine-tune\ncannot be compared without re-running all systems jointly.\n\nJafar, Sarmad, Yousaf & Bashir: Preprint submitted to Computer Speech & Language\n\nPage 2 of 17\n\n\fDomain-Specific Evaluation of Urdu TTS Systems\n\non small Urdu or South Asian corpora have demonstrated\nthat cross-lingual transfer can yield intelligible synthesis\nwith limited target-language data (Arshad et al., 2022).\nThe second strategy is massively multilingual pre-training.\nPratap et al. (2023) scaled VITS-based synthesis (Kim, Kong\nand Son, 2021) to over 1,100 languages by training on\naligned religious text recordings, providing a publicly available Urdu model (facebook/mms-tts-urd-script_arabic) that\nserves as a strong baseline in zero-resource settings. Parallel\nefforts for Indian languages have explored multi-speaker and\nmultilingual VITS training across thirteen Indo-Aryan and\nDravidian languages, finding that shared acoustic models\nimprove both objective metrics and subjective naturalness\ncompared to monolingual systems, though gains are uneven\nacross language families (Jain et al., 2022).\nMore recently, description-conditioned language models have emerged as a paradigm shift in controllable TTS.\nParler-TTS (Lyth and King, 2024; Lacombe et al., 2024)\nencodes a natural-language speaker description through a\nfrozen Flan-T5 encoder (Chung et al., 2022) and generates audio tokens via a cross-attention-conditioned causal\nTransformer over a Descript Audio Codec with nine residual\nvector quantisation codebooks. This architecture, adopted in\nai4bharat/indic-parler-tts, enables fine-grained voice control without requiring speaker embeddings extracted from\nreference recordings-a significant advantage in low-resource\nsettings where clean reference recordings for target speakers\nmay be unavailable.\nDespite these modelling advances, evaluation practice\nfor low-resource TTS has lagged. Single-metric intelligibility checks such as word or character error rate computed\nvia ASR round-trips remain common but are insufficient:\nthey fail to capture naturalness, prosodic quality, or domain\nsuitability, and are blind to failures in language identification\nand script fidelity that matter for non-Latin-script languages\nsuch as Urdu (Anonymous, 2025). The lack of standardised,\nmulti-domain evaluation benchmarks for South Asian languages means that practitioners have no principled basis for\nselecting the most appropriate system for a given application.\n\n2.3. Urdu and South Asian Speech Synthesis\nUrdu presents a distinct set of challenges for TTS that\nare not shared by better-resourced languages. Its Nastaliqstyle Perso-Arabic script encodes vowels as optional diacritics (i’raab), creating text normalisation and grapheme-tophoneme ambiguities that most multilingual models handle\npoorly. The language’s phonological proximity to Hindiwhile sharing largely the same spoken phoneme inventorymeans that models trained on Hindi data can transfer to Urdu\nsynthesis, but the resulting systems often produce unnaturally Hindustani prosody. Additionally, Urdu-English codeswitching is prevalent in natural speech, a phenomenon that\nbenchmark evaluations on monolingual read-speech corpora\ncannot capture.\nEarly Urdu TTS systems relied on concatenative synthesis or statistical parametric models before the shift to\n\nneural synthesis. Transfer learning from English Tacotron\nand Transformer-TTS has demonstrated that pre-trained\nacoustic models can be adapted to Urdu with limited inlanguage data (Arshad et al., 2022). Naseem et al. (2025)\ncompared Tacotron-based Urdu models against MMS-TTS\nusing MOS, Semantically Unstructured Sentence (SUS)\ntests, and comprehension evaluations, finding that Tacotronbased models with script-specific text analysis modules\noutperform MMS in intelligibility and naturalness on read\nspeech, while MMS shows relative advantages on certain word-boundary consonants. Despite this, evaluation\nin Naseem et al. (2025) is conducted entirely on readspeech prompts with no examination of domain-specific\nperformance variation.\nFor Indian languages more broadly, Jain et al. (2022)\nevaluated multi-speaker VITS systems across thirteen languages, reporting MOS and objective metrics but, again,\nusing a single-domain read-speech protocol. The absence\nof domain-stratified evaluation corpora for South Asian languages reflects a general methodological deficit that extends\nbeyond Urdu: to our knowledge, no published benchmark\nexists that systematically evaluates how synthesis quality\nvaries across communicative styles for any South Asian\nlanguage. This paper provides such a benchmark for Urdu,\nestablishing a reproducible evaluation framework that can be\nextended to related languages as dedicated datasets become\navailable.\n\n3. Methodology for TTS System Selection\nThis section describes the selection process for the four\ntext-to-speech (TTS) systems evaluated in this study. It first\npresents the inclusion criteria used to ensure fair and representative system selection, then outlines the architecture and\nimplementation details of each system, and finally compares\ntheir key characteristics relevant to the subsequent evaluation.\n\n3.1. Selection Criteria\nSystem selection was governed by three operationally\ndefined criteria applied jointly. First, each system must provide publicly accessible, programmatic Urdu synthesis at the\ntime of data collection (November–December 2024), either\nthrough a released model checkpoint under an open licence\nor through a documented cloud API endpoint, such that any\nresearch group can reproduce inference without institutional\nagreements or manual access requests. Second, each system\nmust accept Urdu Perso-Arabic Unicode text as direct input\nwithout requiring the evaluator to perform manual transliteration or romanisation; imposing such pre-processing would\nintroduce a confound external to the synthesis engine and\nwould not reflect realistic deployment conditions. Third,\nthe selected systems must collectively span architecturally\nand methodologically distinct paradigms currently represented in the TTS literature, specifically, massively multilingual end-to-end synthesis, description-conditioned audio\n\nJafar, Sarmad, Yousaf & Bashir: Preprint submitted to Computer Speech & Language\n\nPage 3 of 17\n\n\fDomain-Specific Evaluation of Urdu TTS Systems\n\nlanguage modelling, commercial neural TTS, and large audio language model generation, so that benchmark findings\ngeneralise beyond any single modelling approach.\nApplying these criteria yielded four systems: MMSTTS (Pratap et al., 2023), Indic-Parler-TTS (Lyth and King,\n2024; Lacombe et al., 2024), Microsoft Edge TTS (urPK-AsadNeural), and Google Gemini TTS. Two candidate\nsystems were considered but excluded. A publicly available\nTacotron-2 fine-tune trained on the Urdu Mozilla Common\nVoice corpus was withdrawn from its Hugging Face repository prior to data collection, precluding reproducible inference. A FastSpeech-2 checkpoint trained on an institutional\ncorpus at the National University of Sciences and Technology (NUST) required institutional VPN access, violating\nthe public accessibility criterion. No other Urdu-capable\nTTS systems meeting all three criteria were identified in\nthe literature or public model repositories at the time of\nselection.\n\n3.2. Description of Evaluated TTS Models\nMMS-TTS\n(facebook/mms-tts-urd-script_arabic).\nMeta’s Massively Multilingual Speech (MMS) project\nextended VITS-based end-to-end TTS (Kim et al., 2021)\nto 1,107 languages by training on sentence-aligned\nread-speech recordings sourced from publicly available\nmultilingual corpora (Pratap et al., 2023). VITS jointly\noptimises a conditional variational autoencoder over\nmel-spectrogram frames, a normalising flow-based\nprior, and an adversarial waveform decoder, enabling\nsingle-stage, non-autoregressive synthesis (Kim et al.,\n2021). Text is processed through the UROMAN universal\ngrapheme-to-phoneme converter before being passed to the\nacoustic model (Pratap et al., 2023). The Urdu checkpoint\ntargets the Perso-Arabic script partition and, per the MMS\ntraining documentation, is derived predominantly from a\nsingle-speaker read-speech source. Inference was performed\nlocally via the Hugging Face transformers library (v4.40.0)", "numLines": 300, "startLine": 1, "totalLines": 2047}}}
