{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-9b8323ba1088b843", "type": "tool_result", "content": "300\tlocally via the Hugging Face transformers library (v4.40.0)\n301\tat a native output sample rate of 16 kHz. MMS-TTS is the\n302\tonly fully open-weight, locally executable system in this\n303\tevaluation, which has practical implications for deployment\n304\tin resource-constrained or low-connectivity environments.\n305\tIndic-Parler-TTS\n306\t(ai4bharat / indic-parler-tts).\n307\tParler-TTS (Lyth and King, 2024; Lacombe et al., 2024)\n308\tis a description-conditioned audio language model that\n309\tgenerates speech by conditioning a causal Transformer\n310\tdecoder on both the target text and a natural-language\n311\tspeaker description. The description is encoded by a\n312\tfrozen Flan-T5-Large encoder (Chung et al., 2022), and\n313\tthe resulting representations cross-attend to a GPT-2scale decoder that predicts discrete audio tokens from a\n314\tnine-codebook Descript Audio Codec (DAC) (Lacombe\n315\tet al., 2024). A corresponding DAC decoder renders\n316\twaveforms at 44.1 kHz. The ai4bharat / indic-parler-tts\n317\tcheckpoint (AI4Bharat, 2024) extends this architecture\n318\tto twenty-two Indic languages, including Urdu, through\n319\tcontinued pre-training on a multilingual read-speech and\n320\taudiobook corpus assembled by AI4Bharat. Because voice\n321\t\n322\tcharacteristics are specified through free-text descriptions\n323\trather than extracted speaker embeddings, the model does\n324\tnot require a reference recording to condition synthesis,\n325\ta property that is advantageous in low-resource settings\n326\twhere clean reference audio for target speakers may be\n327\tunavailable (Lacombe et al., 2024). A fixed description\n328\tspecifying a male speaker with a clear, moderate-paced\n329\tvoice was used uniformly across all evaluation domains to\n330\tcontrol for inter-domain description variation.\n331\tMicrosoft Edge TTS (ur-PK-AsadNeural). Microsoft\n332\tEdge TTS is a cloud-based neural TTS service that exposes\n333\tsynthesis capability through a documented WebSocket endpoint accessed via the open-source edge-tts Python library\n334\t(v6.1.9). The ur-PK-AsadNeural voice targets Pakistani\n335\tUrdu and is part of Microsoft’s Azure Cognitive Services\n336\tNeural TTS platform (Microsoft Corporation, 2023), which\n337\temploys a Transformer-based acoustic model combined\n338\twith a neural vocoder, trained on professionally recorded\n339\tstudio speech. Synthesis is produced at 24 kHz. Microsoft\n340\tdoes not publicly disclose training corpus composition,\n341\tmodel architecture specifications, or speaker recording details for production neural voices, and accordingly this system is treated as an opaque commercial baseline within the\n342\tevaluation. Its inclusion is motivated by the documented\n343\tadoption of the AsadNeural voice in Urdu-language assistive\n344\ttechnology and digital media applications, making its performance profile directly relevant to practitioners evaluating\n345\tdeployment options.\n346\tGoogle Gemini TTS. Google Gemini TTS generates\n347\tspeech natively through the Gemini 2.5 Flash model with\n348\taudio output enabled, accessed via the google-generativeai\n349\tPython SDK (v0.8.3) using the gemini-2.5-flash-preview-tts endpoint. Unlike conventional cascaded TTS pipelines\n350\tthat decompose synthesis into independent text analysis,\n351\tacoustic modelling, and vocoding stages, Gemini TTS renders speech directly from the language model’s contextual\n352\trepresentations through a dedicated speech decoder, a design that has been reported to improve prosodic coherence\n353\tover multi-sentence inputs (Google DeepMind, 2025). The\n354\tsystem is configured using the PrebuiltVoiceConfig parameter; the Charon voice preset was applied uniformly across\n355\tall evaluation domains to isolate domain-specific synthesis\n356\tvariation from speaker-selection effects. Output audio was\n357\tproduced at 24 kHz. Google does not publicly disclose the\n358\tUrdu training data volume, language-specific fine-tuning\n359\tprocedures, or internal model specifications for this system.\n360\tTo the best of our knowledge, this paper presents the first\n361\tpublished objective and subjective evaluation of Gemini\n362\tTTS on Urdu speech.\n363\t\n364\t3.3. Comparative System Properties\n365\tTable 1 summarises the architectural, training, and deployment properties of the four systems. Differences along\n366\tfour dimensions are particularly relevant to interpreting\n367\tdomain-specific results. (i) Synthesis paradigm: MMS-TTS\n368\tuses non-autoregressive flow-based generation; Indic-ParlerTTS uses autoregressive discrete token prediction; Edge TTS\n369\t\n370\tJafar, Sarmad, Yousaf & Bashir: Preprint submitted to Computer Speech & Language\n371\t\n372\tPage 4 of 17\n373\t\n374\t\fDomain-Specific Evaluation of Urdu TTS Systems\n375\tTable 1\n376\tArchitectural and deployment properties of the four evaluated Urdu TTS systems. Proprietary systems are marked with (†) to\n377\tindicate that architecture and training details are not publicly disclosed.\n378\tSystem\n379\t\n380\tParadigm\n381\t\n382\tTraining Scope\n383\t\n384\tSpeaker Control\n385\t\n386\tRate\n387\t\n388\tAccess\n389\t\n390\tMMS-TTS\n391\t\n392\tVITS: VAE + flow + GAN\n393\t(Kim et al., 2021)\n394\tAutoregressive LM + DAC\n395\t(Lacombe et al., 2024)\n396\tNeural (undisclosed) (Microsoft Corporation, 2023)\n397\tAudio LM, direct render\n398\t(Google DeepMind, 2025)\n399\t\n400\tMassively multilingual\n401\t(1,107 lang.)\n402\t22 Indic languages\n403\t\n404\tSingle speaker (M)\n405\t\n406\t16 kHz\n407\t\n408\tOpen weight\n409\t\n410\t44.1 kHz\n411\t\n412\tOpen weight\n413\t\n414\tProprietary (ur-PK)\n415\t\n416\tDescriptionconditioned\n417\tPreset (M, ur-PK)\n418\t\n419\t24 kHz\n420\t\n421\tCloud API\n422\t\n423\tProprietary multilingual\n424\t\n425\tPreset voices\n426\t\n427\t24 kHz\n428\t\n429\tCloud API\n430\t\n431\tIndic-ParlerTTS\n432\tEdge TTS†\n433\tGemini TTS†\n434\t\n435\tuses an undisclosed Transformer-based pipeline; and Gemini TTS uses direct audio rendering from a large language\n436\tmodel. (ii) Training scope: MMS-TTS and Gemini TTS are\n437\ttrained at massive multilingual scale, whereas Indic-ParlerTTS targets a defined set of twenty-two Indic languages and\n438\tEdge TTS is a dedicated Pakistani Urdu voice. (iii) Speaker\n439\tcontrol: MMS-TTS produces a fixed single-speaker output;\n440\tIndic-Parler-TTS accepts free-text description conditioning;\n441\tEdge and Gemini TTS offer discrete preset selection. (iv)\n442\tOutput sample rate: 16 kHz (MMS-TTS), 24 kHz (Edge\n443\tTTS, Gemini TTS), and 44.1 kHz (Indic-Parler-TTS); prior\n444\tto objective evaluation, audio was resampled at metricspecific rates (22.05 kHz for MCD and 16 kHz for F0 RMSE\n445\tand Resemblyzer speaker embeddings) to match each feature\n446\textractor’s requirements (see Section 5).\n447\t\n448\t4. Domain-Wise Dataset Setup\n449\tThis section describes the construction and organisation\n450\tof the evaluation corpus used in this study. It motivates the\n451\tneed for domain diversity in TTS evaluation and presents\n452\teach of the four speech domains, namely Formal, Conversational, Emotional, and Literary/Storytelling.\n453\t\n454\t4.1. Need for Domain Diversity in TTS Evaluation\n455\tThe construction of a domain-diverse dataset is an important factor in evaluating modern text-to-speech (TTS)\n456\tsystems, particularly for low-resource languages such as\n457\tUrdu. Recent research has shown that the quality of speech\n458\tsynthesis is strongly influenced not only by model architecture but also by the diversity and distribution of evaluation\n459\tdata between speaking styles and domains (Kong, Kim and\n460\tBae, 2020; Tan, Qin, Soong and Liu, 2021). Traditional evaluation datasets often focus on homogeneous read speech,\n461\twhich limits their ability to capture real-world variability\n462\tin prosody, emotion, and communicative intent. As TTS\n463\tsystems continue to evolve towards more expressive and\n464\tcontrollable generation, the need for diverse and domainaware datasets has become increasingly important for robust\n465\tbenchmarking.\n466\tIn this study, the evaluation dataset is organized into\n467\tfour distinct speech domains, namely Formal, Conversational, Literary/Storytelling, and Emotional. Each domain\n468\t\n469\trepresents a different communicative and stylistic setting,\n470\tcharacterized by unique linguistic and prosodic patterns.\n471\tThis domain selection aligns with recent advances in expressive and controllable speech synthesis, where systems\n472\tare expected to generalize across multiple speaking styles\n473\trather than in a single uniform voice setting (Wang et al.,\n474\t2017; Kim et al., 2021). The resulting dataset design enables\n475\ta more comprehensive evaluation of Urdu TTS systems\n476\tunder varied real-world conditions, supporting fine-grained\n477\tanalysis across both neutral and expressive speech scenarios.\n478\t\n479\t4.2. Domain 1: Formal Speech\n480\tThis subsection defines the Formal speech domain and\n481\tdescribes the datasets selected to represent it in the evaluation corpus. Together, these datasets capture the linguistic\n482\tand communicative characteristics of formal speech required\n483\tfor domain-specific evaluation.\n484\t\n485\t4.2.1. Domain Characteristics\n486\tThe Formal domain represents controlled and structured\n487\tspeech produced in situations where the primary objective is clarity, correctness, and linguistic precision. This\n488\tcan include read-aloud scenarios such as news broadcasting, academic narration, and scripted announcements, where\n489\tspeakers typically follow prepared text without spontaneous\n490\tdeviations. The importance of this domain lies in its role as\n491\ta baseline condition for text-to-speech evaluation, as it minimizes variability in speaking style, emotion, and discourse\n492\tstructure.\n493\tIn the context of TTS systems, formal read speech provides a stable reference point for assessing fundamental\n494\tsynthesis capabilities such as pronunciation accuracy, fluency, and prosodic consistency. Since the linguistic input in\n495\tthis domain is well-formed and predictable, it allows for a\n496\tmore controlled evaluation of system performance, making\n497\tit widely used in both benchmarking and comparative analysis of speech synthesis models.\n498\t4.2.2. Selected Dataset(s)\n499\tThis domain is constructed using publicly available multilingual speech corpora that provide high-quality, studiolike read speech recordings under controlled acoustic conditions. The Google FLEURS dataset is used as a primary\n500\t\n501\tJafar, Sarmad, Yousaf & Bashir: Preprint submitted to Computer Speech & Language\n502\t\n503\tPage 5 of 17\n504\t\n505\t\fDomain-Specific Evaluation of Urdu TTS Systems\n506\t\n507\tsource due to its well-standardized recording protocol, multilingual coverage, and consistent speech delivery across\n508\tspeakers and languages. In this study, only Urdu-language\n509\tutterances are selected to ensure linguistic relevance, while\n510\tspeaker-level filtering is applied to retain male voice recordings for maintaining uniformity across all experimental conditions.\n511\tDataset link: https : / / huggingface . co / datasets / google /\n512\tfleurs\n513\t\n514\tIn addition, the UrduSpeech dataset is incorporated as a\n515\tcomplementary source of formal read speech. This dataset\n516\tprovides high-quality Urdu speech with multiple speaking\n517\tstyles and linguistic variations. For the purpose of this work,\n518\tonly Urdu samples are retained, as the corpus also contains\n519\tclosely related Indic language data such as Kashmiri. Furthermore, style-based filtering is applied to select Wiki-style\n520\tutterances, ensuring adherence to a formal and structured\n521\treading modality. Gender-based filtering is again applied to\n522\tretain male speakers, ensuring consistency across datasets\n523\tused in the evaluation framework.\n524\tDataset link: https://huggingface.co/datasets/humairawan/\n525\tUrduSpeech\n526\t\n527\t4.3. Domain 2: Conversational Speech\n528\tThis subsection introduces the Conversational speech\n529\tdomain and the dataset selected to represent it within the\n530\tevaluation corpus.\n531\t\n532\t4.3.1. Domain Characteristics\n533\tThe conversational domain represents spontaneous and\n534\tsemi-structured dialogue-based communication that occurs\n535\tin everyday interactions. Unlike formal read speech, this\n536\tdomain is characterized by natural speaking patterns, including variations in intonation, informal phrasing, and contextual dependencies between utterances. The importance\n537\tof this domain lies in its ability to capture realistic speech\n538\tbehavior, making it essential for evaluating the robustness of\n539\ttext-to-speech systems in non-scripted and socially interactive scenarios. This domain introduces greater variability in\n540\tprosody, rhythm, and speaking style, providing a more challenging evaluation setting compared to controlled speech\n541\tconditions.\n542\t4.3.2. Selected Dataset(s)\n543\tThe data for this domain are derived from the UrduSpeech dataset publicly available on Hugging Face. Since\n544\tthe dataset contains multilingual recordings, only Urdulanguage utterances are retained for this study. A style-based\n545\tfiltering criterion is applied to isolate conversational speech\n546\tsamples and only recordings labeled as conversational are\n547\tincluded. With both male and female voice audios being\n548\tpresent, only male speaker recordings are selected.\n549\tDataset link: https://huggingface.co/datasets/humairawan/\n550\tUrduSpeech\n551\t\n552\t4.4. Domain 3: Emotional Speech\n553\tThis subsection describes the Emotional speech domain,\n554\toutlining the acoustic and affective properties that distinguish it from other domains and the dataset from which\n555\temotional utterances are drawn.\n556\t\n557\t4.4.1. Domain Characteristics\n558\tThe emotional domain represents expressive speech\n559\tcharacterized by systematic variations in prosody, pitch,\n560\tintensity, and speaking rate that correspond to different\n561\taffective states. Unlike formal or conversational speech,\n562\tthis domain explicitly encodes emotional information within\n563\tthe acoustic signal, making it significantly more variable\n564\tand challenging for text-to-speech systems. The inclusion\n565\tof emotional speech is essential for evaluating expressive\n566\tspeech synthesis capabilities, as it tests whether a system\n567\tcan generate natural and contextually appropriate emotional\n568\tvariation beyond neutral speaking styles.\n569\t4.4.2. Selected Dataset(s)\n570\tThe emotional speech data is obtained from the Urdu\n571\tSpeech Emotion Corpus (UrSEC), a comprehensive dataset\n572\tdesigned for speech emotion recognition in the Urdu language. The dataset contains 3,500 annotated speech samples\n573\tcollected from professional actors and covers seven emotional categories: Angry, Fear, Boredom, Disgust, Happy,\n574\tNeutral, and Sad. The recordings are sourced from naturally\n575\tspoken Urdu dialogues derived from drama serials and telefilms, ensuring realistic and contextually rich utterances.\n576\tDataset link: https : / / data . mendeley . com / datasets /\n577\tjcpfjnk5c2/4\n578\t\n579\tFor the purpose of this study, a balanced subset of\n580\tsamples is selected across all emotion categories to ensure\n581\tuniform representation of affective states. The Neutral category is excluded from evaluation to avoid overlap with the\n582\tFormal and Conversational Speech domain defined in this\n583\twork. Only male-speaker recordings are retained to maintain\n584\tconsistency across all experimental domains.\n585\t\n586\t4.5. Domain 4: Literary/Storytelling Speech\n587\tThis subsection describes the Literary/Storytelling speech\n588\tdomain, detailing the narrative and prosodic features that\n589\tcharacterise it and the dataset used for its construction.\n590\t\n591\t4.5.1. Domain Characteristics\n592\tThis domain represents expressive narrative speech used\n593\tin contexts such as storytelling, book reading, and dramatic narration. Unlike formal or conversational speech,\n594\tthis domain is characterized by dynamic prosodic variation,\n595\textended utterance structure, and deliberate modulation of\n596\tpitch and rhythm to convey narrative meaning and emotional\n597\tcontext. This makes it an important evaluation setting for\n598\ttext-to-speech systems aimed at expressive synthesis, as it\n599\ttests the ability of models to generate natural and engaging\n600\tnarrative speech beyond neutral or conversational styles.\n601\t\n602\tJafar, Sarmad, Yousaf & Bashir: Preprint submitted to Computer Speech & Language\n603\t\n604\tPage 6 of 17\n605\t\n606\t\fDomain-Specific Evaluation of Urdu TTS Systems\n607\t\n608\t4.5.2. Selected Dataset(s)\n609\tThe Literary/Storytelling domain is constructed using\n610\tthe diverse UrduSpeech dataset, which contains multiple\n611\tspeaking styles. From this dataset, only Urdu-language samples are selected. Additionally, style-based filtering is applied to retain BOOK-style recordings, which best represent\n612\tstorytelling and literary narration. Speaker-level filtering is\n613\tfurther applied to maintain male-speaker consistency across\n614\tall evaluation domains.\n615\tDataset link: https://huggingface.co/datasets/humairawan/\n616\tUrduSpeech\n617\t\n618\t5. Evaluation Methodology\n619\tThis section presents the evaluation framework employed in this study. To provide a comprehensive assessment\n620\tof text-to-speech (TTS) quality, the framework integrates\n621\tcomplementary subjective and objective evaluation methodologies, capturing perceptual quality, speaker similarity,\n622\tand acoustic fidelity. Together, these evaluation components\n623\tprovide a holistic basis for comparing TTS systems across\n624\tdifferent speech domains while ensuring a reproducible and\n625\tconsistent benchmarking process.\n626\t\n627\t5.1. Evaluation Framework Design\n628\tThe evaluation pipeline is organised around two complementary design principles. The first is metric complementarity: no single metric captures all perceptually relevant dimensions of synthesis quality (Kirkland et al., 2023;\n629\tLe Maguer et al., 2024), and accordingly the framework\n630\tcombines subjective listening tests with three classes of objective measurement, namely speaker identity preservation,\n631\tspectral envelope fidelity, and prosodic accuracy. The second\n632\tis domain stratification: all metrics are computed separately\n633\tfor each of the four speech domains (Formal, Conversational,\n634\tLiterary/Storytelling, and Emotional) rather than aggregated\n635\tacross domains, so that domain-specific performance variation is directly observable rather than obscured by averaging. Together, these principles operationalise the task-andsituation-specific evaluation paradigm advocated by Bailly\n636\tet al. (2025) for the specific case of Urdu TTS.\n637\tThe complete pipeline proceeds as follows. For each domain, reference recordings are paired with synthetic outputs\n638\tfrom each of the four TTS systems at the utterance level,\n639\tyielding 960 audio pairs in total (4 systems × 4 domains × 60\n640\tutterances per domain). Each pair is then passed through four\n641\tevaluation modules: a subjective MUSHRA-style listening\n642\ttest, a speaker similarity scoring procedure via Resemblyzer\n643\t(Wan et al., 2018), mel-cepstral distortion (MCD) computation (Kubichek, 1993), and fundamental frequency rootmean-square error (F0 RMSE) computation (Kominek and\n644\tBlack, 2008). An additional forced-choice ABX discrimination test is conducted as a second-stage evaluation, applied\n645\tto the two top-performing systems identified through the\n646\tMUSHRA stage, to assess perceptual indistinguishability\n647\tfrom natural speech. All evaluation scripts and result tables are publicly available at https://github.com/Shifa402/\n648\tUrdu-Synthetic-Speech-Evaluation.\n649\t\n650\t5.2. Subjective Evaluation\n651\tTwo complementary subjective evaluation procedures\n652\tare employed. A MUSHRA-style listening test provides\n653\tcomparative quality ratings across all four systems, while\n654\tan ABX discrimination test assesses perceptual indistinguishability from natural speech for the two highest-ranked\n655\tsystems.\n656\t\n657\t5.2.1. MUSHRA-Style Listening Test\n658\tSubjective evaluation was conducted using a MUSHRAinspired protocol (International Telecommunication Union,\n659\t2015) adapted for online administration via Google Forms.\n660\tThe adaptation retains the core structural elements of\n661\tMUSHRA, specifically the simultaneous presentation of a\n662\thidden reference, a degraded anchor, and all systems under test, while replacing the standard MUSHRA software\n663\tinterface with a structured questionnaire format accessible\n664\tto participants without specialist software installation. This\n665\tapproach is consistent with practices reported in recent\n666\tremote TTS evaluation studies (Chiang et al., 2024).\n667\tEach evaluation page presented listeners with six audio\n668\tsamples for a given utterance: one reference recording drawn\n669\tfrom the source dataset representing original human speech,\n670\tone anchor recording consisting of a lower-quality degraded\n671\tversion of the same utterance, and four synthetic outputs\n672\tproduced by MMS-TTS, Indic-Parler-TTS, Edge TTS, and\n673\tGemini TTS respectively. Listeners were instructed to rate\n674\teach of the four synthetic outputs on a discrete 1–5 scale\n675\tacross three perceptual dimensions: Naturalness, defined as\n676\twhether the speech sounds human-like and fluent; Clarity,\n677\tdefined as whether the speech is intelligible and free of\n678\tartefacts; and Overall Speech Quality, defined as a holistic\n679\tjudgement relative to the reference. The reference and anchor were presented for orientation purposes and were not\n680\tthemselves rated.\n681\tA total of 20 native Urdu-speaking listeners participated\n682\tin the evaluation. Participants were were native Urdu speakers within the same demographic range. As the research\n683\tstudy was situated in Lahore and its population comprises\n684\tpredominantly native speakers of Urdu, formal language\n685\tproficiency screening was not considered necessary. The\n686\tonline Google Forms platform was selected because it was\n687\taccessible to this population without requiring attendance at\n688\ta dedicated laboratory session, thereby reducing recruitment\n689\tbarriers while maintaining a controlled listening protocol\n690\tthrough the standardised instructions detailed above. Listeners were assigned to two domains each, yielding 10\n691\tlisteners per domain pair, in order to limit session length to a\n692\tmanageable duration and to reduce listener fatigue, which is\n693\ta recognised source of rating variance in extended subjective\n694\tspeech evaluations (Le Maguer et al., 2024). No formal\n695\tphonetics training was required; participants were screened\n696\tfor self-reported normal hearing. Each listener evaluated all\n697\tfour systems within their assigned domains, ensuring that\n698\twithin-domain system comparisons are fully within-subject.\n699\t\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "3483c3dd-35ce-4c18-9530-2fe55290a039", "uuid": "195170e8-89f2-4113-858b-5523565a1bb9", "timestamp": "2026-08-04T11:46:21.983Z", "tool_use_result": {"type": "text", "file": {"filePath": "/tmp/2608.02235.txt", "content": "locally via the Hugging Face transformers library (v4.40.0)\nat a native output sample rate of 16 kHz. MMS-TTS is the\nonly fully open-weight, locally executable system in this\nevaluation, which has practical implications for deployment\nin resource-constrained or low-connectivity environments.\nIndic-Parler-TTS\n(ai4bharat / indic-parler-tts).\nParler-TTS (Lyth and King, 2024; Lacombe et al., 2024)\nis a description-conditioned audio language model that\ngenerates speech by conditioning a causal Transformer\ndecoder on both the target text and a natural-language\nspeaker description. The description is encoded by a\nfrozen Flan-T5-Large encoder (Chung et al., 2022), and\nthe resulting representations cross-attend to a GPT-2scale decoder that predicts discrete audio tokens from a\nnine-codebook Descript Audio Codec (DAC) (Lacombe\net al., 2024). A corresponding DAC decoder renders\nwaveforms at 44.1 kHz. The ai4bharat / indic-parler-tts\ncheckpoint (AI4Bharat, 2024) extends this architecture\nto twenty-two Indic languages, including Urdu, through\ncontinued pre-training on a multilingual read-speech and\naudiobook corpus assembled by AI4Bharat. Because voice\n\ncharacteristics are specified through free-text descriptions\nrather than extracted speaker embeddings, the model does\nnot require a reference recording to condition synthesis,\na property that is advantageous in low-resource settings\nwhere clean reference audio for target speakers may be\nunavailable (Lacombe et al., 2024). A fixed description\nspecifying a male speaker with a clear, moderate-paced\nvoice was used uniformly across all evaluation domains to\ncontrol for inter-domain description variation.\nMicrosoft Edge TTS (ur-PK-AsadNeural). Microsoft\nEdge TTS is a cloud-based neural TTS service that exposes\nsynthesis capability through a documented WebSocket endpoint accessed via the open-source edge-tts Python library\n(v6.1.9). The ur-PK-AsadNeural voice targets Pakistani\nUrdu and is part of Microsoft’s Azure Cognitive Services\nNeural TTS platform (Microsoft Corporation, 2023), which\nemploys a Transformer-based acoustic model combined\nwith a neural vocoder, trained on professionally recorded\nstudio speech. Synthesis is produced at 24 kHz. Microsoft\ndoes not publicly disclose training corpus composition,\nmodel architecture specifications, or speaker recording details for production neural voices, and accordingly this system is treated as an opaque commercial baseline within the\nevaluation. Its inclusion is motivated by the documented\nadoption of the AsadNeural voice in Urdu-language assistive\ntechnology and digital media applications, making its performance profile directly relevant to practitioners evaluating\ndeployment options.\nGoogle Gemini TTS. Google Gemini TTS generates\nspeech natively through the Gemini 2.5 Flash model with\naudio output enabled, accessed via the google-generativeai\nPython SDK (v0.8.3) using the gemini-2.5-flash-preview-tts endpoint. Unlike conventional cascaded TTS pipelines\nthat decompose synthesis into independent text analysis,\nacoustic modelling, and vocoding stages, Gemini TTS renders speech directly from the language model’s contextual\nrepresentations through a dedicated speech decoder, a design that has been reported to improve prosodic coherence\nover multi-sentence inputs (Google DeepMind, 2025). The\nsystem is configured using the PrebuiltVoiceConfig parameter; the Charon voice preset was applied uniformly across\nall evaluation domains to isolate domain-specific synthesis\nvariation from speaker-selection effects. Output audio was\nproduced at 24 kHz. Google does not publicly disclose the\nUrdu training data volume, language-specific fine-tuning\nprocedures, or internal model specifications for this system.\nTo the best of our knowledge, this paper presents the first\npublished objective and subjective evaluation of Gemini\nTTS on Urdu speech.\n\n3.3. Comparative System Properties\nTable 1 summarises the architectural, training, and deployment properties of the four systems. Differences along\nfour dimensions are particularly relevant to interpreting\ndomain-specific results. (i) Synthesis paradigm: MMS-TTS\nuses non-autoregressive flow-based generation; Indic-ParlerTTS uses autoregressive discrete token prediction; Edge TTS\n\nJafar, Sarmad, Yousaf & Bashir: Preprint submitted to Computer Speech & Language\n\nPage 4 of 17\n\n\fDomain-Specific Evaluation of Urdu TTS Systems\nTable 1\nArchitectural and deployment properties of the four evaluated Urdu TTS systems. Proprietary systems are marked with (†) to\nindicate that architecture and training details are not publicly disclosed.\nSystem\n\nParadigm\n\nTraining Scope\n\nSpeaker Control\n\nRate\n\nAccess\n\nMMS-TTS\n\nVITS: VAE + flow + GAN\n(Kim et al., 2021)\nAutoregressive LM + DAC\n(Lacombe et al., 2024)\nNeural (undisclosed) (Microsoft Corporation, 2023)\nAudio LM, direct render\n(Google DeepMind, 2025)\n\nMassively multilingual\n(1,107 lang.)\n22 Indic languages\n\nSingle speaker (M)\n\n16 kHz\n\nOpen weight\n\n44.1 kHz\n\nOpen weight\n\nProprietary (ur-PK)\n\nDescriptionconditioned\nPreset (M, ur-PK)\n\n24 kHz\n\nCloud API\n\nProprietary multilingual\n\nPreset voices\n\n24 kHz\n\nCloud API\n\nIndic-ParlerTTS\nEdge TTS†\nGemini TTS†\n\nuses an undisclosed Transformer-based pipeline; and Gemini TTS uses direct audio rendering from a large language\nmodel. (ii) Training scope: MMS-TTS and Gemini TTS are\ntrained at massive multilingual scale, whereas Indic-ParlerTTS targets a defined set of twenty-two Indic languages and\nEdge TTS is a dedicated Pakistani Urdu voice. (iii) Speaker\ncontrol: MMS-TTS produces a fixed single-speaker output;\nIndic-Parler-TTS accepts free-text description conditioning;\nEdge and Gemini TTS offer discrete preset selection. (iv)\nOutput sample rate: 16 kHz (MMS-TTS), 24 kHz (Edge\nTTS, Gemini TTS), and 44.1 kHz (Indic-Parler-TTS); prior\nto objective evaluation, audio was resampled at metricspecific rates (22.05 kHz for MCD and 16 kHz for F0 RMSE\nand Resemblyzer speaker embeddings) to match each feature\nextractor’s requirements (see Section 5).\n\n4. Domain-Wise Dataset Setup\nThis section describes the construction and organisation\nof the evaluation corpus used in this study. It motivates the\nneed for domain diversity in TTS evaluation and presents\neach of the four speech domains, namely Formal, Conversational, Emotional, and Literary/Storytelling.\n\n4.1. Need for Domain Diversity in TTS Evaluation\nThe construction of a domain-diverse dataset is an important factor in evaluating modern text-to-speech (TTS)\nsystems, particularly for low-resource languages such as\nUrdu. Recent research has shown that the quality of speech\nsynthesis is strongly influenced not only by model architecture but also by the diversity and distribution of evaluation\ndata between speaking styles and domains (Kong, Kim and\nBae, 2020; Tan, Qin, Soong and Liu, 2021). Traditional evaluation datasets often focus on homogeneous read speech,\nwhich limits their ability to capture real-world variability\nin prosody, emotion, and communicative intent. As TTS\nsystems continue to evolve towards more expressive and\ncontrollable generation, the need for diverse and domainaware datasets has become increasingly important for robust\nbenchmarking.\nIn this study, the evaluation dataset is organized into\nfour distinct speech domains, namely Formal, Conversational, Literary/Storytelling, and Emotional. Each domain\n\nrepresents a different communicative and stylistic setting,\ncharacterized by unique linguistic and prosodic patterns.\nThis domain selection aligns with recent advances in expressive and controllable speech synthesis, where systems\nare expected to generalize across multiple speaking styles\nrather than in a single uniform voice setting (Wang et al.,\n2017; Kim et al., 2021). The resulting dataset design enables\na more comprehensive evaluation of Urdu TTS systems\nunder varied real-world conditions, supporting fine-grained\nanalysis across both neutral and expressive speech scenarios.\n\n4.2. Domain 1: Formal Speech\nThis subsection defines the Formal speech domain and\ndescribes the datasets selected to represent it in the evaluation corpus. Together, these datasets capture the linguistic\nand communicative characteristics of formal speech required\nfor domain-specific evaluation.\n\n4.2.1. Domain Characteristics\nThe Formal domain represents controlled and structured\nspeech produced in situations where the primary objective is clarity, correctness, and linguistic precision. This\ncan include read-aloud scenarios such as news broadcasting, academic narration, and scripted announcements, where\nspeakers typically follow prepared text without spontaneous\ndeviations. The importance of this domain lies in its role as\na baseline condition for text-to-speech evaluation, as it minimizes variability in speaking style, emotion, and discourse\nstructure.\nIn the context of TTS systems, formal read speech provides a stable reference point for assessing fundamental\nsynthesis capabilities such as pronunciation accuracy, fluency, and prosodic consistency. Since the linguistic input in\nthis domain is well-formed and predictable, it allows for a\nmore controlled evaluation of system performance, making\nit widely used in both benchmarking and comparative analysis of speech synthesis models.\n4.2.2. Selected Dataset(s)\nThis domain is constructed using publicly available multilingual speech corpora that provide high-quality, studiolike read speech recordings under controlled acoustic conditions. The Google FLEURS dataset is used as a primary\n\nJafar, Sarmad, Yousaf & Bashir: Preprint submitted to Computer Speech & Language\n\nPage 5 of 17\n\n\fDomain-Specific Evaluation of Urdu TTS Systems\n\nsource due to its well-standardized recording protocol, multilingual coverage, and consistent speech delivery across\nspeakers and languages. In this study, only Urdu-language\nutterances are selected to ensure linguistic relevance, while\nspeaker-level filtering is applied to retain male voice recordings for maintaining uniformity across all experimental conditions.\nDataset link: https : / / huggingface . co / datasets / google /\nfleurs\n\nIn addition, the UrduSpeech dataset is incorporated as a\ncomplementary source of formal read speech. This dataset\nprovides high-quality Urdu speech with multiple speaking\nstyles and linguistic variations. For the purpose of this work,\nonly Urdu samples are retained, as the corpus also contains\nclosely related Indic language data such as Kashmiri. Furthermore, style-based filtering is applied to select Wiki-style\nutterances, ensuring adherence to a formal and structured\nreading modality. Gender-based filtering is again applied to\nretain male speakers, ensuring consistency across datasets\nused in the evaluation framework.\nDataset link: https://huggingface.co/datasets/humairawan/\nUrduSpeech\n\n4.3. Domain 2: Conversational Speech\nThis subsection introduces the Conversational speech\ndomain and the dataset selected to represent it within the\nevaluation corpus.\n\n4.3.1. Domain Characteristics\nThe conversational domain represents spontaneous and\nsemi-structured dialogue-based communication that occurs\nin everyday interactions. Unlike formal read speech, this\ndomain is characterized by natural speaking patterns, including variations in intonation, informal phrasing, and contextual dependencies between utterances. The importance\nof this domain lies in its ability to capture realistic speech\nbehavior, making it essential for evaluating the robustness of\ntext-to-speech systems in non-scripted and socially interactive scenarios. This domain introduces greater variability in\nprosody, rhythm, and speaking style, providing a more challenging evaluation setting compared to controlled speech\nconditions.\n4.3.2. Selected Dataset(s)\nThe data for this domain are derived from the UrduSpeech dataset publicly available on Hugging Face. Since\nthe dataset contains multilingual recordings, only Urdulanguage utterances are retained for this study. A style-based\nfiltering criterion is applied to isolate conversational speech\nsamples and only recordings labeled as conversational are\nincluded. With both male and female voice audios being\npresent, only male speaker recordings are selected.\nDataset link: https://huggingface.co/datasets/humairawan/\nUrduSpeech\n\n4.4. Domain 3: Emotional Speech\nThis subsection describes the Emotional speech domain,\noutlining the acoustic and affective properties that distinguish it from other domains and the dataset from which\nemotional utterances are drawn.\n\n4.4.1. Domain Characteristics\nThe emotional domain represents expressive speech\ncharacterized by systematic variations in prosody, pitch,\nintensity, and speaking rate that correspond to different\naffective states. Unlike formal or conversational speech,\nthis domain explicitly encodes emotional information within\nthe acoustic signal, making it significantly more variable\nand challenging for text-to-speech systems. The inclusion\nof emotional speech is essential for evaluating expressive\nspeech synthesis capabilities, as it tests whether a system\ncan generate natural and contextually appropriate emotional\nvariation beyond neutral speaking styles.\n4.4.2. Selected Dataset(s)\nThe emotional speech data is obtained from the Urdu\nSpeech Emotion Corpus (UrSEC), a comprehensive dataset\ndesigned for speech emotion recognition in the Urdu language. The dataset contains 3,500 annotated speech samples\ncollected from professional actors and covers seven emotional categories: Angry, Fear, Boredom, Disgust, Happy,\nNeutral, and Sad. The recordings are sourced from naturally\nspoken Urdu dialogues derived from drama serials and telefilms, ensuring realistic and contextually rich utterances.\nDataset link: https : / / data . mendeley . com / datasets /\njcpfjnk5c2/4\n\nFor the purpose of this study, a balanced subset of\nsamples is selected across all emotion categories to ensure\nuniform representation of affective states. The Neutral category is excluded from evaluation to avoid overlap with the\nFormal and Conversational Speech domain defined in this\nwork. Only male-speaker recordings are retained to maintain\nconsistency across all experimental domains.\n\n4.5. Domain 4: Literary/Storytelling Speech\nThis subsection describes the Literary/Storytelling speech\ndomain, detailing the narrative and prosodic features that\ncharacterise it and the dataset used for its construction.\n\n4.5.1. Domain Characteristics\nThis domain represents expressive narrative speech used\nin contexts such as storytelling, book reading, and dramatic narration. Unlike formal or conversational speech,\nthis domain is characterized by dynamic prosodic variation,\nextended utterance structure, and deliberate modulation of\npitch and rhythm to convey narrative meaning and emotional\ncontext. This makes it an important evaluation setting for\ntext-to-speech systems aimed at expressive synthesis, as it\ntests the ability of models to generate natural and engaging\nnarrative speech beyond neutral or conversational styles.\n\nJafar, Sarmad, Yousaf & Bashir: Preprint submitted to Computer Speech & Language\n\nPage 6 of 17\n\n\fDomain-Specific Evaluation of Urdu TTS Systems\n\n4.5.2. Selected Dataset(s)\nThe Literary/Storytelling domain is constructed using\nthe diverse UrduSpeech dataset, which contains multiple\nspeaking styles. From this dataset, only Urdu-language samples are selected. Additionally, style-based filtering is applied to retain BOOK-style recordings, which best represent\nstorytelling and literary narration. Speaker-level filtering is\nfurther applied to maintain male-speaker consistency across\nall evaluation domains.\nDataset link: https://huggingface.co/datasets/humairawan/\nUrduSpeech\n\n5. Evaluation Methodology\nThis section presents the evaluation framework employed in this study. To provide a comprehensive assessment\nof text-to-speech (TTS) quality, the framework integrates\ncomplementary subjective and objective evaluation methodologies, capturing perceptual quality, speaker similarity,\nand acoustic fidelity. Together, these evaluation components\nprovide a holistic basis for comparing TTS systems across\ndifferent speech domains while ensuring a reproducible and\nconsistent benchmarking process.\n\n5.1. Evaluation Framework Design\nThe evaluation pipeline is organised around two complementary design principles. The first is metric complementarity: no single metric captures all perceptually relevant dimensions of synthesis quality (Kirkland et al., 2023;\nLe Maguer et al., 2024), and accordingly the framework\ncombines subjective listening tests with three classes of objective measurement, namely speaker identity preservation,\nspectral envelope fidelity, and prosodic accuracy. The second\nis domain stratification: all metrics are computed separately\nfor each of the four speech domains (Formal, Conversational,\nLiterary/Storytelling, and Emotional) rather than aggregated\nacross domains, so that domain-specific performance variation is directly observable rather than obscured by averaging. Together, these principles operationalise the task-andsituation-specific evaluation paradigm advocated by Bailly\net al. (2025) for the specific case of Urdu TTS.\nThe complete pipeline proceeds as follows. For each domain, reference recordings are paired with synthetic outputs\nfrom each of the four TTS systems at the utterance level,\nyielding 960 audio pairs in total (4 systems × 4 domains × 60\nutterances per domain). Each pair is then passed through four\nevaluation modules: a subjective MUSHRA-style listening\ntest, a speaker similarity scoring procedure via Resemblyzer\n(Wan et al., 2018), mel-cepstral distortion (MCD) computation (Kubichek, 1993), and fundamental frequency rootmean-square error (F0 RMSE) computation (Kominek and\nBlack, 2008). An additional forced-choice ABX discrimination test is conducted as a second-stage evaluation, applied\nto the two top-performing systems identified through the\nMUSHRA stage, to assess perceptual indistinguishability\nfrom natural speech. All evaluation scripts and result tables are publicly available at https://github.com/Shifa402/\nUrdu-Synthetic-Speech-Evaluation.\n\n5.2. Subjective Evaluation\nTwo complementary subjective evaluation procedures\nare employed. A MUSHRA-style listening test provides\ncomparative quality ratings across all four systems, while\nan ABX discrimination test assesses perceptual indistinguishability from natural speech for the two highest-ranked\nsystems.\n\n5.2.1. MUSHRA-Style Listening Test\nSubjective evaluation was conducted using a MUSHRAinspired protocol (International Telecommunication Union,\n2015) adapted for online administration via Google Forms.\nThe adaptation retains the core structural elements of\nMUSHRA, specifically the simultaneous presentation of a\nhidden reference, a degraded anchor, and all systems under test, while replacing the standard MUSHRA software\ninterface with a structured questionnaire format accessible\nto participants without specialist software installation. This\napproach is consistent with practices reported in recent\nremote TTS evaluation studies (Chiang et al., 2024).\nEach evaluation page presented listeners with six audio\nsamples for a given utterance: one reference recording drawn\nfrom the source dataset representing original human speech,\none anchor recording consisting of a lower-quality degraded\nversion of the same utterance, and four synthetic outputs\nproduced by MMS-TTS, Indic-Parler-TTS, Edge TTS, and\nGemini TTS respectively. Listeners were instructed to rate\neach of the four synthetic outputs on a discrete 1–5 scale\nacross three perceptual dimensions: Naturalness, defined as\nwhether the speech sounds human-like and fluent; Clarity,\ndefined as whether the speech is intelligible and free of\nartefacts; and Overall Speech Quality, defined as a holistic\njudgement relative to the reference. The reference and anchor were presented for orientation purposes and were not\nthemselves rated.\nA total of 20 native Urdu-speaking listeners participated\nin the evaluation. Participants were were native Urdu speakers within the same demographic range. As the research\nstudy was situated in Lahore and its population comprises\npredominantly native speakers of Urdu, formal language\nproficiency screening was not considered necessary. The\nonline Google Forms platform was selected because it was\naccessible to this population without requiring attendance at\na dedicated laboratory session, thereby reducing recruitment\nbarriers while maintaining a controlled listening protocol\nthrough the standardised instructions detailed above. Listeners were assigned to two domains each, yielding 10\nlisteners per domain pair, in order to limit session length to a\nmanageable duration and to reduce listener fatigue, which is\na recognised source of rating variance in extended subjective\nspeech evaluations (Le Maguer et al., 2024). No formal\nphonetics training was required; participants were screened\nfor self-reported normal hearing. Each listener evaluated all\nfour systems within their assigned domains, ensuring that\nwithin-domain system comparisons are fully within-subject.\n", "numLines": 400, "startLine": 300, "totalLines": 2047}}}
{"type": "assistant", "message": {"id": "msg_1785843986999", "type": "message", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "tool_use", "id": "chatcmpl-tool-ab7571e68633b8ae", "name": "Read", "input": {"file_path": "/tmp/2608.02235.txt", "limit": 400, "offset": 700}}], "model": "GLM-5.2-w4a8", "stop_reason": null, "stop_sequence": null, "usage": {"input_tokens": 0, "output_tokens": 0, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 0, "cache_read_input_tokens": 0}, "context_management": null}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "3483c3dd-35ce-4c18-9530-2fe55290a039", "uuid": "5d163b8f-2c8f-4dcf-bc9f-8abc89a59783"}
{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-ab7571e68633b8ae", "type": "tool_result", "content": "700\tJafar, Sarmad, Yousaf & Bashir: Preprint submitted to Computer Speech & Language\n701\t\n702\tPage 7 of 17\n703\t\n704\t\fDomain-Specific Evaluation of Urdu TTS Systems\n705\t\n706\tIt is acknowledged that a panel of 20 listeners, with\n707\t10 per domain, is smaller than the listener pools recommended by the ITU-R BS.1534 MUSHRA standard (International Telecommunication Union, 2015), and that the\n708\tGoogle Forms interface does not implement the continuous\n709\t0–100 slider specified by the standard. These deviations\n710\tare documented explicitly so that readers may calibrate the\n711\tstrength of the subjective findings accordingly. The objective\n712\tmetrics reported in Section 5.3 do not share these limitations\n713\tand are accorded commensurate weight in the cross-metric\n714\tsynthesis presented in Section 7.6.\n715\t\n716\t5.2.2. ABX Discrimination Test\n717\tThe ABX discrimination test was conducted as a secondstage evaluation, applied selectively to the two systems that\n718\tachieved the highest MUSHRA-style ratings in the first\n719\tstage, namely Edge TTS and Gemini TTS. This two-stage\n720\tdesign is motivated by the observation that ABX discrimination is most informative when applied to high-performing\n721\tsystems, given that lower-quality systems are trivially distinguishable from natural speech and contribute limited diagnostic value to a forced-choice paradigm (Schatz, Peddinti,\n722\tBach, Jansen, Hermansky and Dupoux, 2013). By restricting the ABX stage to the top-ranked systems identified\n723\tthrough the MUSHRA procedure, the test directly addresses\n724\tthe more challenging and practically relevant question of\n725\twhether the best-performing Urdu TTS systems approach\n726\tperceptual indistinguishability from natural speech.\n727\tIn each ABX trial, listeners were presented with three\n728\tstimuli: a reference utterance (A), a second presentation\n729\tof the same reference (B), and an unknown sample (X)\n730\tdrawn from either the reference or a synthetic output produced by Edge-TTS or Gemini TTS. Listeners indicated\n731\twhether X was perceptually more similar to A or to B. A\n732\tdiscrimination rate significantly above 50% indicates that the\n733\tsynthetic output remains perceptually distinguishable from\n734\tnatural speech, whereas a rate at or near 50% indicates nearindistinguishability. The test was administered under conditions identical to those of the MUSHRA-style listening test,\n735\tvia Google Forms, using the same standardised listening\n736\tinstructions, and within the same online session. Participants\n737\twere native Urdu speakers aged 18–25 years, recruited from\n738\tthe immediate social and academic networks of the research\n739\tteam. The listener panel was gender-balanced, with an equal\n740\tnumber of male and female participants, to mitigate potential\n741\tsystematic gender-based perceptual bias in the discrimination of synthesised Urdu speech. As with the MUSHRAstyle evaluation, listeners were assigned to two domains\n742\teach, yielding 10 listeners per domain pair, and both systems\n743\twere evaluated within each listener’s assigned domains to\n744\tpreserve the within-subject comparison structure.\n745\t\n746\t5.3. Objective Evaluation\n747\tThree complementary objective metrics are employed to\n748\tassess different aspects of synthesis quality. Speaker similarity, computed using Resemblyzer, evaluates voice identity\n749\t\n750\tpreservation; mel-cepstral distortion (MCD) measures spectral envelope fidelity; and fundamental frequency root mean\n751\tsquare error (F0 RMSE) quantifies pitch contour accuracy.\n752\t\n753\t5.3.1. Speaker Similarity via Resemblyzer\n754\tSpeaker identity preservation was quantified using the\n755\tcosine similarity between d-vector speaker embeddings extracted by Resemblyzer (Wan et al., 2018). Resemblyzer implements the Generalised End-to-End (GE2E) loss-trained\n756\tLSTM encoder of Wan et al. (2018), which maps a variablelength speech segment to a fixed-dimensional embedding in\n757\ta speaker-discriminative space. For each reference-synthetic\n758\tpair, embeddings were extracted independently and their\n759\tcosine similarity was computed; values approaching 1.0\n760\tindicate higher speaker identity similarity between the reference and the synthesised output.\n761\tOne methodological consideration specific to this evaluation concerns MMS-TTS, which produces a fixed singlespeaker output whose voice identity is independent of the\n762\treference speaker. All reference and synthetic audio in this\n763\tstudy is restricted to male speakers, so every comparison\n764\tis within-gender; nonetheless, the fixed MMS-TTS voice\n765\tdiffers in speaker identity from each domain’s reference\n766\tspeaker, and its cosine similarity therefore primarily reflects\n767\tspeaker-identity mismatch rather than synthesis degradation, consistent with cautions raised for speaker-similarity\n768\tevaluation of single-speaker systems (Wang, Chen, Wu,\n769\tZhang, Zhou, Liu, Chen, Liu, Wang, Li et al., 2023a). All audio was resampled to 16 kHz prior to embedding extraction,\n770\tas required by the Resemblyzer encoder input specification.\n771\t5.3.2. Mel-Cepstral Distortion\n772\tMel-cepstral distortion (MCD) (Kubichek, 1993) quantifies spectral envelope similarity between a reference and\n773\ta synthetic utterance by computing the Euclidean distance\n774\tbetween their mel-cepstral coefficient sequences after dynamic time warping (DTW) alignment. Lower MCD values\n775\tindicate greater spectral similarity. MCD is computed as:\n776\t√\n777\t√\n778\t𝑇 √∑\n779\t𝐾 (\n780\t)2\n781\t10 2 1 ∑ √\n782\t√\n783\tref − 𝑐 syn\n784\t𝑐𝑘,𝑡\n785\tMCD =\n786\t𝑘,𝑡\n787\tln 10 𝑇 𝑡=1 𝑘=1\n788\t\n789\t(1)\n790\t\n791\tsyn\n792\t\n793\tref and 𝑐\n794\twhere 𝑐𝑘,𝑡\n795\tare the 𝑘-th mel-cepstral coefficients of\n796\t𝑘,𝑡\n797\tthe 𝑡-th DTW-aligned reference and synthetic frame pair,\n798\t𝑇 is the number of aligned frames, and 𝐾 = 13 melcepstral coefficients are retained following standard practice\n799\t(Kominek\n800\tand Black, 2008); the leading scaling constant\n801\t√\n802\t10 2∕ ln 10 ≈ 6.142 expresses the distortion in decibels.\n803\tMel-cepstra were obtained from a WORLD spectral envelope followed by SPTK mel-cepstral analysis (order 13,\n804\twarping coefficient 𝛼 = 0.65) using the pymcd implementation, with DTW alignment on the 𝐶1 –𝐶13 coefficients\n805\tperformed by fastdtw under a Euclidean cost. Audio was\n806\tresampled to 22.05 kHz for this analysis, ensuring comparability across systems with differing native output sample\n807\trates.\n808\t\n809\tJafar, Sarmad, Yousaf & Bashir: Preprint submitted to Computer Speech & Language\n810\t\n811\tPage 8 of 17\n812\t\n813\t\fDomain-Specific Evaluation of Urdu TTS Systems\n814\tTable 2\n815\tSummary of evaluation metrics, the quality dimension each captures, and result directionality.\n816\t\n817\tMetric\n818\t\n819\tType\n820\t\n821\tDimension\n822\t\n823\tBetter\n824\t\n825\tMUSHRA-style rating\n826\tABX discrimination\n827\tSpeaker similarity\n828\tMCD\n829\tF0 RMSE\n830\t\n831\tSubjective\n832\tSubjective\n833\tObjective\n834\tObjective\n835\tObjective\n836\t\n837\tNaturalness, clarity, overall quality\n838\tPerceptual distinguishability from natural speech\n839\tSpeaker identity preservation\n840\tSpectral envelope fidelity\n841\tPitch contour accuracy\n842\t\n843\tHigher\n844\tLower\n845\tHigher\n846\tLower\n847\tLower\n848\t\n849\t5.3.3. Fundamental Frequency RMSE\n850\tPitch accuracy was assessed using the root-mean-square\n851\terror between the fundamental frequency (F0) contours of\n852\tthe reference and synthetic utterances, expressed in cents on\n853\ta logarithmic scale (Kominek and Black, 2008):\n854\t√\n855\t√\n856\t(\n857\tsyn )2\n858\t√ ∑\n859\t𝐹 0𝑖\n860\t√1 𝑁\n861\t1200 log2\n862\tF0 RMSE = √\n863\t𝑁 𝑖=1\n864\t𝐹 0ref\n865\t\n866\t(2)\n867\t\n868\t𝑖\n869\t\n870\twhere 𝑁 is the number of voiced frames present in both\n871\tthe reference and synthetic signals after DTW alignment.\n872\tThe cent scale is employed because approximately 5 cents\n873\tapproximates one just-noticeable difference in pitch under\n874\ttypical listening conditions (Kominek and Black, 2008).\n875\tF0 was extracted with pyworld using the harvest algorithm\n876\tfollowed by stonemask refinement (50–800 Hz search range)\n877\tat a 16 kHz sampling rate, and the reference and synthetic\n878\tcontours were compared on the frame pairs of a DTW\n879\talignment. Unvoiced frames and frames in which either the\n880\treference or synthetic F0 estimate was detected as zero were\n881\texcluded from the RMSE computation to avoid artefacts attributable to voicing detection errors. Because each synthetic\n882\tutterance is compared against a natural reference produced\n883\tby a different speaker, the reported F0 RMSE reflects both\n884\tpitch-contour deviation and a speaker-dependent register\n885\toffset; the interpretation of such reference-distance metrics\n886\tis discussed further in Section 8.3.\n887\t\n888\t5.4. Evaluation Pipeline Summary\n889\tTable 2 summarises the four evaluation modules, the\n890\tdimension of synthesis quality each addresses, and the directionality of each metric. The complete pipeline, including\n891\tpreprocessing scripts, metric computation notebooks, and\n892\traw result tables, is publicly released at https://github.\n893\tcom/Shifa402/Urdu-Synthetic-Speech-Evaluation to support\n894\treproducibility and to serve as an extensible baseline for\n895\tfuture Urdu TTS benchmarking.\n896\t\n897\t6. Experimental Setup\n898\tThis section presents the implementation details of the\n899\tsynthesis and evaluation pipeline. It describes the inference configuration for each TTS system and the hardware\n900\tand software environment used to conduct all experiments,\n901\t\n902\tensuring reproducibility and consistent evaluation across\n903\tsystems.\n904\t\n905\t6.1. Implementation Details\n906\tAll synthesis and evaluation were implemented in Python\n907\twithin cloud-hosted notebooks. For each domain, the reference utterances were drawn from the source datasets\n908\tdescribed in Section 4 and exported as single-channel WAV\n909\tfiles. Each reference utterance was then synthesised once by\n910\tevery one of the four TTS systems directly from its original\n911\tUrdu Perso-Arabic transcript, without manual transliteration\n912\tor romanisation. For every system the speaker or voice\n913\tconfiguration was held fixed across all four domains, so that\n914\tthe communicative domain remained the only manipulated\n915\tfactor.\n916\tMMS-TTS. The model was instantiated as a VitsModel with its associated AutoTokenizer from the facebook/mms-tts-urd-script_arabic checkpoint through the Hugging\n917\tFace transformers library. Waveforms were produced in\n918\ta single non-autoregressive forward pass under torch.no_\n919\tgrad() at the model’s native 16 kHz output rate. Synthesis is\n920\tdeterministic and no decoding hyperparameters were tuned.\n921\tIndic-Parler-TTS. The model was loaded as\n922\tParlerTTSForConditionalGeneration\n923\tfrom\n924\tai4bharat /\n925\tindic-parler-tts, with separate tokenisers for the transcript\n926\tand for the natural-language speaker description. A single\n927\tfixed description, “Rohit’s voice is clear and natural with a\n928\tmoderate speed and pitch”, which selects the named male\n929\tspeaker Rohit, was used unchanged across all domains to\n930\thold voice identity constant. Audio tokens were generated\n931\twith do_sample=True and temperature = 0.8 and decoded to\n932\twaveforms at the native 44.1 kHz.\n933\tMicrosoft Edge TTS. Speech was synthesised through\n934\tthe edge-tts client using the ur-PK-AsadNeural voice with\n935\tthe service’s default prosody (rate, pitch, and volume left at\n936\ttheir default values). Audio was returned at 24 kHz.\n937\tGoogle Gemini TTS. Speech was generated through\n938\tthe gemini-2.5-flash-preview-tts model with response_\n939\tmodalities set to audio and a PrebuiltVoiceConfig specifying\n940\tthe Charon preset voice, again held constant across domains.\n941\tAudio was returned as 24 kHz PCM.\n942\tAcross the four domains, 60 reference utterances per\n943\tdomain were used for the objective metrics; for the Formal domain these comprised 30 FLEURS and 30 UrduSpeech Wikipedia-style utterances. This yields 4 systems ×\n944\t\n945\tJafar, Sarmad, Yousaf & Bashir: Preprint submitted to Computer Speech & Language\n946\t\n947\tPage 9 of 17\n948\t\n949\t\fDomain-Specific Evaluation of Urdu TTS Systems\n950\tTable 3\n951\tKey software components used for synthesis and objective\n952\tevaluation.\n953\tComponent\n954\t\n955\tRole\n956\t\n957\tVersion\n958\t\n959\ttransformers\n960\tparler-tts\n961\t\n962\tMMS-TTS / tokenisers\n963\tIndic-Parler-TTS\n964\t\n965\tedge-tts\n966\tgoogle-generativeai\n967\tresemblyzer\n968\tlibrosa\n969\tpyworld\n970\tpymcd\n971\tpysptk\n972\tfastdtw\n973\t\n974\tEdge TTS client\n975\tGemini TTS client\n976\tSpeaker embeddings\n977\tAudio I/O, resampling\n978\tF0 extraction (WORLD)\n979\tMel-cepstral distortion\n980\tMel-cepstral analysis\n981\tDTW alignment\n982\t\n983\t4.40.0\n984\trelease at\n985\taccess\n986\t6.1.9\n987\t0.8.3\n988\t0.1.4\n989\t0.11.0\n990\t0.3.5\n991\t0.2.1\n992\t1.0.1\n993\t0.3.4\n994\t\n995\tconcluding with a cross-metric synthesis. For each metric,\n996\tresults are reported first at the domain level, aggregated\n997\tacross all four systems, and then at the system level within\n998\teach domain. This organisation directly addresses the core\n999\tresearch question: whether synthesis quality varies systematically across communicative domains and whether domain\n1000\tsensitivity is consistent across evaluation dimensions. All\n1001\t960 reference–synthetic pairs were evaluated without computation failure, confirming complete corpus coverage.\n1002\t\n1003\t7.1. MUSHRA-Style Listening Test Scores\n1004\tMUSHRA-style results are analysed at both the domain\n1005\tand system levels. Domain-level results are first presented by\n1006\taggregating scores across all systems, followed by systemlevel analyses within each speech domain.\n1007\t\n1008\t7.1.1. Domain-Level MUSHRA Results\n1009\t4 domains × 60 = 960 reference–synthetic pairs for speaker\n1010\tsimilarity, MCD, and F0 RMSE. The subjective listening\n1011\ttests used a 30-utterance subset per domain to limit session\n1012\tlength and listener fatigue. Reference and synthetic clips\n1013\twere paired by filename stem. Because the two cloud services (Edge, Gemini) and the sampling-based Indic-ParlerTTS decoder are not seed-controllable, a single synthesis\n1014\twas generated per utterance; MMS-TTS and the two cloud\n1015\tpresets are otherwise deterministic for a fixed input.\n1016\t\n1017\t6.2. Hardware and Software Configuration\n1018\tThe neural synthesis systems (MMS-TTS and IndicParler-TTS) and the speaker-embedding extraction were executed on a single CUDA-enabled GPU within a Google Colab environment running Python 3.12, while the two cloud\n1019\tsystems (Edge TTS and Gemini TTS) were accessed over\n1020\ttheir respective network APIs. The mel-cepstral distortion\n1021\tand fundamental-frequency computations are CPU-bound\n1022\tand were run locally.\n1023\tSpeaker similarity was computed with the GE2E-trained\n1024\tVoiceEncoder of Resemblyzer (Wan et al., 2018): reference\n1025\tand synthetic clips were passed through preprocess_wav\n1026\t(16 kHz resampling with voice-activity trimming) and compared by the cosine similarity of their utterance embeddings.\n1027\tThe MCD and F0 RMSE were computed by a single inhouse script. For MCD, mel-cepstral coefficients (order 13,\n1028\t𝛼 = 0.65) were extracted from a WORLD spectral envelope\n1029\tvia SPTK using the pymcd library at 22.05 kHz, DTWaligned with fastdtw under a Euclidean cost, and scored\n1030\twith the standard Kominek–Black scaling constant. For\n1031\tF0 RMSE, audio was resampled to 16 kHz mono and F0\n1032\twas extracted with pyworld (harvest+stonemask, 50–800 Hz),\n1033\twith the reference and synthetic contours compared in cents\n1034\ton DTW frame pairs. Key software components and versions\n1035\tare listed in Table 3.\n1036\t\n1037\t7. Results\n1038\tThis section presents evaluation results organised by\n1039\tmetric, proceeding from subjective perceptual assessment\n1040\tto objective acoustic and speaker identity measures, and\n1041\t\n1042\tFigure 1: Domain-level mean MUSHRA-style scores.\n1043\t\n1044\t7.1.2. System-Level MUSHRA Results\n1045\tTable 4: Per-system mean MUSHRA-style scores (1–5\n1046\tscale) by domain. Conv. = Conversational, Lit. = Literary/Storytelling, Form. = Formal, Emot. = Emotional. Bold\n1047\t= best system per domain (column).\n1048\tSystem\n1049\t\n1050\tConv.\n1051\t\n1052\tLit.\n1053\t\n1054\tForm.\n1055\t\n1056\tEmot.\n1057\t\n1058\tOverall\n1059\t\n1060\tEdge TTS\n1061\tGemini TTS\n1062\tIndic-Parler-TTS\n1063\tMMS-TTS\n1064\t\n1065\t3.093\n1066\t3.148\n1067\t2.148\n1068\t2.556\n1069\t\n1070\t3.361\n1071\t3.602\n1072\t2.214\n1073\t2.631\n1074\t\n1075\t3.373\n1076\t3.244\n1077\t2.738\n1078\t2.619\n1079\t\n1080\t1.963\n1081\t2.167\n1082\t1.296\n1083\t1.481\n1084\t\n1085\t2.947\n1086\t3.040\n1087\t2.099\n1088\t2.322\n1089\t\n1090\tNote: SDs (in parentheses, per domain-system cell) – Edge TTS: 0.442,\n1091\t1.071, 1.210, 0.666; Gemini TTS: 0.487, 1.097, 1.416, 0.739;\n1092\tIndic-Parler-TTS: 0.650, 1.103, 1.001, 0.496; MMS-TTS: 0.598, 1.232,\n1093\t1.090, 0.569 (order: Conv., Lit., Form., Emot.).\n1094\t\n1095\tGemini TTS remains the top-ranked system overall\n1096\t(3.040), narrowly ahead of Edge TTS (2.947), with IndicParler-TTS (2.099) and MMS-TTS (2.322) trailing substantially. Gemini TTS leads on three of four domains (Conversational, Literary/Storytelling, Emotional); Edge TTS leads\n1097\tonly on Formal (3.373 vs. 3.244). The Emotional domain\n1098\tagain produces the lowest scores for every system, with even\n1099\tthe best performer (Gemini, 2.167) falling well below the\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "3483c3dd-35ce-4c18-9530-2fe55290a039", "uuid": "d28efebd-564c-4196-8e83-6c45f97eebd0", "timestamp": "2026-08-04T11:46:29.429Z", "tool_use_result": {"type": "text", "file": {"filePath": "/tmp/2608.02235.txt", "content": "Jafar, Sarmad, Yousaf & Bashir: Preprint submitted to Computer Speech & Language\n\nPage 7 of 17\n\n\fDomain-Specific Evaluation of Urdu TTS Systems\n\nIt is acknowledged that a panel of 20 listeners, with\n10 per domain, is smaller than the listener pools recommended by the ITU-R BS.1534 MUSHRA standard (International Telecommunication Union, 2015), and that the\nGoogle Forms interface does not implement the continuous\n0–100 slider specified by the standard. These deviations\nare documented explicitly so that readers may calibrate the\nstrength of the subjective findings accordingly. The objective\nmetrics reported in Section 5.3 do not share these limitations\nand are accorded commensurate weight in the cross-metric\nsynthesis presented in Section 7.6.\n\n5.2.2. ABX Discrimination Test\nThe ABX discrimination test was conducted as a secondstage evaluation, applied selectively to the two systems that\nachieved the highest MUSHRA-style ratings in the first\nstage, namely Edge TTS and Gemini TTS. This two-stage\ndesign is motivated by the observation that ABX discrimination is most informative when applied to high-performing\nsystems, given that lower-quality systems are trivially distinguishable from natural speech and contribute limited diagnostic value to a forced-choice paradigm (Schatz, Peddinti,\nBach, Jansen, Hermansky and Dupoux, 2013). By restricting the ABX stage to the top-ranked systems identified\nthrough the MUSHRA procedure, the test directly addresses\nthe more challenging and practically relevant question of\nwhether the best-performing Urdu TTS systems approach\nperceptual indistinguishability from natural speech.\nIn each ABX trial, listeners were presented with three\nstimuli: a reference utterance (A), a second presentation\nof the same reference (B), and an unknown sample (X)\ndrawn from either the reference or a synthetic output produced by Edge-TTS or Gemini TTS. Listeners indicated\nwhether X was perceptually more similar to A or to B. A\ndiscrimination rate significantly above 50% indicates that the\nsynthetic output remains perceptually distinguishable from\nnatural speech, whereas a rate at or near 50% indicates nearindistinguishability. The test was administered under conditions identical to those of the MUSHRA-style listening test,\nvia Google Forms, using the same standardised listening\ninstructions, and within the same online session. Participants\nwere native Urdu speakers aged 18–25 years, recruited from\nthe immediate social and academic networks of the research\nteam. The listener panel was gender-balanced, with an equal\nnumber of male and female participants, to mitigate potential\nsystematic gender-based perceptual bias in the discrimination of synthesised Urdu speech. As with the MUSHRAstyle evaluation, listeners were assigned to two domains\neach, yielding 10 listeners per domain pair, and both systems\nwere evaluated within each listener’s assigned domains to\npreserve the within-subject comparison structure.\n\n5.3. Objective Evaluation\nThree complementary objective metrics are employed to\nassess different aspects of synthesis quality. Speaker similarity, computed using Resemblyzer, evaluates voice identity\n\npreservation; mel-cepstral distortion (MCD) measures spectral envelope fidelity; and fundamental frequency root mean\nsquare error (F0 RMSE) quantifies pitch contour accuracy.\n\n5.3.1. Speaker Similarity via Resemblyzer\nSpeaker identity preservation was quantified using the\ncosine similarity between d-vector speaker embeddings extracted by Resemblyzer (Wan et al., 2018). Resemblyzer implements the Generalised End-to-End (GE2E) loss-trained\nLSTM encoder of Wan et al. (2018), which maps a variablelength speech segment to a fixed-dimensional embedding in\na speaker-discriminative space. For each reference-synthetic\npair, embeddings were extracted independently and their\ncosine similarity was computed; values approaching 1.0\nindicate higher speaker identity similarity between the reference and the synthesised output.\nOne methodological consideration specific to this evaluation concerns MMS-TTS, which produces a fixed singlespeaker output whose voice identity is independent of the\nreference speaker. All reference and synthetic audio in this\nstudy is restricted to male speakers, so every comparison\nis within-gender; nonetheless, the fixed MMS-TTS voice\ndiffers in speaker identity from each domain’s reference\nspeaker, and its cosine similarity therefore primarily reflects\nspeaker-identity mismatch rather than synthesis degradation, consistent with cautions raised for speaker-similarity\nevaluation of single-speaker systems (Wang, Chen, Wu,\nZhang, Zhou, Liu, Chen, Liu, Wang, Li et al., 2023a). All audio was resampled to 16 kHz prior to embedding extraction,\nas required by the Resemblyzer encoder input specification.\n5.3.2. Mel-Cepstral Distortion\nMel-cepstral distortion (MCD) (Kubichek, 1993) quantifies spectral envelope similarity between a reference and\na synthetic utterance by computing the Euclidean distance\nbetween their mel-cepstral coefficient sequences after dynamic time warping (DTW) alignment. Lower MCD values\nindicate greater spectral similarity. MCD is computed as:\n√\n√\n𝑇 √∑\n𝐾 (\n)2\n10 2 1 ∑ √\n√\nref − 𝑐 syn\n𝑐𝑘,𝑡\nMCD =\n𝑘,𝑡\nln 10 𝑇 𝑡=1 𝑘=1\n\n(1)\n\nsyn\n\nref and 𝑐\nwhere 𝑐𝑘,𝑡\nare the 𝑘-th mel-cepstral coefficients of\n𝑘,𝑡\nthe 𝑡-th DTW-aligned reference and synthetic frame pair,\n𝑇 is the number of aligned frames, and 𝐾 = 13 melcepstral coefficients are retained following standard practice\n(Kominek\nand Black, 2008); the leading scaling constant\n√\n10 2∕ ln 10 ≈ 6.142 expresses the distortion in decibels.\nMel-cepstra were obtained from a WORLD spectral envelope followed by SPTK mel-cepstral analysis (order 13,\nwarping coefficient 𝛼 = 0.65) using the pymcd implementation, with DTW alignment on the 𝐶1 –𝐶13 coefficients\nperformed by fastdtw under a Euclidean cost. Audio was\nresampled to 22.05 kHz for this analysis, ensuring comparability across systems with differing native output sample\nrates.\n\nJafar, Sarmad, Yousaf & Bashir: Preprint submitted to Computer Speech & Language\n\nPage 8 of 17\n\n\fDomain-Specific Evaluation of Urdu TTS Systems\nTable 2\nSummary of evaluation metrics, the quality dimension each captures, and result directionality.\n\nMetric\n\nType\n\nDimension\n\nBetter\n\nMUSHRA-style rating\nABX discrimination\nSpeaker similarity\nMCD\nF0 RMSE\n\nSubjective\nSubjective\nObjective\nObjective\nObjective\n\nNaturalness, clarity, overall quality\nPerceptual distinguishability from natural speech\nSpeaker identity preservation\nSpectral envelope fidelity\nPitch contour accuracy\n\nHigher\nLower\nHigher\nLower\nLower\n\n5.3.3. Fundamental Frequency RMSE\nPitch accuracy was assessed using the root-mean-square\nerror between the fundamental frequency (F0) contours of\nthe reference and synthetic utterances, expressed in cents on\na logarithmic scale (Kominek and Black, 2008):\n√\n√\n(\nsyn )2\n√ ∑\n𝐹 0𝑖\n√1 𝑁\n1200 log2\nF0 RMSE = √\n𝑁 𝑖=1\n𝐹 0ref\n\n(2)\n\n𝑖\n\nwhere 𝑁 is the number of voiced frames present in both\nthe reference and synthetic signals after DTW alignment.\nThe cent scale is employed because approximately 5 cents\napproximates one just-noticeable difference in pitch under\ntypical listening conditions (Kominek and Black, 2008).\nF0 was extracted with pyworld using the harvest algorithm\nfollowed by stonemask refinement (50–800 Hz search range)\nat a 16 kHz sampling rate, and the reference and synthetic\ncontours were compared on the frame pairs of a DTW\nalignment. Unvoiced frames and frames in which either the\nreference or synthetic F0 estimate was detected as zero were\nexcluded from the RMSE computation to avoid artefacts attributable to voicing detection errors. Because each synthetic\nutterance is compared against a natural reference produced\nby a different speaker, the reported F0 RMSE reflects both\npitch-contour deviation and a speaker-dependent register\noffset; the interpretation of such reference-distance metrics\nis discussed further in Section 8.3.\n\n5.4. Evaluation Pipeline Summary\nTable 2 summarises the four evaluation modules, the\ndimension of synthesis quality each addresses, and the directionality of each metric. The complete pipeline, including\npreprocessing scripts, metric computation notebooks, and\nraw result tables, is publicly released at https://github.\ncom/Shifa402/Urdu-Synthetic-Speech-Evaluation to support\nreproducibility and to serve as an extensible baseline for\nfuture Urdu TTS benchmarking.\n\n6. Experimental Setup\nThis section presents the implementation details of the\nsynthesis and evaluation pipeline. It describes the inference configuration for each TTS system and the hardware\nand software environment used to conduct all experiments,\n\nensuring reproducibility and consistent evaluation across\nsystems.\n\n6.1. Implementation Details\nAll synthesis and evaluation were implemented in Python\nwithin cloud-hosted notebooks. For each domain, the reference utterances were drawn from the source datasets\ndescribed in Section 4 and exported as single-channel WAV\nfiles. Each reference utterance was then synthesised once by\nevery one of the four TTS systems directly from its original\nUrdu Perso-Arabic transcript, without manual transliteration\nor romanisation. For every system the speaker or voice\nconfiguration was held fixed across all four domains, so that\nthe communicative domain remained the only manipulated\nfactor.\nMMS-TTS. The model was instantiated as a VitsModel with its associated AutoTokenizer from the facebook/mms-tts-urd-script_arabic checkpoint through the Hugging\nFace transformers library. Waveforms were produced in\na single non-autoregressive forward pass under torch.no_\ngrad() at the model’s native 16 kHz output rate. Synthesis is\ndeterministic and no decoding hyperparameters were tuned.\nIndic-Parler-TTS. The model was loaded as\nParlerTTSForConditionalGeneration\nfrom\nai4bharat /\nindic-parler-tts, with separate tokenisers for the transcript\nand for the natural-language speaker description. A single\nfixed description, “Rohit’s voice is clear and natural with a\nmoderate speed and pitch”, which selects the named male\nspeaker Rohit, was used unchanged across all domains to\nhold voice identity constant. Audio tokens were generated\nwith do_sample=True and temperature = 0.8 and decoded to\nwaveforms at the native 44.1 kHz.\nMicrosoft Edge TTS. Speech was synthesised through\nthe edge-tts client using the ur-PK-AsadNeural voice with\nthe service’s default prosody (rate, pitch, and volume left at\ntheir default values). Audio was returned at 24 kHz.\nGoogle Gemini TTS. Speech was generated through\nthe gemini-2.5-flash-preview-tts model with response_\nmodalities set to audio and a PrebuiltVoiceConfig specifying\nthe Charon preset voice, again held constant across domains.\nAudio was returned as 24 kHz PCM.\nAcross the four domains, 60 reference utterances per\ndomain were used for the objective metrics; for the Formal domain these comprised 30 FLEURS and 30 UrduSpeech Wikipedia-style utterances. This yields 4 systems ×\n\nJafar, Sarmad, Yousaf & Bashir: Preprint submitted to Computer Speech & Language\n\nPage 9 of 17\n\n\fDomain-Specific Evaluation of Urdu TTS Systems\nTable 3\nKey software components used for synthesis and objective\nevaluation.\nComponent\n\nRole\n\nVersion\n\ntransformers\nparler-tts\n\nMMS-TTS / tokenisers\nIndic-Parler-TTS\n\nedge-tts\ngoogle-generativeai\nresemblyzer\nlibrosa\npyworld\npymcd\npysptk\nfastdtw\n\nEdge TTS client\nGemini TTS client\nSpeaker embeddings\nAudio I/O, resampling\nF0 extraction (WORLD)\nMel-cepstral distortion\nMel-cepstral analysis\nDTW alignment\n\n4.40.0\nrelease at\naccess\n6.1.9\n0.8.3\n0.1.4\n0.11.0\n0.3.5\n0.2.1\n1.0.1\n0.3.4\n\nconcluding with a cross-metric synthesis. For each metric,\nresults are reported first at the domain level, aggregated\nacross all four systems, and then at the system level within\neach domain. This organisation directly addresses the core\nresearch question: whether synthesis quality varies systematically across communicative domains and whether domain\nsensitivity is consistent across evaluation dimensions. All\n960 reference–synthetic pairs were evaluated without computation failure, confirming complete corpus coverage.\n\n7.1. MUSHRA-Style Listening Test Scores\nMUSHRA-style results are analysed at both the domain\nand system levels. Domain-level results are first presented by\naggregating scores across all systems, followed by systemlevel analyses within each speech domain.\n\n7.1.1. Domain-Level MUSHRA Results\n4 domains × 60 = 960 reference–synthetic pairs for speaker\nsimilarity, MCD, and F0 RMSE. The subjective listening\ntests used a 30-utterance subset per domain to limit session\nlength and listener fatigue. Reference and synthetic clips\nwere paired by filename stem. Because the two cloud services (Edge, Gemini) and the sampling-based Indic-ParlerTTS decoder are not seed-controllable, a single synthesis\nwas generated per utterance; MMS-TTS and the two cloud\npresets are otherwise deterministic for a fixed input.\n\n6.2. Hardware and Software Configuration\nThe neural synthesis systems (MMS-TTS and IndicParler-TTS) and the speaker-embedding extraction were executed on a single CUDA-enabled GPU within a Google Colab environment running Python 3.12, while the two cloud\nsystems (Edge TTS and Gemini TTS) were accessed over\ntheir respective network APIs. The mel-cepstral distortion\nand fundamental-frequency computations are CPU-bound\nand were run locally.\nSpeaker similarity was computed with the GE2E-trained\nVoiceEncoder of Resemblyzer (Wan et al., 2018): reference\nand synthetic clips were passed through preprocess_wav\n(16 kHz resampling with voice-activity trimming) and compared by the cosine similarity of their utterance embeddings.\nThe MCD and F0 RMSE were computed by a single inhouse script. For MCD, mel-cepstral coefficients (order 13,\n𝛼 = 0.65) were extracted from a WORLD spectral envelope\nvia SPTK using the pymcd library at 22.05 kHz, DTWaligned with fastdtw under a Euclidean cost, and scored\nwith the standard Kominek–Black scaling constant. For\nF0 RMSE, audio was resampled to 16 kHz mono and F0\nwas extracted with pyworld (harvest+stonemask, 50–800 Hz),\nwith the reference and synthetic contours compared in cents\non DTW frame pairs. Key software components and versions\nare listed in Table 3.\n\n7. Results\nThis section presents evaluation results organised by\nmetric, proceeding from subjective perceptual assessment\nto objective acoustic and speaker identity measures, and\n\nFigure 1: Domain-level mean MUSHRA-style scores.\n\n7.1.2. System-Level MUSHRA Results\nTable 4: Per-system mean MUSHRA-style scores (1–5\nscale) by domain. Conv. = Conversational, Lit. = Literary/Storytelling, Form. = Formal, Emot. = Emotional. Bold\n= best system per domain (column).\nSystem\n\nConv.\n\nLit.\n\nForm.\n\nEmot.\n\nOverall\n\nEdge TTS\nGemini TTS\nIndic-Parler-TTS\nMMS-TTS\n\n3.093\n3.148\n2.148\n2.556\n\n3.361\n3.602\n2.214\n2.631\n\n3.373\n3.244\n2.738\n2.619\n\n1.963\n2.167\n1.296\n1.481\n\n2.947\n3.040\n2.099\n2.322\n\nNote: SDs (in parentheses, per domain-system cell) – Edge TTS: 0.442,\n1.071, 1.210, 0.666; Gemini TTS: 0.487, 1.097, 1.416, 0.739;\nIndic-Parler-TTS: 0.650, 1.103, 1.001, 0.496; MMS-TTS: 0.598, 1.232,\n1.090, 0.569 (order: Conv., Lit., Form., Emot.).\n\nGemini TTS remains the top-ranked system overall\n(3.040), narrowly ahead of Edge TTS (2.947), with IndicParler-TTS (2.099) and MMS-TTS (2.322) trailing substantially. Gemini TTS leads on three of four domains (Conversational, Literary/Storytelling, Emotional); Edge TTS leads\nonly on Formal (3.373 vs. 3.244). The Emotional domain\nagain produces the lowest scores for every system, with even\nthe best performer (Gemini, 2.167) falling well below the", "numLines": 400, "startLine": 700, "totalLines": 2047}}}
