{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-96c8f3fcf5897206", "type": "tool_result", "content": "1100\tscale midpoint.\n1101\t\n1102\tJafar, Sarmad, Yousaf & Bashir: Preprint submitted to Computer Speech & Language\n1103\t\n1104\tPage 10 of 17\n1105\t\n1106\t\fDomain-Specific Evaluation of Urdu TTS Systems\n1107\t\n1108\t7.2. ABX Discrimination Results\n1109\tListeners correctly identified the reference-matching\n1110\tsystem in 90.7% of trials overall (98/108), well above the\n1111\t50% chance level in both domains. Accuracy was identical\n1112\tto one decimal place across Literary/Storytelling and Formal\n1113\t(49/54 each), indicating MMS-TTS and Gemini-TTS remain\n1114\treliably distinguishable from the reference regardless of\n1115\tdomain.\n1116\t\n1117\tin Section 7.3.2. The Emotional domain ranks last on speaker\n1118\tsimilarity (0.5437), consistent with its ranking on both MCD and\n1119\tF0 RMSE, confirming that emotional speech presents the most\n1120\tchallenging conditions for synthesis quality preservation across all\n1121\tthree objective dimensions.\n1122\t\n1123\t7.3.2. System-Level Speaker Similarity\n1124\t\n1125\tSpeaker similarity results are analysed at the domain, system, and cross-domain levels. Domain-level analyses characterise\n1126\toverall voice identity preservation across speech domains, systemlevel analyses compare the performance of individual TTS systems,\n1127\tand cross-domain analyses assess the stability of system rankings\n1128\tacross different domains.\n1129\t\n1130\tTable 7 reports cosine similarity per system per domain. All\n1131\treference and synthetic audio is male-speaker. MMS-TTS produces\n1132\ta fixed single-speaker (male) output; because its voice identity\n1133\tis fixed and differs from each domain’s reference speaker, its\n1134\tsimilarity scores primarily reflect speaker-identity mismatch rather\n1135\tthan synthesis quality, and should be interpreted accordingly.\n1136\tTable 8 reports the cross-system emotion-level breakdown.\n1137\tBoredom yields the lowest cross-system mean similarity (0.5283),\n1138\ta counterintuitive finding given that boredom is a low-arousal\n1139\tstate with reduced prosodic variation. A plausible explanation is\n1140\tthat boredom in Urdu speech is characterised by atypical pitch\n1141\tdeclination patterns and reduced loudness that deviate from neutral\n1142\tread-speech baselines in ways distinct from high-arousal emotions,\n1143\tmaking speaker embedding extraction less reliable even when the\n1144\tacoustic signal is relatively flat. Edge TTS is the only system that\n1145\tachieves its highest emotion-level similarity on Happy (0.6106),\n1146\twhile all other systems rank Happy fourth or lower, suggesting\n1147\tthat Edge TTS’s neural vocoder better preserves speaker identity\n1148\tunder conditions of increased breathiness and higher pitch that\n1149\tcharacterise happy speech in Urdu.\n1150\t\n1151\t7.3.1. Domain-Level Speaker Similarity\n1152\t\n1153\t7.3.3. Cross-Domain Speaker Similarity Pattern\n1154\t\n1155\tTable 5: ABX discrimination accuracy (%), Edge-TTS vs.\n1156\tGemini-TTS. Chance level is 50%. 𝑛 = 54 trials per domain\n1157\t(9 listeners × 6 samples).\n1158\tDomain\n1159\t\n1160\tAccuracy (%)\n1161\t\n1162\t𝑛\n1163\t\n1164\tLiterary/Storytelling\n1165\tFormal\n1166\t\n1167\t90.7\n1168\t90.7\n1169\t\n1170\t54\n1171\t54\n1172\t\n1173\tOverall\n1174\t\n1175\t90.7\n1176\t\n1177\t108\n1178\t\n1179\t7.3. Speaker Similarity via Resemblyzer\n1180\t\n1181\tTable 6 reports mean cosine similarity between reference\n1182\tand synthetic speaker embeddings aggregated across all four systems per domain. A consistent domain-level gradient is observed,\n1183\twith Literary/ Storytelling achieving the highest mean similarity\n1184\t(0.6903) and the Emotional domain the lowest (0.5437), representing a difference of 0.1466 cosine similarity units across the domain\n1185\trange.\n1186\t\n1187\tTable 6: Domain-level mean Resemblyzer cosine similarity\n1188\taggregated across all four TTS systems (𝑛 = 240 pairs per\n1189\tdomain).\n1190\tDomain\n1191\t\n1192\tMean\n1193\t\n1194\tSD\n1195\t\n1196\tLiterary/Storytelling\n1197\tConversational\n1198\tFormal\n1199\tEmotional\n1200\t\n1201\t0.6903\n1202\t0.6592\n1203\t0.5758\n1204\t0.5437\n1205\t\n1206\t0.0793\n1207\t0.0739\n1208\t0.0765\n1209\t0.0667\n1210\t\n1211\tThe domain ranking on speaker similarity differs from the\n1212\tdomain rankings on MCD and F0 RMSE in one substantive respect:\n1213\tLiterary/Storytelling ranks first on speaker similarity (0.6903)\n1214\tbut second on MCD (7.87 dB) and jointly first on F0 RMSE\n1215\t(521.24 cents). Conversational ranks second on speaker similarity\n1216\t(0.6592) but first on MCD. This dissociation indicates that speaker\n1217\tidentity preservation and spectral envelope fidelity are partially\n1218\tindependent dimensions of synthesis quality and that a system\n1219\tmay preserve the perceptual characteristics of a speaker’s voice\n1220\twhile still diverging acoustically from the reference signal at the\n1221\tframe level. The Formal domain ranks third on speaker similarity\n1222\t(0.5758) despite containing what might be expected to be the\n1223\tmost predictable prosodic content, a pattern attributable to withindomain heterogeneity between its two source subsets, as elaborated\n1224\t\n1225\tIndic-Parler-TTS leads on Conversational and Literary but\n1226\tranks last on Formal. MMS-TTS leads on Formal and Emotional\n1227\tbut ranks last on Conversational and Literary. This cross-domain\n1228\trank instability on speaker similarity mirrors the rank reversal\n1229\tobserved on MCD in the Emotional domain (Section 7.4) and\n1230\tprovides convergent evidence that system selection based on singledomain evaluation would systematically misrepresent deployment\n1231\tperformance across communicative contexts. The practical implication is that no currently available Urdu TTS system provides\n1232\tuniformly high speaker identity preservation across the full range\n1233\tof speech domains evaluated in this study.\n1234\t\n1235\t7.4. Mel-Cepstral Distortion\n1236\tMCD results are analysed at both the domain and system levels.\n1237\tDomain-level analyses characterise overall spectral fidelity across\n1238\tspeech domains, while system-level analyses compare the spectral\n1239\treconstruction performance of individual TTS systems within each\n1240\tdomain.\n1241\t\n1242\t7.4.1. Domain-Level MCD Results\n1243\tAcross all 960 pairs the global mean MCD was 8.98 dB (𝜎 =\n1244\t2.77 dB). Table 9 reports domain-level aggregates.\n1245\t\n1246\tTable 9: Domain-level mean MCD (dB) aggregated across\n1247\tall four TTS systems (𝑛 = 240 pairs per domain). Lower\n1248\tvalues indicate better spectral envelope fidelity.\n1249\tDomain\n1250\tConversational\n1251\tLiterary/Storytelling\n1252\tFormal\n1253\tEmotional\n1254\t\n1255\tJafar, Sarmad, Yousaf & Bashir: Preprint submitted to Computer Speech & Language\n1256\t\n1257\tMean MCD (dB)\n1258\t\n1259\tSD\n1260\t\n1261\t6.90\n1262\t7.87\n1263\t9.12\n1264\t12.03\n1265\t\n1266\t1.76\n1267\t1.56\n1268\t1.79\n1269\t2.69\n1270\tPage 11 of 17\n1271\t\n1272\t\fDomain-Specific Evaluation of Urdu TTS Systems\n1273\tTable 7\n1274\tPer-system Resemblyzer cosine similarity across four speech domains (𝑛 = 60 pairs per system-domain cell). Higher values indicate\n1275\tbetter speaker identity preservation. Bold denotes the best-performing system within each domain. All reference and synthetic\n1276\taudio is male-speaker; MMS-TTS uses a fixed single-speaker voice, so its scores reflect speaker-identity mismatch rather than\n1277\tgender mismatch.\n1278\tDomain\n1279\t\n1280\tEdge TTS\n1281\t\n1282\tGemini TTS\n1283\t\n1284\tIndic-Parler-TTS\n1285\t\n1286\tMMS-TTS\n1287\t\n1288\tConversational\n1289\tLiterary/Storytelling\n1290\tFormal\n1291\tEmotional\n1292\t\n1293\t0.6916\n1294\t0.7393\n1295\t0.5398\n1296\t0.5610\n1297\t\n1298\t0.6303\n1299\t0.6676\n1300\t0.5647\n1301\t0.5196\n1302\t\n1303\t0.7293\n1304\t0.7542\n1305\t0.5172\n1306\t0.5241\n1307\t\n1308\t0.5857\n1309\t0.6000\n1310\t0.6814\n1311\t0.5700\n1312\t\n1313\tOverall Mean\n1314\t\n1315\t0.6329\n1316\t\n1317\t0.5956\n1318\t\n1319\t0.6312\n1320\t\n1321\t0.6093\n1322\t\n1323\tTable 8\n1324\tCross-system mean Resemblyzer cosine similarity by emotion within the Emotional domain (𝑛 = 10 pairs per system per emotion).\n1325\tHigher values indicate better speaker identity preservation. Emotions are listed in descending order of cross-system mean similarity.\n1326\tEmotion\n1327\t\n1328\tEdge\n1329\t\n1330\tGemini\n1331\t\n1332\tIndic\n1333\t\n1334\tMMS\n1335\t\n1336\tMean\n1337\t\n1338\tSad\n1339\tFear\n1340\tAnger\n1341\tDisgust\n1342\tHappy\n1343\tBoredom\n1344\t\n1345\t0.5828\n1346\t0.5756\n1347\t0.5662\n1348\t0.5383\n1349\t0.6106\n1350\t0.5169\n1351\t\n1352\t0.5412\n1353\t0.5343\n1354\t0.5059\n1355\t0.5339\n1356\t0.5103\n1357\t0.4926\n1358\t\n1359\t0.5375\n1360\t0.5204\n1361\t0.5618\n1362\t0.5155\n1363\t0.4949\n1364\t0.5105\n1365\t\n1366\t0.5855\n1367\t0.5759\n1368\t0.5580\n1369\t0.5701\n1370\t0.5243\n1371\t0.5931\n1372\t\n1373\t0.5618\n1374\t0.5516\n1375\t0.5480\n1376\t0.5395\n1377\t0.5350\n1378\t0.5283\n1379\t\n1380\tThe Conversational domain achieves the lowest mean MCD\n1381\t(6.90 dB), while the Emotional domain produces the highest\n1382\t(12.03 dB), representing a 74.3% increase in spectral distortion.\n1383\tThe Literary/Storytelling domain occupies an intermediate position (7.87 dB), ranking second despite containing narratively\n1384\tcomplex, stylistically marked speech, a finding elaborated in Section 8.2. The Formal domain produces higher mean MCD (9.12 dB)\n1385\tthan Literary/Storytelling, attributable in part to within-domain\n1386\theterogeneity arising from its two source subsets (FLEURS and UrduSpeech), as reflected in its elevated standard deviation (1.79 dB)\n1387\trelative to Literary/Storytelling (1.56 dB).\n1388\t\n1389\t7.4.2. System-Level MCD Results\n1390\tTable 10 reports MCD per system per domain.\n1391\tEdge TTS achieves the lowest MCD in four of five domain-subset\n1392\tconditions (Conversational, Literary/Storytelling, and both Formal\n1393\tsubsets), establishing it as the most spectrally accurate system\n1394\toverall, with a mean MCD of 7.91 dB. MMS-TTS produces the\n1395\thighest overall mean MCD (9.93 dB), a difference of 2.02 dB\n1396\trelative to Edge TTS, and the highest MCD in the Conversational, Literary/Storytelling, and Formal (Humairawan) conditions.\n1397\tWithin the Emotional domain, system rankings shift notably: IndicParler-TTS achieves the lowest MCD (10.62 dB) despite ranking\n1398\tsecond overall, while Gemini TTS, which performs competitively\n1399\tin other domains, produces the highest Emotional MCD (13.58 dB).\n1400\tThis rank reversal on the most challenging domain is a substantive\n1401\tfinding examined in Section 8.1.\n1402\t\n1403\t7.5. Fundamental Frequency RMSE\n1404\tF0 RMSE results are presented at the domain level before being\n1405\tdisaggregated by system to examine the pitch contour accuracy of\n1406\tindividual TTS models within each speech domain.\n1407\t\n1408\t7.5.1. Domain-Level F0 RMSE Results\n1409\tThe global mean F0 RMSE across all 960 pairs was 619.34 cents\n1410\t(𝜎 = 229.44 cents), with a coefficient of variation of 0.371, higher\n1411\tthan the MCD coefficient of variation of 0.309. This indicates\n1412\tthat pitch accuracy is more variable across the system-domain\n1413\tspace than spectral envelope fidelity. Table 11 reports domain-level\n1414\taggregates.\n1415\t\n1416\tTable 11: Domain-level mean F0 RMSE (cents) aggregated\n1417\tacross all four TTS systems (𝑛 = 240 pairs per domain).\n1418\tLower values indicate better pitch contour accuracy.\n1419\tDomain\n1420\tLiterary/Storytelling\n1421\tFormal\n1422\tConversational\n1423\tEmotional\n1424\t\n1425\tMean F0 RMSE (cents)\n1426\t\n1427\tSD\n1428\t\n1429\t521.24\n1430\t520.98\n1431\t546.02\n1432\t888.54\n1433\t\n1434\t89.63\n1435\t95.67\n1436\t114.25\n1437\t288.75\n1438\t\n1439\tThe F0 RMSE domain ranking differs from the MCD ranking\n1440\tin one notable respect: Literary/Storytelling and Formal are ranked\n1441\tfirst and second respectively on F0 RMSE, whereas Conversational\n1442\tranked first on MCD. This dissociation indicates that Conversational speech, while spectrally close to the reference, exhibits\n1443\tgreater pitch trajectory deviation than Literary or Formal speech.\n1444\tA plausible explanation is that conversational Urdu employs more\n1445\tvaried intonational patterns than read or narrated speech, producing\n1446\tlarger F0 deviations even when the spectral character of the voice\n1447\tis well preserved. The Emotional domain again stands apart, with\n1448\ta mean F0 RMSE of 888.54 cents representing a 70.4% increase\n1449\tover the next highest domain (Conversational at 546.02 cents).\n1450\tThe standard deviation of 288.75 cents on the Emotional domain\n1451\t\n1452\tJafar, Sarmad, Yousaf & Bashir: Preprint submitted to Computer Speech & Language\n1453\t\n1454\tPage 12 of 17\n1455\t\n1456\t\fDomain-Specific Evaluation of Urdu TTS Systems\n1457\tTable 10\n1458\tPer-system mean MCD (dB) across four speech domains (𝑛 = 60 pairs per system-domain cell). Lower values indicate better\n1459\tspectral envelope fidelity. Bold denotes the best-performing system within each domain.\n1460\tDomain\n1461\t\n1462\tEdge TTS\n1463\t\n1464\tGemini TTS\n1465\t\n1466\tIndic-Parler-TTS\n1467\t\n1468\tMMS-TTS\n1469\t\n1470\tConversational\n1471\tLiterary/Storytelling\n1472\tFormal (FLEURS)\n1473\tFormal (UrduSpeech)\n1474\tEmotional\n1475\t\n1476\t5.62\n1477\t6.32\n1478\t7.98\n1479\t7.47\n1480\t12.17\n1481\t\n1482\t7.30\n1483\t8.67\n1484\t11.15\n1485\t8.24\n1486\t13.58\n1487\t\n1488\t6.44\n1489\t7.09\n1490\t10.37\n1491\t7.51\n1492\t10.62\n1493\t\n1494\t8.25\n1495\t9.40\n1496\t10.37\n1497\t9.90\n1498\t11.75\n1499\t\n1500\tOverall Mean\n1501\t\n1502\t7.91\n1503\t\n1504\t9.79\n1505\t\n1506\t8.41\n1507\t\n1508\t9.93\n1509\t\n1510\tTable 12\n1511\tPer-system mean F0 RMSE (cents) across four speech domains (𝑛 = 60 pairs per system-domain cell). Lower values indicate\n1512\tbetter pitch contour accuracy. Bold denotes the best-performing system within each domain.\n1513\tDomain\n1514\t\n1515\tEdge TTS\n1516\t\n1517\tGemini TTS\n1518\t\n1519\tIndic-Parler-TTS\n1520\t\n1521\tMMS-TTS\n1522\t\n1523\tConversational\n1524\tLiterary/Storytelling\n1525\tFormal (FLEURS)\n1526\tFormal (UrduSpeech)\n1527\tEmotional\n1528\t\n1529\t500.95\n1530\t505.53\n1531\t570.77\n1532\t482.63\n1533\t765.14\n1534\t\n1535\t555.40\n1536\t537.21\n1537\t620.41\n1538\t490.04\n1539\t1027.44\n1540\t\n1541\t592.89\n1542\t550.12\n1543\t470.66\n1544\t541.34\n1545\t915.53\n1546\t\n1547\t535.77\n1548\t492.08\n1549\t470.66\n1550\t521.31\n1551\t846.05\n1552\t\n1553\tOverall Mean\n1554\t\n1555\t565.00\n1556\t\n1557\t646.10\n1558\t\n1559\t614.11\n1560\t\n1561\t573.17\n1562\t\n1563\tis more than three times that of Formal (95.67 cents), reflecting\n1564\tthe high inter-utterance variability inherent in emotionally marked\n1565\tspeech.\n1566\t\n1567\t7.6.1. Convergence at the Domain Level\n1568\t\n1569\tTable 12 reports F0 RMSE per system per domain.\n1570\tEdge TTS achieves the lowest F0 RMSE in four of five domainsubset conditions, with an overall mean of 565.00 cents. MMS-TTS\n1571\tranks second overall (573.17 cents), closely trailing Edge TTS despite ranking last on MCD, indicating a dissociation between spectral and prosodic accuracy for this system. Gemini TTS produces\n1572\tthe highest F0 RMSE in the Emotional domain (1027.44 cents),\n1573\texceeding the chance-level pitch deviation expected from a system\n1574\tgenerating unconditioned prosody, and ranking last overall on this\n1575\tmetric (646.10 cents). Within the Formal FLEURS subset, IndicParler-TTS and MMS-TTS produce identical F0 RMSE values\n1576\t(470.66 cents), suggesting that both systems converge on a similar\n1577\tpitch strategy for read-speech prompts from this source, likely\n1578\treflecting shared training on formal read-speech data.\n1579\t\n1580\tAll five paradigms agree on one point: the Emotional domain\n1581\tis the most difficult condition in this corpus. MUSHRA scores are\n1582\tlowest for every system on Emotional (Table 4), MCD is highest for\n1583\tevery system on this domain (12.03 dB at the domain level, against\n1584\ta corpus mean of 8.98 dB), F0 RMSE is more than 1.6 times higher\n1585\tthan the next-worst domain, and Resemblyzer similarity is at its\n1586\tglobal minimum (0.5437). This five-way convergence is notable\n1587\tprecisely because the paradigms differ so substantially in what they\n1588\toperationalize: MUSHRA reflects integrated human preference,\n1589\tMCD reflects frame-level spectral match, F0 RMSE reflects pitch\n1590\ttrajectory accuracy, and Resemblyzer reflects speaker-embedding\n1591\tdistance. Agreement across all four independent measurement constructs constitutes considerably stronger evidence for the Emotional\n1592\tdomain’s difficulty than any single metric could provide in isolation. This pattern implies that affective prosody is not merely underrated by listeners; it is genuinely harder to reproduce at the acoustic\n1593\tlevel, and the difficulty is multidimensional rather than confined to\n1594\tpitch, timbre, or spectral shape alone.\n1595\t\n1596\t7.6. Cross-Metric Synthesis\n1597\t\n1598\t7.6.2. Divergence at the System Level\n1599\t\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "3483c3dd-35ce-4c18-9530-2fe55290a039", "uuid": "8171513e-6b05-4c06-9a5e-603576896b6e", "timestamp": "2026-08-04T11:46:48.801Z", "tool_use_result": {"type": "text", "file": {"filePath": "/tmp/2608.02235.txt", "content": "scale midpoint.\n\nJafar, Sarmad, Yousaf & Bashir: Preprint submitted to Computer Speech & Language\n\nPage 10 of 17\n\n\fDomain-Specific Evaluation of Urdu TTS Systems\n\n7.2. ABX Discrimination Results\nListeners correctly identified the reference-matching\nsystem in 90.7% of trials overall (98/108), well above the\n50% chance level in both domains. Accuracy was identical\nto one decimal place across Literary/Storytelling and Formal\n(49/54 each), indicating MMS-TTS and Gemini-TTS remain\nreliably distinguishable from the reference regardless of\ndomain.\n\nin Section 7.3.2. The Emotional domain ranks last on speaker\nsimilarity (0.5437), consistent with its ranking on both MCD and\nF0 RMSE, confirming that emotional speech presents the most\nchallenging conditions for synthesis quality preservation across all\nthree objective dimensions.\n\n7.3.2. System-Level Speaker Similarity\n\nSpeaker similarity results are analysed at the domain, system, and cross-domain levels. Domain-level analyses characterise\noverall voice identity preservation across speech domains, systemlevel analyses compare the performance of individual TTS systems,\nand cross-domain analyses assess the stability of system rankings\nacross different domains.\n\nTable 7 reports cosine similarity per system per domain. All\nreference and synthetic audio is male-speaker. MMS-TTS produces\na fixed single-speaker (male) output; because its voice identity\nis fixed and differs from each domain’s reference speaker, its\nsimilarity scores primarily reflect speaker-identity mismatch rather\nthan synthesis quality, and should be interpreted accordingly.\nTable 8 reports the cross-system emotion-level breakdown.\nBoredom yields the lowest cross-system mean similarity (0.5283),\na counterintuitive finding given that boredom is a low-arousal\nstate with reduced prosodic variation. A plausible explanation is\nthat boredom in Urdu speech is characterised by atypical pitch\ndeclination patterns and reduced loudness that deviate from neutral\nread-speech baselines in ways distinct from high-arousal emotions,\nmaking speaker embedding extraction less reliable even when the\nacoustic signal is relatively flat. Edge TTS is the only system that\nachieves its highest emotion-level similarity on Happy (0.6106),\nwhile all other systems rank Happy fourth or lower, suggesting\nthat Edge TTS’s neural vocoder better preserves speaker identity\nunder conditions of increased breathiness and higher pitch that\ncharacterise happy speech in Urdu.\n\n7.3.1. Domain-Level Speaker Similarity\n\n7.3.3. Cross-Domain Speaker Similarity Pattern\n\nTable 5: ABX discrimination accuracy (%), Edge-TTS vs.\nGemini-TTS. Chance level is 50%. 𝑛 = 54 trials per domain\n(9 listeners × 6 samples).\nDomain\n\nAccuracy (%)\n\n𝑛\n\nLiterary/Storytelling\nFormal\n\n90.7\n90.7\n\n54\n54\n\nOverall\n\n90.7\n\n108\n\n7.3. Speaker Similarity via Resemblyzer\n\nTable 6 reports mean cosine similarity between reference\nand synthetic speaker embeddings aggregated across all four systems per domain. A consistent domain-level gradient is observed,\nwith Literary/ Storytelling achieving the highest mean similarity\n(0.6903) and the Emotional domain the lowest (0.5437), representing a difference of 0.1466 cosine similarity units across the domain\nrange.\n\nTable 6: Domain-level mean Resemblyzer cosine similarity\naggregated across all four TTS systems (𝑛 = 240 pairs per\ndomain).\nDomain\n\nMean\n\nSD\n\nLiterary/Storytelling\nConversational\nFormal\nEmotional\n\n0.6903\n0.6592\n0.5758\n0.5437\n\n0.0793\n0.0739\n0.0765\n0.0667\n\nThe domain ranking on speaker similarity differs from the\ndomain rankings on MCD and F0 RMSE in one substantive respect:\nLiterary/Storytelling ranks first on speaker similarity (0.6903)\nbut second on MCD (7.87 dB) and jointly first on F0 RMSE\n(521.24 cents). Conversational ranks second on speaker similarity\n(0.6592) but first on MCD. This dissociation indicates that speaker\nidentity preservation and spectral envelope fidelity are partially\nindependent dimensions of synthesis quality and that a system\nmay preserve the perceptual characteristics of a speaker’s voice\nwhile still diverging acoustically from the reference signal at the\nframe level. The Formal domain ranks third on speaker similarity\n(0.5758) despite containing what might be expected to be the\nmost predictable prosodic content, a pattern attributable to withindomain heterogeneity between its two source subsets, as elaborated\n\nIndic-Parler-TTS leads on Conversational and Literary but\nranks last on Formal. MMS-TTS leads on Formal and Emotional\nbut ranks last on Conversational and Literary. This cross-domain\nrank instability on speaker similarity mirrors the rank reversal\nobserved on MCD in the Emotional domain (Section 7.4) and\nprovides convergent evidence that system selection based on singledomain evaluation would systematically misrepresent deployment\nperformance across communicative contexts. The practical implication is that no currently available Urdu TTS system provides\nuniformly high speaker identity preservation across the full range\nof speech domains evaluated in this study.\n\n7.4. Mel-Cepstral Distortion\nMCD results are analysed at both the domain and system levels.\nDomain-level analyses characterise overall spectral fidelity across\nspeech domains, while system-level analyses compare the spectral\nreconstruction performance of individual TTS systems within each\ndomain.\n\n7.4.1. Domain-Level MCD Results\nAcross all 960 pairs the global mean MCD was 8.98 dB (𝜎 =\n2.77 dB). Table 9 reports domain-level aggregates.\n\nTable 9: Domain-level mean MCD (dB) aggregated across\nall four TTS systems (𝑛 = 240 pairs per domain). Lower\nvalues indicate better spectral envelope fidelity.\nDomain\nConversational\nLiterary/Storytelling\nFormal\nEmotional\n\nJafar, Sarmad, Yousaf & Bashir: Preprint submitted to Computer Speech & Language\n\nMean MCD (dB)\n\nSD\n\n6.90\n7.87\n9.12\n12.03\n\n1.76\n1.56\n1.79\n2.69\nPage 11 of 17\n\n\fDomain-Specific Evaluation of Urdu TTS Systems\nTable 7\nPer-system Resemblyzer cosine similarity across four speech domains (𝑛 = 60 pairs per system-domain cell). Higher values indicate\nbetter speaker identity preservation. Bold denotes the best-performing system within each domain. All reference and synthetic\naudio is male-speaker; MMS-TTS uses a fixed single-speaker voice, so its scores reflect speaker-identity mismatch rather than\ngender mismatch.\nDomain\n\nEdge TTS\n\nGemini TTS\n\nIndic-Parler-TTS\n\nMMS-TTS\n\nConversational\nLiterary/Storytelling\nFormal\nEmotional\n\n0.6916\n0.7393\n0.5398\n0.5610\n\n0.6303\n0.6676\n0.5647\n0.5196\n\n0.7293\n0.7542\n0.5172\n0.5241\n\n0.5857\n0.6000\n0.6814\n0.5700\n\nOverall Mean\n\n0.6329\n\n0.5956\n\n0.6312\n\n0.6093\n\nTable 8\nCross-system mean Resemblyzer cosine similarity by emotion within the Emotional domain (𝑛 = 10 pairs per system per emotion).\nHigher values indicate better speaker identity preservation. Emotions are listed in descending order of cross-system mean similarity.\nEmotion\n\nEdge\n\nGemini\n\nIndic\n\nMMS\n\nMean\n\nSad\nFear\nAnger\nDisgust\nHappy\nBoredom\n\n0.5828\n0.5756\n0.5662\n0.5383\n0.6106\n0.5169\n\n0.5412\n0.5343\n0.5059\n0.5339\n0.5103\n0.4926\n\n0.5375\n0.5204\n0.5618\n0.5155\n0.4949\n0.5105\n\n0.5855\n0.5759\n0.5580\n0.5701\n0.5243\n0.5931\n\n0.5618\n0.5516\n0.5480\n0.5395\n0.5350\n0.5283\n\nThe Conversational domain achieves the lowest mean MCD\n(6.90 dB), while the Emotional domain produces the highest\n(12.03 dB), representing a 74.3% increase in spectral distortion.\nThe Literary/Storytelling domain occupies an intermediate position (7.87 dB), ranking second despite containing narratively\ncomplex, stylistically marked speech, a finding elaborated in Section 8.2. The Formal domain produces higher mean MCD (9.12 dB)\nthan Literary/Storytelling, attributable in part to within-domain\nheterogeneity arising from its two source subsets (FLEURS and UrduSpeech), as reflected in its elevated standard deviation (1.79 dB)\nrelative to Literary/Storytelling (1.56 dB).\n\n7.4.2. System-Level MCD Results\nTable 10 reports MCD per system per domain.\nEdge TTS achieves the lowest MCD in four of five domain-subset\nconditions (Conversational, Literary/Storytelling, and both Formal\nsubsets), establishing it as the most spectrally accurate system\noverall, with a mean MCD of 7.91 dB. MMS-TTS produces the\nhighest overall mean MCD (9.93 dB), a difference of 2.02 dB\nrelative to Edge TTS, and the highest MCD in the Conversational, Literary/Storytelling, and Formal (Humairawan) conditions.\nWithin the Emotional domain, system rankings shift notably: IndicParler-TTS achieves the lowest MCD (10.62 dB) despite ranking\nsecond overall, while Gemini TTS, which performs competitively\nin other domains, produces the highest Emotional MCD (13.58 dB).\nThis rank reversal on the most challenging domain is a substantive\nfinding examined in Section 8.1.\n\n7.5. Fundamental Frequency RMSE\nF0 RMSE results are presented at the domain level before being\ndisaggregated by system to examine the pitch contour accuracy of\nindividual TTS models within each speech domain.\n\n7.5.1. Domain-Level F0 RMSE Results\nThe global mean F0 RMSE across all 960 pairs was 619.34 cents\n(𝜎 = 229.44 cents), with a coefficient of variation of 0.371, higher\nthan the MCD coefficient of variation of 0.309. This indicates\nthat pitch accuracy is more variable across the system-domain\nspace than spectral envelope fidelity. Table 11 reports domain-level\naggregates.\n\nTable 11: Domain-level mean F0 RMSE (cents) aggregated\nacross all four TTS systems (𝑛 = 240 pairs per domain).\nLower values indicate better pitch contour accuracy.\nDomain\nLiterary/Storytelling\nFormal\nConversational\nEmotional\n\nMean F0 RMSE (cents)\n\nSD\n\n521.24\n520.98\n546.02\n888.54\n\n89.63\n95.67\n114.25\n288.75\n\nThe F0 RMSE domain ranking differs from the MCD ranking\nin one notable respect: Literary/Storytelling and Formal are ranked\nfirst and second respectively on F0 RMSE, whereas Conversational\nranked first on MCD. This dissociation indicates that Conversational speech, while spectrally close to the reference, exhibits\ngreater pitch trajectory deviation than Literary or Formal speech.\nA plausible explanation is that conversational Urdu employs more\nvaried intonational patterns than read or narrated speech, producing\nlarger F0 deviations even when the spectral character of the voice\nis well preserved. The Emotional domain again stands apart, with\na mean F0 RMSE of 888.54 cents representing a 70.4% increase\nover the next highest domain (Conversational at 546.02 cents).\nThe standard deviation of 288.75 cents on the Emotional domain\n\nJafar, Sarmad, Yousaf & Bashir: Preprint submitted to Computer Speech & Language\n\nPage 12 of 17\n\n\fDomain-Specific Evaluation of Urdu TTS Systems\nTable 10\nPer-system mean MCD (dB) across four speech domains (𝑛 = 60 pairs per system-domain cell). Lower values indicate better\nspectral envelope fidelity. Bold denotes the best-performing system within each domain.\nDomain\n\nEdge TTS\n\nGemini TTS\n\nIndic-Parler-TTS\n\nMMS-TTS\n\nConversational\nLiterary/Storytelling\nFormal (FLEURS)\nFormal (UrduSpeech)\nEmotional\n\n5.62\n6.32\n7.98\n7.47\n12.17\n\n7.30\n8.67\n11.15\n8.24\n13.58\n\n6.44\n7.09\n10.37\n7.51\n10.62\n\n8.25\n9.40\n10.37\n9.90\n11.75\n\nOverall Mean\n\n7.91\n\n9.79\n\n8.41\n\n9.93\n\nTable 12\nPer-system mean F0 RMSE (cents) across four speech domains (𝑛 = 60 pairs per system-domain cell). Lower values indicate\nbetter pitch contour accuracy. Bold denotes the best-performing system within each domain.\nDomain\n\nEdge TTS\n\nGemini TTS\n\nIndic-Parler-TTS\n\nMMS-TTS\n\nConversational\nLiterary/Storytelling\nFormal (FLEURS)\nFormal (UrduSpeech)\nEmotional\n\n500.95\n505.53\n570.77\n482.63\n765.14\n\n555.40\n537.21\n620.41\n490.04\n1027.44\n\n592.89\n550.12\n470.66\n541.34\n915.53\n\n535.77\n492.08\n470.66\n521.31\n846.05\n\nOverall Mean\n\n565.00\n\n646.10\n\n614.11\n\n573.17\n\nis more than three times that of Formal (95.67 cents), reflecting\nthe high inter-utterance variability inherent in emotionally marked\nspeech.\n\n7.6.1. Convergence at the Domain Level\n\nTable 12 reports F0 RMSE per system per domain.\nEdge TTS achieves the lowest F0 RMSE in four of five domainsubset conditions, with an overall mean of 565.00 cents. MMS-TTS\nranks second overall (573.17 cents), closely trailing Edge TTS despite ranking last on MCD, indicating a dissociation between spectral and prosodic accuracy for this system. Gemini TTS produces\nthe highest F0 RMSE in the Emotional domain (1027.44 cents),\nexceeding the chance-level pitch deviation expected from a system\ngenerating unconditioned prosody, and ranking last overall on this\nmetric (646.10 cents). Within the Formal FLEURS subset, IndicParler-TTS and MMS-TTS produce identical F0 RMSE values\n(470.66 cents), suggesting that both systems converge on a similar\npitch strategy for read-speech prompts from this source, likely\nreflecting shared training on formal read-speech data.\n\nAll five paradigms agree on one point: the Emotional domain\nis the most difficult condition in this corpus. MUSHRA scores are\nlowest for every system on Emotional (Table 4), MCD is highest for\nevery system on this domain (12.03 dB at the domain level, against\na corpus mean of 8.98 dB), F0 RMSE is more than 1.6 times higher\nthan the next-worst domain, and Resemblyzer similarity is at its\nglobal minimum (0.5437). This five-way convergence is notable\nprecisely because the paradigms differ so substantially in what they\noperationalize: MUSHRA reflects integrated human preference,\nMCD reflects frame-level spectral match, F0 RMSE reflects pitch\ntrajectory accuracy, and Resemblyzer reflects speaker-embedding\ndistance. Agreement across all four independent measurement constructs constitutes considerably stronger evidence for the Emotional\ndomain’s difficulty than any single metric could provide in isolation. This pattern implies that affective prosody is not merely underrated by listeners; it is genuinely harder to reproduce at the acoustic\nlevel, and the difficulty is multidimensional rather than confined to\npitch, timbre, or spectral shape alone.\n\n7.6. Cross-Metric Synthesis\n\n7.6.2. Divergence at the System Level\n", "numLines": 500, "startLine": 1100, "totalLines": 2047}}}
{"type": "assistant", "message": {"id": "msg_1785844011897", "type": "message", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "tool_use", "id": "chatcmpl-tool-96d22e3770736171", "name": "Read", "input": {"file_path": "/tmp/2608.02235.txt", "limit": 500, "offset": 1600}}], "model": "GLM-5.2-w4a8", "stop_reason": null, "stop_sequence": null, "usage": {"input_tokens": 0, "output_tokens": 0, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 0, "cache_read_input_tokens": 0}, "context_management": null}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "3483c3dd-35ce-4c18-9530-2fe55290a039", "uuid": "4d7b7310-4a17-4cab-b81d-e599d2bb0916"}
{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-96d22e3770736171", "type": "tool_result", "content": "1600\t7.5.2. System-Level F0 RMSE Results\n1601\t\n1602\tThe preceding subsections reported each evaluation paradigm\n1603\tindependently. Considered jointly, however, the evaluation framework captures three complementary dimensions of TTS performance: a holistic perceptual quality (MUSHRA, ABX), a spectralacoustic fidelity (MCD), and a prosodic and speaker identity\n1604\tpreservation (F0 RMSE and Resemblyzer similarity, respectively).\n1605\tThe central question is not which system wins on aggregate, but\n1606\twhere these axes agree, where they diverge, and what the divergence reveals about what each method actually measures.\n1607\t\n1608\tThe system-level picture is considerably less consistent, and the\n1609\tdisagreements are informative. Gemini TTS achieves the highest\n1610\toverall MUSHRA score (3.040) yet the highest (worst) overall F0\n1611\tRMSE (646.10 cents) and the lowest overall Resemblyzer similarity (0.5956) among the four systems; it additionally produces\n1612\tthe highest Emotional-domain MCD (13.58 dB) and the highest\n1613\tEmotional-domain F0 RMSE (1027.44 cents) of any system in this\n1614\tstudy. In other words, the system that listeners preferred most was,\n1615\ton every objective acoustic measure examined, the system whose\n1616\toutput deviated most from the reference signal. This is not a small\n1617\tor marginal discrepancy; it is the single largest subjective–objective\n1618\tinversion in the dataset.\n1619\t\n1620\tJafar, Sarmad, Yousaf & Bashir: Preprint submitted to Computer Speech & Language\n1621\t\n1622\tPage 13 of 17\n1623\t\n1624\t\fDomain-Specific Evaluation of Urdu TTS Systems\n1625\tA plausible explanation, consistent with established findings\n1626\tin expressive speech synthesis, is that MUSHRA-style ratings\n1627\tcapture perceived naturalness and well-formedness, not reference\n1628\tfidelity. Gemini TTS likely generates fluent, confidently articulated\n1629\tprosodic contours that sound natural in isolation even when they\n1630\tdo not track the idiosyncratic pitch excursions or spectral irregularities of the specific reference recording – irregularities that, in\n1631\tthe Emotional domain particularly, may themselves be partially\n1632\tattributable to expressive variability rather than to a canonical\n1633\t“correct” target. Listeners evaluating a synthesized clip against a\n1634\treference and an anchor in a MUSHRA paradigm are not penalized\n1635\tfor prosodic divergence per se; they are rewarded for sounding convincing. MCD and F0 RMSE, by contrast, penalize any departure\n1636\tfrom the reference trajectory, regardless of whether that departure\n1637\tsounds more or less natural to a listener. The divergence between\n1638\tthe perceptual and signal-fidelity metrics therefore indicates that\n1639\treference-matching and perceived quality are partially orthogonal\n1640\tconstructs for emotionally expressive speech: a system can sound\n1641\tconvincingly emotional while diverging substantially, in cents and\n1642\tdecibels, from the specific acoustic realization a human speaker\n1643\thappened to produce.\n1644\tA complementary disagreement appears for Indic-Parler-TTS,\n1645\twhich ranks lowest or near-lowest on MUSHRA in three of four\n1646\tdomains (Table 4) yet achieves the highest Resemblyzer similarity overall (0.6312) and the lowest Emotional-domain MCD\n1647\t(10.62 dB). Here the inversion runs in the opposite direction: a\n1648\tsystem that is objectively close to the reference, both spectrally\n1649\tand in speaker identity, is nonetheless rated poorly by listeners.\n1650\tThis pattern is consistent with Indic-Parler-TTS’s descriptionconditioned architecture producing output that matches the coarse\n1651\tspectral envelope and speaker embedding of the reference while\n1652\texhibiting local artifacts, monotone delivery, or unnatural microprosody that frame-level and embedding-level metrics are not designed to detect. Embedding-based similarity and cepstral distance\n1653\toperate at a granularity – global spectral shape, fixed-dimensional\n1654\tspeaker representation – that does not capture the kind of momentto-moment naturalness defects that drive human dispreference.\n1655\tConversely, MUSHRA captures exactly these defects but provides\n1656\tno diagnostic insight into why a system sounds unnatural, since it\n1657\treturns a single integrated quality judgment.\n1658\tThe ABX results add a further dimension that neither MUSHRA\n1659\tnor the acoustic metrics provide. Even the systems involved in the\n1660\tABX comparison (Edge-TTS and Gemini TTS) remain discriminable from the reference in 90.7% of trials in both domains tested,\n1661\tdespite Gemini TTS’s relatively strong MUSHRA performance.\n1662\tThis confirms that perceived quality and perceived authenticity\n1663\tare separable constructs: a synthesis can be rated as good or even\n1664\texcellent on a quality scale while still being reliably identifiable as\n1665\tnon-human in a forced-choice discrimination task. This distinction\n1666\tis consequential for deployment contexts in which the goal is not\n1667\tmerely high quality but indistinguishability from human speech\n1668\t(e.g., voice cloning, dubbing); MUSHRA alone would systematically overstate progress toward that goal.\n1669\t\n1670\t7.6.3. Complementary Coverage Across Paradigms\n1671\tTaken together, no single paradigm in this study subsumes the\n1672\tothers. MUSHRA captures integrated human preference but conflates multiple underlying causes of (dis)satisfaction into one score\n1673\tand cannot localize whether a deficit originates in pitch, timbre,\n1674\tsegmental quality, or speaker identity. ABX isolates discriminability from naturalness, a distinction MUSHRA cannot make on its\n1675\town. MCD isolates segmental spectral accuracy but is insensitive\n1676\t\n1677\tto global naturalness, as the Gemini case demonstrates. F0 RMSE\n1678\tisolates prosodic contour accuracy and is the most volatile metric\n1679\tacross domains (coefficient of variation 0.371 versus 0.309 for\n1680\tMCD), consistent with pitch being the acoustic dimension most\n1681\tsensitive to expressive content and least constrained by trainingdata priors. Resemblyzer isolates speaker identity preservation\n1682\tindependent of content and prosody, but, as the Indic-Parler-TTS\n1683\tand MMS-TTS cases illustrate, can be confounded by architectural\n1684\tproperties (a fixed single-speaker output, in the case of MMS-TTS)\n1685\tunrelated to synthesis quality.\n1686\t\n1687\t7.6.4. Domain-Specific Cross-Metric Effects\n1688\tThe Formal domain illustrates a further complication that aggregate domain-level statistics conceal. Its two constituent subsets,\n1689\tFLEURS and UrduSpeech, produce divergent system orderings on\n1690\tboth MCD and F0 RMSE (Tables 10 and 12): Indic-Parler-TTS\n1691\tand MMS-TTS tie for the lowest F0 RMSE on FLEURS but IndicParler-TTS ranks worst on speaker similarity for the domain overall.\n1692\tThis within-domain heterogeneity, also visible in Edge TTS’s split\n1693\tbetween UrduSpeech and FLEURS subsets in Section 7.3 (a 0.2324\n1694\tsimilarity gap between subsets), indicates that \"Formal\" is not\n1695\tacoustically homogeneous in this corpus, and that conclusions\n1696\tdrawn from its domain-level aggregate risk masking subset-specific\n1697\teffects that are themselves as large as the cross-domain effects the\n1698\tstudy set out to measure.\n1699\t\n1700\t7.6.5. Integrated Interpretation\n1701\tCollectively, these findings suggest that no evaluation paradigm\n1702\temployed here, taken alone, would have produced a complete or\n1703\teven directionally reliable account of system quality. MUSHRA\n1704\talone would have crowned Gemini TTS the unambiguous winner;\n1705\tthe objective metrics alone would have crowned Edge TTS instead;\n1706\tResemblyzer alone would have favored Indic-Parler-TTS for nonFormal domains. The disagreement is not noise – it recurs consistently across the Emotional domain in particular, where every\n1707\tparadigm agrees the task is hard but no paradigm agrees on who\n1708\thandles the difficulty best. This indicates that \"best TTS system\" is\n1709\tnot a domain- or metric-invariant property in this evaluation, and\n1710\tthat any single-number leaderboard claim for Urdu TTS would be\n1711\tan artifact of which evaluation paradigm, and which domain, was\n1712\tchosen to report it.\n1713\t\n1714\t8. Discussion\n1715\tThis section interprets the evaluation results presented in Section 7 and discusses their broader implications for the evaluation\n1716\tof modern text-to-speech (TTS) systems. The findings demonstrate that synthesis quality varies substantially across systems\n1717\tand speech domains, while different evaluation metrics often capture complementary aspects of performance rather than yielding\n1718\tconsistent rankings. These observations highlight the importance\n1719\tof multi-metric evaluation and provide insights into the strengths\n1720\tand limitations of current TTS systems, with Urdu serving as a\n1721\trepresentative low-resource case study.\n1722\t\n1723\t8.1. System-Level Findings\n1724\tThe cross-metric synthesis indicates that system performance\n1725\tin this study is paradigm-dependent rather than uniform, and this\n1726\tdependency is itself the most informative system-level finding.\n1727\tEdge TTS is the only system that performs strongly across all\n1728\tobjective measures (lowest overall MCD and F0 RMSE, Tables 10\n1729\tand 12) while remaining competitive, though not dominant, on\n1730\t\n1731\tJafar, Sarmad, Yousaf & Bashir: Preprint submitted to Computer Speech & Language\n1732\t\n1733\tPage 14 of 17\n1734\t\n1735\t\fDomain-Specific Evaluation of Urdu TTS Systems\n1736\tMUSHRA. This profile is consistent with a synthesis strategy that\n1737\ttracks the reference acoustic envelope and pitch trajectory closely,\n1738\twhich objective metrics reward directly, while producing output\n1739\tthat listeners judge as good but not exceptional – plausibly because\n1740\tconservative, closely-tracked prosody reads as competent rather\n1741\tthan expressive.\n1742\tGemini TTS exhibits the inverse profile: weakest or nearweakest on every objective measure, yet strongest on MUSHRA\n1743\t(3.040 overall). This is the single largest subjective–objective inversion observed in the study. A plausible explanation is that Gemini TTS prioritizes fluent, confident delivery over strict reference\n1744\ttracking, a trade-off that pays off in human preference judgments\n1745\tbut does not register as a fidelity gain on signal-level metrics, which\n1746\tpenalize any departure from the reference trajectory regardless of\n1747\twhether that departure sounds more natural.\n1748\tIndic-Parler-TTS occupies an unusual middle position: objectively competitive on speaker identity (highest overall Resemblyzer\n1749\tsimilarity, 0.6312) and, on the Emotional domain specifically,\n1750\ton spectral fidelity (lowest MCD among all systems, 10.62 dB),\n1751\tyet consistently the lowest-rated or near-lowest-rated system on\n1752\tMUSHRA in three of four domains. This indicates that its\n1753\tdescription-conditioned architecture reproduces the coarse spectral\n1754\tenvelope and speaker embedding of the reference while exhibiting\n1755\tlocal artifacts or unnatural micro-prosody that frame-level and\n1756\tembedding-level metrics are not designed to detect. MMS-TTS,\n1757\tmeanwhile, is the most domain-conditional system: it ranks highest\n1758\ton speaker similarity in the Formal domain, consistent with training\n1759\tdata dominated by formal read-speech, but last on Conversational\n1760\tand Literary similarity and consistently worst on MCD across all\n1761\tdomains.\n1762\tConsidered together, these four profiles indicate that none of the\n1763\tsystems evaluated has converged on a strategy that simultaneously\n1764\tsatisfies signal-fidelity and listener-preference criteria. Each makes\n1765\ta different, identifiable trade-off, and that trade-off – not a single\n1766\tquality ranking – is the more accurate description of the current\n1767\tstate of these systems for Urdu speech synthesis.\n1768\t\n1769\t8.2. Domain-Level Variation\n1770\tAll five evaluation paradigms agree that the Emotional domain\n1771\tis the most difficult condition in this corpus: MUSHRA scores\n1772\tare lowest for every system, MCD and F0 RMSE are highest for\n1773\tevery system, and Resemblyzer similarity is at its global minimum\n1774\t(0.5437). This convergence across methodologically independent\n1775\tconstructs – holistic preference, spectral distance, pitch-contour\n1776\tdistance, and embedding distance – constitutes stronger evidence\n1777\tfor the domain’s difficulty than any single metric could provide,\n1778\tand indicates that affective prosody is not merely under-rated by\n1779\tlisteners but is genuinely harder to reproduce at the acoustic level,\n1780\tacross multiple independent acoustic dimensions simultaneously.\n1781\tThe reasons for this difficulty are plausibly compounding rather\n1782\tthan singular. Emotional reference speech itself exhibits substantially higher acoustic variability than the other three domains,\n1783\tevident in its larger standard deviations across every metric (e.g.,\n1784\tF0 RMSE SD of 288.75 cents versus 95.67 cents for Formal). A\n1785\tnoisier, more variable reference target is harder for any synthesis\n1786\tsystem to track, and is also harder for human listeners to use as a\n1787\tstable comparison point – a factor independently implicated in the\n1788\tcomparatively reduced ABX discrimination rate observed for Edge\n1789\tTTS on this domain. The domain’s difficulty therefore appears to\n1790\tarise jointly from the synthesis task and the evaluation task, rather\n1791\tthan from synthesis quality alone.\n1792\t\n1793\tThe remaining three domains show a more nuanced pattern\n1794\tthat aggregate domain-level statistics partially obscure. Conversational speech achieves the lowest MCD of any domain but does\n1795\tnot achieve the lowest F0 RMSE, indicating that conversational\n1796\tUrdu is spectrally well-matched but prosodically more variable\n1797\tthan read or narrated speech, plausibly reflecting the more varied\n1798\tintonational patterns characteristic of spontaneous dialogue. Literary/Storytelling, despite its narratively complex and stylistically\n1799\tmarked content, ranks favorably on MCD and F0 RMSE alike,\n1800\tand achieves the highest Resemblyzer similarity of any domain –\n1801\tconsistent with sustained, evenly-paced narrative speech providing\n1802\tmore stable acoustic context for both synthesis and embedding\n1803\textraction than shorter or more variable utterance types.\n1804\tThe Formal domain illustrates a further complication: its two\n1805\tsource subsets, FLEURS and UrduSpeech, produce divergent system orderings on both MCD and F0 RMSE, and Edge TTS shows\n1806\ta 0.2324 similarity gap between its UrduSpeech and FLEURS\n1807\tsubset performance. This within-domain heterogeneity indicates\n1808\tthat \"Formal\" is not acoustically homogeneous in this corpus,\n1809\tand that domain-level aggregates for Formal risk masking subsetspecific effects that are themselves as large as the cross-domain\n1810\teffects the study set out to measure. This pattern reinforces the\n1811\tbroader point that domain labels in this evaluation are useful\n1812\torganizing categories but should not be treated as guarantees of\n1813\tinternal acoustic homogeneity.\n1814\t\n1815\t8.3. Metric Agreement and Disagreement Analysis\n1816\tThe most consequential pattern in this study is the dissociation\n1817\tbetween what MUSHRA measures and what the objective acoustic\n1818\tmetrics measure. Gemini TTS diverges from the reference signal\n1819\tmore than any other system on MCD, F0 RMSE, and Resemblyzer\n1820\tsimilarity, yet is the most preferred system on MUSHRA. This\n1821\tdivergence indicates that reference-matching and perceived quality\n1822\tare partially orthogonal constructs for expressive Urdu speech: a\n1823\tsystem can sound convincingly natural while diverging substantially, in cents and decibels, from the specific acoustic realization\n1824\ta human reference speaker happened to produce. The implication\n1825\tgeneralizes beyond this dataset – a synthesis system optimized\n1826\tto minimize a reference-matching loss may be optimizing for a\n1827\ttarget that is only loosely coupled to the naturalness judgments that\n1828\tultimately determine perceived quality.\n1829\tA complementary disagreement runs in the opposite direction\n1830\tfor Indic-Parler-TTS, which is objectively close to the reference,\n1831\tboth spectrally and in speaker identity, yet rated poorly by listeners.\n1832\tTaken together with the Gemini TTS case, these two inversions\n1833\tshow that neither subjective nor objective evaluation alone is\n1834\tsufficient: MUSHRA captures naturalness defects that frame-level\n1835\tand embedding-level metrics cannot detect, since cepstral distance\n1836\tand fixed-dimensional speaker embeddings operate at a granularity\n1837\tthat does not register local artifacts or unnatural micro-prosody;\n1838\tconversely, the acoustic metrics capture systematic reference deviation that an integrated MUSHRA score cannot localize or explain.\n1839\tEach paradigm is sensitive to defects the other is structurally unable\n1840\tto detect.\n1841\tThe ABX results add a dimension that neither MUSHRA nor\n1842\tthe acoustic metrics provide. Even Gemini TTS, the strongest\n1843\tsystem on MUSHRA, remains discriminable from the reference\n1844\tin 87.5–88.9% of Emotional and Literary/Storytelling trials. This\n1845\tconfirms that perceived quality and perceived authenticity are\n1846\tseparable: a synthesis can be rated highly on a quality scale while\n1847\tremaining reliably identifiable as non-human in a forced-choice\n1848\t\n1849\tJafar, Sarmad, Yousaf & Bashir: Preprint submitted to Computer Speech & Language\n1850\t\n1851\tPage 15 of 17\n1852\t\n1853\t\fDomain-Specific Evaluation of Urdu TTS Systems\n1854\tdiscrimination task. A quality-only evaluation would systematically overstate progress toward indistinguishability from human\n1855\tspeech, a distinction that matters directly for applications such as\n1856\tvoice cloning or dubbing where authenticity, not merely quality, is\n1857\tthe deployment criterion.\n1858\t\n1859\t8.4. Key Findings\n1860\tThe key findings of this study are as follows:\n1861\t• TTS system performance is inherently multidimensional. No single system achieves consistently\n1862\tsuperior performance across perceptual quality, acoustic\n1863\tfidelity, and speaker similarity measures, demonstrating\n1864\tthat different evaluation criteria capture complementary\n1865\taspects of synthesis quality.\n1866\t• Subjective naturalness and objective reference similarity are not always aligned. The results show clear cases\n1867\twhere systems preferred by listeners differ substantially\n1868\tfrom those achieving the closest acoustic match to reference\n1869\tspeech, indicating that perceived quality extends beyond\n1870\tsignal-level similarity.\n1871\t• Emotional speech remains the most challenging synthesis domain. Across subjective and objective evaluations,\n1872\temotional speech consistently exhibits the lowest perceptual\n1873\tquality, highest acoustic distortion, largest pitch variation\n1874\terrors, and weakest speaker similarity, highlighting the difficulty of modeling expressive prosody.\n1875\t• Modern TTS systems exhibit distinct optimization tradeoffs. Different systems demonstrate different strengths, with\n1876\tsome favoring acoustic and speaker fidelity while others\n1877\tachieve higher listener preference through more natural and\n1878\tfluent speech generation.\n1879\t• Speech quality and speech authenticity represent different evaluation objectives. ABX discrimination results\n1880\tshow that highly rated synthetic speech can still be reliably\n1881\tdistinguished from natural speech, suggesting that current\n1882\tsystems have improved naturalness without fully achieving\n1883\thuman-level authenticity.\n1884\t• Comprehensive TTS evaluation requires multiple complementary metrics. Combining perceptual, discriminative, acoustic, and speaker-similarity analyses provides a\n1885\tmore complete understanding of synthesis performance than\n1886\tany individual evaluation measure.\n1887\t\n1888\t9. Conclusion\n1889\tThis study addressed the need for systematic and comprehensive evaluation of modern text-to-speech systems across diverse\n1890\tcommunicative domains. To this end, it introduced a domainstratified evaluation framework covering Formal, Conversational,\n1891\tLiterary/Storytelling, and Emotional speech, and evaluated four\n1892\tstate-of-the-art TTS systems using complementary subjective and\n1893\tobjective measures, including MUSHRA-style listening tests, ABX\n1894\tdiscrimination, speaker similarity analysis with Resemblyzer, and\n1895\tacoustic evaluations based on MCD and F0 RMSE. The framework\n1896\tprovides a structured approach for analyzing how synthesis quality\n1897\tvaries across different speech styles and communicative settings.\n1898\tThe results demonstrate that no single system consistently\n1899\tachieves the best performance across all evaluation dimensions and\n1900\tspeech domains. While Gemini TTS obtains the highest overall\n1901\t\n1902\tMUSHRA-style score (3.040), indicating stronger perceived naturalness and overall quality, Edge TTS achieves the strongest objective acoustic performance, with the lowest mean MCD (7.91 dB)\n1903\tand lowest mean F0 RMSE (565.00 cents). Across all evaluated systems, Emotional speech emerges as the most challenging synthesis condition, exhibiting the lowest perceptual scores,\n1904\thighest acoustic distortion (MCD of 12.03 dB and F0 RMSE of\n1905\t888.54 cents), and lowest speaker similarity (0.5437). The ABX\n1906\tresults further indicate that current TTS systems remain distinguishable from natural speech, with listeners correctly identifying\n1907\tthe reference-matching condition in 90.7% of trials, highlighting\n1908\tremaining challenges in achieving human-level expressive synthesis.\n1909\tThe findings reveal several broader insights into the evaluation\n1910\tof modern TTS systems. First, synthesis quality is inherently multidimensional, and system performance depends on the evaluation\n1911\tcriterion considered. Second, subjective naturalness and objective\n1912\treference similarity are not always aligned, demonstrating that perceptual quality cannot be fully characterized by signal-level fidelity\n1913\talone. Third, current TTS systems exhibit distinct optimization\n1914\ttrade-offs, with different models prioritizing naturalness, acoustic\n1915\tsimilarity, or speaker identity. Finally, the results show that perceived quality and authenticity are separate evaluation objectives,\n1916\tas highly rated synthetic speech can still be distinguishable from\n1917\tnatural speech.\n1918\tOverall, this study contributes: (i) a domain-stratified benchmark for evaluating TTS systems across diverse speech conditions;\n1919\t(ii) a multi-metric evaluation framework that integrates perceptual,\n1920\tacoustic, prosodic, and speaker-similarity analyses; and (iii) a publicly released evaluation pipeline, result tables, and reproducible\n1921\tresources that can support future research on speech synthesis\n1922\tevaluation. By demonstrating the importance of domain-aware and\n1923\tmulti-dimensional assessment, this work provides a foundation for\n1924\tmore comprehensive and context-sensitive evaluation of TTS systems, particularly in low-resource and underrepresented language\n1925\tsettings.\n1926\t\n1927\t10. Future Work\n1928\tFuture work can extend this benchmark in several directions.\n1929\tFirst, larger and more diverse listener studies using standardized\n1930\tonline MUSHRA platforms would further improve the reliability\n1931\tand comparability of subjective evaluations. Second, the framework can be expanded to include additional speech scenarios\n1932\tand emerging TTS systems to investigate how synthesis quality\n1933\tvaries across different communicative contexts and technological\n1934\tapproaches. Finally, applying the proposed evaluation framework\n1935\tto other low-resource and underrepresented languages would help\n1936\testablish more inclusive and standardized practices for speech\n1937\tsynthesis evaluation beyond high-resource settings.\n1938\t\n1939\tData Set\n1940\tThe annotated evaluation data, including subjective listening\n1941\ttest responses and benchmark results, is publicly available at:\n1942\thttps://github.com/Shifa402/Urdu-Synthetic-Speech-Evaluation\n1943\t\n1944\tThe annotated dataset used in this study can be accessed with\n1945\tprior permission via this link: https://drive.google.com/file/d/\n1946\t1MfeXJ-LWh1SXptU5gUbXckh27YLMURNW/view?usp=drive_link\n1947\t\n1948\tJafar, Sarmad, Yousaf & Bashir: Preprint submitted to Computer Speech & Language\n1949\t\n1950\tPage 16 of 17\n1951\t\n1952\t\fDomain-Specific Evaluation of Urdu TTS Systems\n1953\t\n1954\tDeclaration of generative AI technologies in\n1955\tthe writing process\n1956\tDuring the preparation of this work the authors used Claude in\n1957\torder to improve the readability and language of the manuscript.\n1958\tAfter using this tool/service, the authors reviewed and edited the\n1959\tcontent as needed and take full responsibility for the content of the\n1960\tpublished article.\n1961\t\n1962\tCRediT authorship contribution statement\n1963\tAli Jafar: Conceptualization, Methodology, Software, Formal\n1964\tAnalysis, Writing – Sections 1, 2, 6 (Introduction, Related Work,\n1965\tExperimental Setup). Amal Sarmad: Subjective Evaluation Setup,\n1966\tWriting – Sections 5, 7, 8 (Evaluation Methodology, Results,\n1967\tDiscussion). Shifa Yousaf: Literature Review, Dataset Curation,\n1968\tDataset Documentation, Writing – Sections 3–4, 9–10 (Methodology for TTS System Selection, Domain-Wise Dataset Setup,\n1969\tConclusion, Future Work). Maryam Bashir: Conceptualization,\n1970\tMethodology, Supervision, Review & Editing.\n1971\t\n1972\tReferences\n1973\tAI4Bharat, 2024. Indic parler-TTS: A multilingual text-to-speech model for\n1974\tindian languages. https://huggingface.co/ai4bharat/indic-parler-tts.\n1975\tModel card and checkpoint, accessed December 2024.\n1976\tAnonymous, 2025. PashtoTTS-bench: Automated screening for lowresource non-Latin-script text-to-speech. arXiv:2605.26978.\n1977\tArshad, H., et al., 2022. Exploring transfer learning for Urdu speech\n1978\tsynthesis, in: Proc. EurALI.\n1979\tBailly, G., et al., 2025. Hot topics in speech synthesis evaluation. arXiv\n1980\tpreprint arXiv:2504.02738 Outcome of Dagstuhl Seminar 25032.\n1981\tChiang, C., et al., 2024. Rethinking MUSHRA: Addressing modern\n1982\tchallenges in text-to-speech evaluation.\n1983\tChiang, C.Y., Lee, H.y., 2024. A study on the applicability of mushra to\n1984\tevaluate neural text-to-speech systems, in: Proceedings of Interspeech,\n1985\tpp. 2195–2199. doi:10.21437/Interspeech.2024-1354.\n1986\tChung, H.W., et al., 2022. Scaling instruction-finetuned language models.\n1987\tarXiv preprint arXiv:2210.11416 .\n1988\tConneau, A., Ma, M., Khanuja, S., Zhang, Y., Axelrod, V., Dalmia, S.,\n1989\tRiesa, J., Rivera, C., Bapna, A., 2022. FLEURS: Few-shot learning\n1990\tevaluation of universal representations of speech, in: 2022 IEEE Spoken\n1991\tLanguage Technology Workshop (SLT), pp. 798–805.\n1992\tGoogle DeepMind, 2025. Gemini 2.5: Our Most Intelligent AI Model.\n1993\tTechnical Report. Google DeepMind. Accessed April 2025.\n1994\tInternational Telecommunication Union, 2015. Method for the subjective\n1995\tassessment of intermediate quality level of audio systems. Recommendation BS.1534-3. ITU-R.\n1996\tJain, P., et al., 2022. Towards building text-to-speech systems for the next\n1997\tbillion users. arXiv preprint arXiv:2211.09536 .\n1998\tKim, J., Kong, J., Son, J., 2021. Conditional variational autoencoder with\n1999\tadversarial learning for end-to-end text-to-speech, in: Proceedings of\n2000\tInternational Conference on Machine Learning (ICML), pp. 5530–5540.\n2001\tKirkland, A., Mehta, S., Lameris, H., Henter, G.E., Szekely, E., 2023. Stuck\n2002\tin the MOS pit: A critical analysis of MOS test methodology in TTS\n2003\tevaluation, in: Proc. SSW.\n2004\tKominek, J., Black, A.W., 2008. Synthesizer voice quality of new languages\n2005\tcalibrated with mean MCD, in: Proc. SLTU.\n2006\tKong, J., Kim, J., Bae, J., 2020. Hifi-gan: Generative adversarial networks\n2007\tfor efficient and high fidelity speech synthesis, in: Advances in Neural\n2008\tInformation Processing Systems (NeurIPS).\n2009\tKubichek, R., 1993. Mel-cepstral distance measure for objective speech\n2010\tquality assessment , 125–128.\n2011\tLacombe, Y., Srivastav, V., Gandhi, S., 2024. Parler-TTS. https://github.\n2012\tcom/huggingface/parler-tts.\n2013\tLe Maguer, S., et al., 2024. The limits of the Mean Opinion Score for speech\n2014\tsynthesis evaluation. Computer Speech & Language 84.\n2015\t\n2016\tLyth, D., King, S., 2024. Natural language guidance of high-fidelity textto-speech with synthetic annotations. arXiv:2402.01912.\n2017\tMicrosoft Corporation, 2023. Azure Cognitive Services: Neural Text-toSpeech Overview. Technical Report. Microsoft Corporation. Accessed\n2018\tDecember 2024.\n2019\tNaseem, S., et al., 2025. Evaluating and developing TTS models for Urdu\n2020\tand Punjabi, in: Proc. Interspeech.\n2021\tPratap, V., Tjandra, A., Shi, B., Tomasello, P., Babu, A., Kundu, S.,\n2022\tElkahky, A., Ni, Z., Vyas, A., Fazel-Zarandi, M., Baevski, A., Adi, Y.,\n2023\tZhang, X., Hsu, W.N., Conneau, A., Auli, M., 2023. Scaling speech\n2024\ttechnology to 1,000+ languages.\n2025\tSchatz, T., Peddinti, V., Bach, F., Jansen, A., Hermansky, H., Dupoux,\n2026\tE., 2013. Evaluating speech features with the ABX discriminability\n2027\tmeasure: Analysis of the classical mfc/plp pipeline, in: Proceedings of\n2028\tInterspeech, pp. 1781–1785. doi:10.21437/Interspeech.2013-564.\n2029\tTan, X., Qin, T., Soong, F., Liu, T.Y., 2021. A survey on neural speech\n2030\tsynthesis. arXiv preprint arXiv:2106.15561 .\n2031\tWan, L., Wang, Q., Papir, A., Moreno, I.L., 2018. Generalized end-to-end\n2032\tloss for speaker verification, in: Proc. ICASSP, pp. 4879–4883.\n2033\tWang, C., Chen, S., Wu, Y., Zhang, Z., Zhou, L., Liu, S., Chen, Z., Liu,\n2034\tY., Wang, H., Li, J., et al., 2023a. Neural codec language models are\n2035\tzero-shot text to speech synthesizers, in: Proceedings of the International\n2036\tConference on Machine Learning (ICML), pp. 36062–36075.\n2037\tWang, H., et al., 2023b. Generalised zero-shot text-to-speech via voice\n2038\tembedding, in: Proc. Interspeech.\n2039\tWang, Y., et al., 2017. Tacotron: Towards end-to-end speech synthesis, in:\n2040\tProceedings of Interspeech, pp. 4006–4010. doi:10.21437/Interspeech.\n2041\t2017-1452.\n2042\t\n2043\tJafar, Sarmad, Yousaf & Bashir: Preprint submitted to Computer Speech & Language\n2044\t\n2045\tPage 17 of 17\n2046\t\n2047\t\f\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "3483c3dd-35ce-4c18-9530-2fe55290a039", "uuid": "716d102e-30cf-4f16-8d16-2a088f0ee88d", "timestamp": "2026-08-04T11:52:54.945Z", "tool_use_result": {"type": "text", "file": {"filePath": "/tmp/2608.02235.txt", "content": "7.5.2. System-Level F0 RMSE Results\n\nThe preceding subsections reported each evaluation paradigm\nindependently. Considered jointly, however, the evaluation framework captures three complementary dimensions of TTS performance: a holistic perceptual quality (MUSHRA, ABX), a spectralacoustic fidelity (MCD), and a prosodic and speaker identity\npreservation (F0 RMSE and Resemblyzer similarity, respectively).\nThe central question is not which system wins on aggregate, but\nwhere these axes agree, where they diverge, and what the divergence reveals about what each method actually measures.\n\nThe system-level picture is considerably less consistent, and the\ndisagreements are informative. Gemini TTS achieves the highest\noverall MUSHRA score (3.040) yet the highest (worst) overall F0\nRMSE (646.10 cents) and the lowest overall Resemblyzer similarity (0.5956) among the four systems; it additionally produces\nthe highest Emotional-domain MCD (13.58 dB) and the highest\nEmotional-domain F0 RMSE (1027.44 cents) of any system in this\nstudy. In other words, the system that listeners preferred most was,\non every objective acoustic measure examined, the system whose\noutput deviated most from the reference signal. This is not a small\nor marginal discrepancy; it is the single largest subjective–objective\ninversion in the dataset.\n\nJafar, Sarmad, Yousaf & Bashir: Preprint submitted to Computer Speech & Language\n\nPage 13 of 17\n\n\fDomain-Specific Evaluation of Urdu TTS Systems\nA plausible explanation, consistent with established findings\nin expressive speech synthesis, is that MUSHRA-style ratings\ncapture perceived naturalness and well-formedness, not reference\nfidelity. Gemini TTS likely generates fluent, confidently articulated\nprosodic contours that sound natural in isolation even when they\ndo not track the idiosyncratic pitch excursions or spectral irregularities of the specific reference recording – irregularities that, in\nthe Emotional domain particularly, may themselves be partially\nattributable to expressive variability rather than to a canonical\n“correct” target. Listeners evaluating a synthesized clip against a\nreference and an anchor in a MUSHRA paradigm are not penalized\nfor prosodic divergence per se; they are rewarded for sounding convincing. MCD and F0 RMSE, by contrast, penalize any departure\nfrom the reference trajectory, regardless of whether that departure\nsounds more or less natural to a listener. The divergence between\nthe perceptual and signal-fidelity metrics therefore indicates that\nreference-matching and perceived quality are partially orthogonal\nconstructs for emotionally expressive speech: a system can sound\nconvincingly emotional while diverging substantially, in cents and\ndecibels, from the specific acoustic realization a human speaker\nhappened to produce.\nA complementary disagreement appears for Indic-Parler-TTS,\nwhich ranks lowest or near-lowest on MUSHRA in three of four\ndomains (Table 4) yet achieves the highest Resemblyzer similarity overall (0.6312) and the lowest Emotional-domain MCD\n(10.62 dB). Here the inversion runs in the opposite direction: a\nsystem that is objectively close to the reference, both spectrally\nand in speaker identity, is nonetheless rated poorly by listeners.\nThis pattern is consistent with Indic-Parler-TTS’s descriptionconditioned architecture producing output that matches the coarse\nspectral envelope and speaker embedding of the reference while\nexhibiting local artifacts, monotone delivery, or unnatural microprosody that frame-level and embedding-level metrics are not designed to detect. Embedding-based similarity and cepstral distance\noperate at a granularity – global spectral shape, fixed-dimensional\nspeaker representation – that does not capture the kind of momentto-moment naturalness defects that drive human dispreference.\nConversely, MUSHRA captures exactly these defects but provides\nno diagnostic insight into why a system sounds unnatural, since it\nreturns a single integrated quality judgment.\nThe ABX results add a further dimension that neither MUSHRA\nnor the acoustic metrics provide. Even the systems involved in the\nABX comparison (Edge-TTS and Gemini TTS) remain discriminable from the reference in 90.7% of trials in both domains tested,\ndespite Gemini TTS’s relatively strong MUSHRA performance.\nThis confirms that perceived quality and perceived authenticity\nare separable constructs: a synthesis can be rated as good or even\nexcellent on a quality scale while still being reliably identifiable as\nnon-human in a forced-choice discrimination task. This distinction\nis consequential for deployment contexts in which the goal is not\nmerely high quality but indistinguishability from human speech\n(e.g., voice cloning, dubbing); MUSHRA alone would systematically overstate progress toward that goal.\n\n7.6.3. Complementary Coverage Across Paradigms\nTaken together, no single paradigm in this study subsumes the\nothers. MUSHRA captures integrated human preference but conflates multiple underlying causes of (dis)satisfaction into one score\nand cannot localize whether a deficit originates in pitch, timbre,\nsegmental quality, or speaker identity. ABX isolates discriminability from naturalness, a distinction MUSHRA cannot make on its\nown. MCD isolates segmental spectral accuracy but is insensitive\n\nto global naturalness, as the Gemini case demonstrates. F0 RMSE\nisolates prosodic contour accuracy and is the most volatile metric\nacross domains (coefficient of variation 0.371 versus 0.309 for\nMCD), consistent with pitch being the acoustic dimension most\nsensitive to expressive content and least constrained by trainingdata priors. Resemblyzer isolates speaker identity preservation\nindependent of content and prosody, but, as the Indic-Parler-TTS\nand MMS-TTS cases illustrate, can be confounded by architectural\nproperties (a fixed single-speaker output, in the case of MMS-TTS)\nunrelated to synthesis quality.\n\n7.6.4. Domain-Specific Cross-Metric Effects\nThe Formal domain illustrates a further complication that aggregate domain-level statistics conceal. Its two constituent subsets,\nFLEURS and UrduSpeech, produce divergent system orderings on\nboth MCD and F0 RMSE (Tables 10 and 12): Indic-Parler-TTS\nand MMS-TTS tie for the lowest F0 RMSE on FLEURS but IndicParler-TTS ranks worst on speaker similarity for the domain overall.\nThis within-domain heterogeneity, also visible in Edge TTS’s split\nbetween UrduSpeech and FLEURS subsets in Section 7.3 (a 0.2324\nsimilarity gap between subsets), indicates that \"Formal\" is not\nacoustically homogeneous in this corpus, and that conclusions\ndrawn from its domain-level aggregate risk masking subset-specific\neffects that are themselves as large as the cross-domain effects the\nstudy set out to measure.\n\n7.6.5. Integrated Interpretation\nCollectively, these findings suggest that no evaluation paradigm\nemployed here, taken alone, would have produced a complete or\neven directionally reliable account of system quality. MUSHRA\nalone would have crowned Gemini TTS the unambiguous winner;\nthe objective metrics alone would have crowned Edge TTS instead;\nResemblyzer alone would have favored Indic-Parler-TTS for nonFormal domains. The disagreement is not noise – it recurs consistently across the Emotional domain in particular, where every\nparadigm agrees the task is hard but no paradigm agrees on who\nhandles the difficulty best. This indicates that \"best TTS system\" is\nnot a domain- or metric-invariant property in this evaluation, and\nthat any single-number leaderboard claim for Urdu TTS would be\nan artifact of which evaluation paradigm, and which domain, was\nchosen to report it.\n\n8. Discussion\nThis section interprets the evaluation results presented in Section 7 and discusses their broader implications for the evaluation\nof modern text-to-speech (TTS) systems. The findings demonstrate that synthesis quality varies substantially across systems\nand speech domains, while different evaluation metrics often capture complementary aspects of performance rather than yielding\nconsistent rankings. These observations highlight the importance\nof multi-metric evaluation and provide insights into the strengths\nand limitations of current TTS systems, with Urdu serving as a\nrepresentative low-resource case study.\n\n8.1. System-Level Findings\nThe cross-metric synthesis indicates that system performance\nin this study is paradigm-dependent rather than uniform, and this\ndependency is itself the most informative system-level finding.\nEdge TTS is the only system that performs strongly across all\nobjective measures (lowest overall MCD and F0 RMSE, Tables 10\nand 12) while remaining competitive, though not dominant, on\n\nJafar, Sarmad, Yousaf & Bashir: Preprint submitted to Computer Speech & Language\n\nPage 14 of 17\n\n\fDomain-Specific Evaluation of Urdu TTS Systems\nMUSHRA. This profile is consistent with a synthesis strategy that\ntracks the reference acoustic envelope and pitch trajectory closely,\nwhich objective metrics reward directly, while producing output\nthat listeners judge as good but not exceptional – plausibly because\nconservative, closely-tracked prosody reads as competent rather\nthan expressive.\nGemini TTS exhibits the inverse profile: weakest or nearweakest on every objective measure, yet strongest on MUSHRA\n(3.040 overall). This is the single largest subjective–objective inversion observed in the study. A plausible explanation is that Gemini TTS prioritizes fluent, confident delivery over strict reference\ntracking, a trade-off that pays off in human preference judgments\nbut does not register as a fidelity gain on signal-level metrics, which\npenalize any departure from the reference trajectory regardless of\nwhether that departure sounds more natural.\nIndic-Parler-TTS occupies an unusual middle position: objectively competitive on speaker identity (highest overall Resemblyzer\nsimilarity, 0.6312) and, on the Emotional domain specifically,\non spectral fidelity (lowest MCD among all systems, 10.62 dB),\nyet consistently the lowest-rated or near-lowest-rated system on\nMUSHRA in three of four domains. This indicates that its\ndescription-conditioned architecture reproduces the coarse spectral\nenvelope and speaker embedding of the reference while exhibiting\nlocal artifacts or unnatural micro-prosody that frame-level and\nembedding-level metrics are not designed to detect. MMS-TTS,\nmeanwhile, is the most domain-conditional system: it ranks highest\non speaker similarity in the Formal domain, consistent with training\ndata dominated by formal read-speech, but last on Conversational\nand Literary similarity and consistently worst on MCD across all\ndomains.\nConsidered together, these four profiles indicate that none of the\nsystems evaluated has converged on a strategy that simultaneously\nsatisfies signal-fidelity and listener-preference criteria. Each makes\na different, identifiable trade-off, and that trade-off – not a single\nquality ranking – is the more accurate description of the current\nstate of these systems for Urdu speech synthesis.\n\n8.2. Domain-Level Variation\nAll five evaluation paradigms agree that the Emotional domain\nis the most difficult condition in this corpus: MUSHRA scores\nare lowest for every system, MCD and F0 RMSE are highest for\nevery system, and Resemblyzer similarity is at its global minimum\n(0.5437). This convergence across methodologically independent\nconstructs – holistic preference, spectral distance, pitch-contour\ndistance, and embedding distance – constitutes stronger evidence\nfor the domain’s difficulty than any single metric could provide,\nand indicates that affective prosody is not merely under-rated by\nlisteners but is genuinely harder to reproduce at the acoustic level,\nacross multiple independent acoustic dimensions simultaneously.\nThe reasons for this difficulty are plausibly compounding rather\nthan singular. Emotional reference speech itself exhibits substantially higher acoustic variability than the other three domains,\nevident in its larger standard deviations across every metric (e.g.,\nF0 RMSE SD of 288.75 cents versus 95.67 cents for Formal). A\nnoisier, more variable reference target is harder for any synthesis\nsystem to track, and is also harder for human listeners to use as a\nstable comparison point – a factor independently implicated in the\ncomparatively reduced ABX discrimination rate observed for Edge\nTTS on this domain. The domain’s difficulty therefore appears to\narise jointly from the synthesis task and the evaluation task, rather\nthan from synthesis quality alone.\n\nThe remaining three domains show a more nuanced pattern\nthat aggregate domain-level statistics partially obscure. Conversational speech achieves the lowest MCD of any domain but does\nnot achieve the lowest F0 RMSE, indicating that conversational\nUrdu is spectrally well-matched but prosodically more variable\nthan read or narrated speech, plausibly reflecting the more varied\nintonational patterns characteristic of spontaneous dialogue. Literary/Storytelling, despite its narratively complex and stylistically\nmarked content, ranks favorably on MCD and F0 RMSE alike,\nand achieves the highest Resemblyzer similarity of any domain –\nconsistent with sustained, evenly-paced narrative speech providing\nmore stable acoustic context for both synthesis and embedding\nextraction than shorter or more variable utterance types.\nThe Formal domain illustrates a further complication: its two\nsource subsets, FLEURS and UrduSpeech, produce divergent system orderings on both MCD and F0 RMSE, and Edge TTS shows\na 0.2324 similarity gap between its UrduSpeech and FLEURS\nsubset performance. This within-domain heterogeneity indicates\nthat \"Formal\" is not acoustically homogeneous in this corpus,\nand that domain-level aggregates for Formal risk masking subsetspecific effects that are themselves as large as the cross-domain\neffects the study set out to measure. This pattern reinforces the\nbroader point that domain labels in this evaluation are useful\norganizing categories but should not be treated as guarantees of\ninternal acoustic homogeneity.\n\n8.3. Metric Agreement and Disagreement Analysis\nThe most consequential pattern in this study is the dissociation\nbetween what MUSHRA measures and what the objective acoustic\nmetrics measure. Gemini TTS diverges from the reference signal\nmore than any other system on MCD, F0 RMSE, and Resemblyzer\nsimilarity, yet is the most preferred system on MUSHRA. This\ndivergence indicates that reference-matching and perceived quality\nare partially orthogonal constructs for expressive Urdu speech: a\nsystem can sound convincingly natural while diverging substantially, in cents and decibels, from the specific acoustic realization\na human reference speaker happened to produce. The implication\ngeneralizes beyond this dataset – a synthesis system optimized\nto minimize a reference-matching loss may be optimizing for a\ntarget that is only loosely coupled to the naturalness judgments that\nultimately determine perceived quality.\nA complementary disagreement runs in the opposite direction\nfor Indic-Parler-TTS, which is objectively close to the reference,\nboth spectrally and in speaker identity, yet rated poorly by listeners.\nTaken together with the Gemini TTS case, these two inversions\nshow that neither subjective nor objective evaluation alone is\nsufficient: MUSHRA captures naturalness defects that frame-level\nand embedding-level metrics cannot detect, since cepstral distance\nand fixed-dimensional speaker embeddings operate at a granularity\nthat does not register local artifacts or unnatural micro-prosody;\nconversely, the acoustic metrics capture systematic reference deviation that an integrated MUSHRA score cannot localize or explain.\nEach paradigm is sensitive to defects the other is structurally unable\nto detect.\nThe ABX results add a dimension that neither MUSHRA nor\nthe acoustic metrics provide. Even Gemini TTS, the strongest\nsystem on MUSHRA, remains discriminable from the reference\nin 87.5–88.9% of Emotional and Literary/Storytelling trials. This\nconfirms that perceived quality and perceived authenticity are\nseparable: a synthesis can be rated highly on a quality scale while\nremaining reliably identifiable as non-human in a forced-choice\n\nJafar, Sarmad, Yousaf & Bashir: Preprint submitted to Computer Speech & Language\n\nPage 15 of 17\n\n\fDomain-Specific Evaluation of Urdu TTS Systems\ndiscrimination task. A quality-only evaluation would systematically overstate progress toward indistinguishability from human\nspeech, a distinction that matters directly for applications such as\nvoice cloning or dubbing where authenticity, not merely quality, is\nthe deployment criterion.\n\n8.4. Key Findings\nThe key findings of this study are as follows:\n• TTS system performance is inherently multidimensional. No single system achieves consistently\nsuperior performance across perceptual quality, acoustic\nfidelity, and speaker similarity measures, demonstrating\nthat different evaluation criteria capture complementary\naspects of synthesis quality.\n• Subjective naturalness and objective reference similarity are not always aligned. The results show clear cases\nwhere systems preferred by listeners differ substantially\nfrom those achieving the closest acoustic match to reference\nspeech, indicating that perceived quality extends beyond\nsignal-level similarity.\n• Emotional speech remains the most challenging synthesis domain. Across subjective and objective evaluations,\nemotional speech consistently exhibits the lowest perceptual\nquality, highest acoustic distortion, largest pitch variation\nerrors, and weakest speaker similarity, highlighting the difficulty of modeling expressive prosody.\n• Modern TTS systems exhibit distinct optimization tradeoffs. Different systems demonstrate different strengths, with\nsome favoring acoustic and speaker fidelity while others\nachieve higher listener preference through more natural and\nfluent speech generation.\n• Speech quality and speech authenticity represent different evaluation objectives. ABX discrimination results\nshow that highly rated synthetic speech can still be reliably\ndistinguished from natural speech, suggesting that current\nsystems have improved naturalness without fully achieving\nhuman-level authenticity.\n• Comprehensive TTS evaluation requires multiple complementary metrics. Combining perceptual, discriminative, acoustic, and speaker-similarity analyses provides a\nmore complete understanding of synthesis performance than\nany individual evaluation measure.\n\n9. Conclusion\nThis study addressed the need for systematic and comprehensive evaluation of modern text-to-speech systems across diverse\ncommunicative domains. To this end, it introduced a domainstratified evaluation framework covering Formal, Conversational,\nLiterary/Storytelling, and Emotional speech, and evaluated four\nstate-of-the-art TTS systems using complementary subjective and\nobjective measures, including MUSHRA-style listening tests, ABX\ndiscrimination, speaker similarity analysis with Resemblyzer, and\nacoustic evaluations based on MCD and F0 RMSE. The framework\nprovides a structured approach for analyzing how synthesis quality\nvaries across different speech styles and communicative settings.\nThe results demonstrate that no single system consistently\nachieves the best performance across all evaluation dimensions and\nspeech domains. While Gemini TTS obtains the highest overall\n\nMUSHRA-style score (3.040), indicating stronger perceived naturalness and overall quality, Edge TTS achieves the strongest objective acoustic performance, with the lowest mean MCD (7.91 dB)\nand lowest mean F0 RMSE (565.00 cents). Across all evaluated systems, Emotional speech emerges as the most challenging synthesis condition, exhibiting the lowest perceptual scores,\nhighest acoustic distortion (MCD of 12.03 dB and F0 RMSE of\n888.54 cents), and lowest speaker similarity (0.5437). The ABX\nresults further indicate that current TTS systems remain distinguishable from natural speech, with listeners correctly identifying\nthe reference-matching condition in 90.7% of trials, highlighting\nremaining challenges in achieving human-level expressive synthesis.\nThe findings reveal several broader insights into the evaluation\nof modern TTS systems. First, synthesis quality is inherently multidimensional, and system performance depends on the evaluation\ncriterion considered. Second, subjective naturalness and objective\nreference similarity are not always aligned, demonstrating that perceptual quality cannot be fully characterized by signal-level fidelity\nalone. Third, current TTS systems exhibit distinct optimization\ntrade-offs, with different models prioritizing naturalness, acoustic\nsimilarity, or speaker identity. Finally, the results show that perceived quality and authenticity are separate evaluation objectives,\nas highly rated synthetic speech can still be distinguishable from\nnatural speech.\nOverall, this study contributes: (i) a domain-stratified benchmark for evaluating TTS systems across diverse speech conditions;\n(ii) a multi-metric evaluation framework that integrates perceptual,\nacoustic, prosodic, and speaker-similarity analyses; and (iii) a publicly released evaluation pipeline, result tables, and reproducible\nresources that can support future research on speech synthesis\nevaluation. By demonstrating the importance of domain-aware and\nmulti-dimensional assessment, this work provides a foundation for\nmore comprehensive and context-sensitive evaluation of TTS systems, particularly in low-resource and underrepresented language\nsettings.\n\n10. Future Work\nFuture work can extend this benchmark in several directions.\nFirst, larger and more diverse listener studies using standardized\nonline MUSHRA platforms would further improve the reliability\nand comparability of subjective evaluations. Second, the framework can be expanded to include additional speech scenarios\nand emerging TTS systems to investigate how synthesis quality\nvaries across different communicative contexts and technological\napproaches. Finally, applying the proposed evaluation framework\nto other low-resource and underrepresented languages would help\nestablish more inclusive and standardized practices for speech\nsynthesis evaluation beyond high-resource settings.\n\nData Set\nThe annotated evaluation data, including subjective listening\ntest responses and benchmark results, is publicly available at:\nhttps://github.com/Shifa402/Urdu-Synthetic-Speech-Evaluation\n\nThe annotated dataset used in this study can be accessed with\nprior permission via this link: https://drive.google.com/file/d/\n1MfeXJ-LWh1SXptU5gUbXckh27YLMURNW/view?usp=drive_link\n\nJafar, Sarmad, Yousaf & Bashir: Preprint submitted to Computer Speech & Language\n\nPage 16 of 17\n\n\fDomain-Specific Evaluation of Urdu TTS Systems\n\nDeclaration of generative AI technologies in\nthe writing process\nDuring the preparation of this work the authors used Claude in\norder to improve the readability and language of the manuscript.\nAfter using this tool/service, the authors reviewed and edited the\ncontent as needed and take full responsibility for the content of the\npublished article.\n\nCRediT authorship contribution statement\nAli Jafar: Conceptualization, Methodology, Software, Formal\nAnalysis, Writing – Sections 1, 2, 6 (Introduction, Related Work,\nExperimental Setup). Amal Sarmad: Subjective Evaluation Setup,\nWriting – Sections 5, 7, 8 (Evaluation Methodology, Results,\nDiscussion). Shifa Yousaf: Literature Review, Dataset Curation,\nDataset Documentation, Writing – Sections 3–4, 9–10 (Methodology for TTS System Selection, Domain-Wise Dataset Setup,\nConclusion, Future Work). Maryam Bashir: Conceptualization,\nMethodology, Supervision, Review & Editing.\n\nReferences\nAI4Bharat, 2024. Indic parler-TTS: A multilingual text-to-speech model for\nindian languages. https://huggingface.co/ai4bharat/indic-parler-tts.\nModel card and checkpoint, accessed December 2024.\nAnonymous, 2025. PashtoTTS-bench: Automated screening for lowresource non-Latin-script text-to-speech. arXiv:2605.26978.\nArshad, H., et al., 2022. Exploring transfer learning for Urdu speech\nsynthesis, in: Proc. EurALI.\nBailly, G., et al., 2025. Hot topics in speech synthesis evaluation. arXiv\npreprint arXiv:2504.02738 Outcome of Dagstuhl Seminar 25032.\nChiang, C., et al., 2024. Rethinking MUSHRA: Addressing modern\nchallenges in text-to-speech evaluation.\nChiang, C.Y., Lee, H.y., 2024. A study on the applicability of mushra to\nevaluate neural text-to-speech systems, in: Proceedings of Interspeech,\npp. 2195–2199. doi:10.21437/Interspeech.2024-1354.\nChung, H.W., et al., 2022. Scaling instruction-finetuned language models.\narXiv preprint arXiv:2210.11416 .\nConneau, A., Ma, M., Khanuja, S., Zhang, Y., Axelrod, V., Dalmia, S.,\nRiesa, J., Rivera, C., Bapna, A., 2022. FLEURS: Few-shot learning\nevaluation of universal representations of speech, in: 2022 IEEE Spoken\nLanguage Technology Workshop (SLT), pp. 798–805.\nGoogle DeepMind, 2025. Gemini 2.5: Our Most Intelligent AI Model.\nTechnical Report. Google DeepMind. Accessed April 2025.\nInternational Telecommunication Union, 2015. Method for the subjective\nassessment of intermediate quality level of audio systems. Recommendation BS.1534-3. ITU-R.\nJain, P., et al., 2022. Towards building text-to-speech systems for the next\nbillion users. arXiv preprint arXiv:2211.09536 .\nKim, J., Kong, J., Son, J., 2021. Conditional variational autoencoder with\nadversarial learning for end-to-end text-to-speech, in: Proceedings of\nInternational Conference on Machine Learning (ICML), pp. 5530–5540.\nKirkland, A., Mehta, S., Lameris, H., Henter, G.E., Szekely, E., 2023. Stuck\nin the MOS pit: A critical analysis of MOS test methodology in TTS\nevaluation, in: Proc. SSW.\nKominek, J., Black, A.W., 2008. Synthesizer voice quality of new languages\ncalibrated with mean MCD, in: Proc. SLTU.\nKong, J., Kim, J., Bae, J., 2020. Hifi-gan: Generative adversarial networks\nfor efficient and high fidelity speech synthesis, in: Advances in Neural\nInformation Processing Systems (NeurIPS).\nKubichek, R., 1993. Mel-cepstral distance measure for objective speech\nquality assessment , 125–128.\nLacombe, Y., Srivastav, V., Gandhi, S., 2024. Parler-TTS. https://github.\ncom/huggingface/parler-tts.\nLe Maguer, S., et al., 2024. The limits of the Mean Opinion Score for speech\nsynthesis evaluation. Computer Speech & Language 84.\n\nLyth, D., King, S., 2024. Natural language guidance of high-fidelity textto-speech with synthetic annotations. arXiv:2402.01912.\nMicrosoft Corporation, 2023. Azure Cognitive Services: Neural Text-toSpeech Overview. Technical Report. Microsoft Corporation. Accessed\nDecember 2024.\nNaseem, S., et al., 2025. Evaluating and developing TTS models for Urdu\nand Punjabi, in: Proc. Interspeech.\nPratap, V., Tjandra, A., Shi, B., Tomasello, P., Babu, A., Kundu, S.,\nElkahky, A., Ni, Z., Vyas, A., Fazel-Zarandi, M., Baevski, A., Adi, Y.,\nZhang, X., Hsu, W.N., Conneau, A., Auli, M., 2023. Scaling speech\ntechnology to 1,000+ languages.\nSchatz, T., Peddinti, V., Bach, F., Jansen, A., Hermansky, H., Dupoux,\nE., 2013. Evaluating speech features with the ABX discriminability\nmeasure: Analysis of the classical mfc/plp pipeline, in: Proceedings of\nInterspeech, pp. 1781–1785. doi:10.21437/Interspeech.2013-564.\nTan, X., Qin, T., Soong, F., Liu, T.Y., 2021. A survey on neural speech\nsynthesis. arXiv preprint arXiv:2106.15561 .\nWan, L., Wang, Q., Papir, A., Moreno, I.L., 2018. Generalized end-to-end\nloss for speaker verification, in: Proc. ICASSP, pp. 4879–4883.\nWang, C., Chen, S., Wu, Y., Zhang, Z., Zhou, L., Liu, S., Chen, Z., Liu,\nY., Wang, H., Li, J., et al., 2023a. Neural codec language models are\nzero-shot text to speech synthesizers, in: Proceedings of the International\nConference on Machine Learning (ICML), pp. 36062–36075.\nWang, H., et al., 2023b. Generalised zero-shot text-to-speech via voice\nembedding, in: Proc. Interspeech.\nWang, Y., et al., 2017. Tacotron: Towards end-to-end speech synthesis, in:\nProceedings of Interspeech, pp. 4006–4010. doi:10.21437/Interspeech.\n2017-1452.\n\nJafar, Sarmad, Yousaf & Bashir: Preprint submitted to Computer Speech & Language\n\nPage 17 of 17\n\n\f", "numLines": 448, "startLine": 1600, "totalLines": 2047}}}
